Baarkam Obama is the fourth U.S. president to receive the Nobel Peace Prize
U.S. President Barack Obama comes to Oslo, Norway for 26 hours to receive the Nobel Peace Prize here as the fourth U.S. president in history.
In addition, he receives the diploma as well as the medal and a cheque for 1.4 million dollars for his extraordinary aspirations for the intensification of world diplomacy and cooperation among peoples.
The head of the White House arrives tomorrow with his wife Michelle in the Nordwegian metropolis and will be busy all the time.
First, he pays a visit to the Nobel Institute, where he meets for the first time with the five committee members who elected him in October out of 172 people and 33 organizations.
The presidential couple then scheduled a meeting with Norwegian King Harald V and Queen Sonja.
In the afternoon, the visit reaches its culmination with the ceremony, at which Obama assumes the prestige prize.
He receives it as the fourth U.S. president, but only as the third who accepts the award directly in office.
The White House has already indicated that Obama will talk about the Afghanistan war in taking over the prize.
The president doesn't want to evade this issue because he knows he's taking over the award as a president who is currently waging war in two countries.
A few days ago he intensified the war effort by sending more troops to Afghanistan, which none of his critics forget to highlight.
At the ceremony, Obama also received the Gol Medal, diploma and cheque over ten million Swedish crowns (about 24 million Czech crowns).
He intends to donate the money to charity, but he has not yet decided on a concrete charitable work.
The programme of events then envisages a banquet with the president and his wife, in addition to Norway's king and queen, the prime minister and another 250 invited guests will also be present.
Obama has been very reluctant from the outset compared to price.
He cited, for example, that he felt he didn’t quite deserve the prize.
Several times he also reiterated that it is the price not only for him, but for all those who strive for the same values as him.
Immediately after the announcement, he stated that this is "call for action" for him.
Conservationist is accused of blackmail
Leitmeritz-Polize accused the chairman of the citizens' association "Naturschutzgemeinschaft Leitmeritz" of extortion.
In several cases, he appealed against some construction proceedings last year, for the withdrawal of his appeals he demanded money from the investors in his favor, said Leitmeritz police spokeswoman, Alena Romova.
The chairman of Leitmeritz nature conservation communist Lubomir Studnicka.
Now he's imprisoned and it threatens him a prison sentence of up to three years.
The sorting waste containers in Brno don't smell good
In Prague, people can sort the beverage packaging, in the South Moravian villages you will find the sorting garbage containers every step of the way.
And in Brno?
There you would have to reserve a few minutes to find the appropriate garbage cans.
I don't want to say that waste separation in Brno wouldn't work at all.
But it seems to me whether the councillors are insufficiently caring about this problem, even if we are the second largest city in the republic.
Brno is located in waste separation in the Urwaldliga, not only because, as a citizen, we are only allowed to dispose of paper, glass and plastics, but above all by the number of garbage cans for such waste.
Did they try to dispose of the PET bottles in a collection basket, for example?
This is just art, above all it takes time and strong nerves.
The onling position I found by chance so far is Moravian Square opposite the Goose.
Mr. Onderka should not annoy his colleagues, but that seems far too little to me.
Apart from the fact that waste separation is not honey licking in other parts.
That's whay entire rows of my friends don't sort the waste at all.
Of laziness.
It's too far from the garbage cans for them.
I'm not surprised.
Even after the celebration, I offered to dispose of a few glass and plastic bottles.
But on Konicova Street I didn't see any paint bins in sight.
Luckily I came to the right place when I was riding the tram.
But the rubbish almost overflowed.
This is still the center of Brno, which represents the whole city, isn’t it?!
However, ecology and some kind of aesthetic probably leaves the Brno socialists cold.
You'd rather buy the city hockey league that'll shame us all over the republic, rather than buying a few more garbage cans and offering the sorting of more raw materials for a cleaner Brno.
I am glad that I was able to confirm my assumption even with official statistics, having searched on the Internet for a while.
According to EKO-KOM's data, Brno is really the worst in the entire district of South Moravia.
The "Paroubek" budget has deprived the money for pensions and sick pay
After the Left Party, despite the view of the government, gave more money to farmers and civil servants, there is no more money available in the budget for pensions, sick pay and construction savings business.
But also not for the national debt interest or for the international court disputes.
That's causing even more problems for those who will be at the government in the second half of next year, says the finance minister, Eduard Janota.
When the Social Democrats and communists enforced the salary increase for some groups, they reckoned that the government would additionally borrow the money for the sensitive expenses, old-age pensions or for sick pay. "That's just a postponement of the problems into the future, mine laying," says Janota.
From the loans, especially higher interest rates are paid, and you have to refund the money at some point.
Too much to choose from, the state treasury is empty.
If the social benefits or costs for the state enterprise are not reduced, the tax must be increased.
For all those to whom the Left Party now gave more money: the fire brigade, the teachers, the farmers.
And later also for their children.
Around the pensions or the sick pay, from whose account 1.8 billions have disappeared, people don't have to be afraid, they get that money.
But at the price that the state owes itself even more - next year, not only 163 billion will be missing from the state treasury, but far more.
Despite the efforts of Minister Janota, the debt will rise faster and faster, and together with this also the costs and interest rates.
The state will need the money for compensation, then it will no longer be sufficient to only slightly increase VAT or to cut the maternity allowance, as the government did in its counter-deficit package.
Due to the transfer of money in favour of some electoral groups, five billions will also be missing for the expansion of highways and railways next year.
These are mainly the sections won in the competition, which can no longer be redesignated and possibly cheaper.
At most received, which is also not free.
This means delay or cessation of thirty large buildings, European money creation is also endangered, warns the spokesperson of the Ministry of Transport, Karel Hanzelka.
The CSSD has suggested that it loosen the outflow of moneyald - it attacks the CEZ stock dividends, where the state is the owner of the majority, and from these are then distributed the money into the traffic fund.
The problem is that the amount of the dividend is never secure and it is by no means an infinite source.
Last year 18 billions flowed from CEZ into the budget.
Every year from the dividends, the environmental damage liquidations are paid, the pension account is replenished and this money also serves as a reserve for the planned pension reform.
With this trick, according to the rules of the EU, the actual budget deficit rises to the nevel of 5.7 percent GDP.
But this does not end the carnival of upheavals yet.
Nearly three billion fell the budget for various compensations for victims of communism and offenses, or for expenses for the judicial disputes, even for building savings.
Whether the state can cope without this money is not certain.
That's pure gambling.
If we lose an international court dispute, then we'll have to pay for that anyway, Janota clarifies.
The minister then has to save the money internally or, in the worst-case, attack the government budget reserve.
One hundred million is also missing for the two-billion-dollar project "Staatskasse", which is supposed to make it possible to monitor on-line, for which the individual authorities spend the money.
The first stage will start in January.
If the finance minister does not find the money elsewhere, the project would have to be halted and, in this case, sanctions will come, Janota warned.
Your next smartphone will master two operating systems
For the future, Americans expect a mobile phone on which the user can switch between different operating systems by pressing a single button.
The plans presented look promising.
It's enough to press just one button, and Windows Mobile turns into Android within a few seconds.
It is precisely this possibility that the American company VMware wants to present, which focuses primarily on the development of the virtual software for the computers.
We enable you to run two user profiles simultaneously on your mobile phone.
You can switch between them, they can separate them into two home and work profiles.
Both will work in parallel, Srinivas Krishnamurti from the company VMware hinted at the interview for Computerworld magazine.
It was presented in November last year and presented for the first time a few days ago.
It's going to come to the market in 2012.
The virtualization of smart phones is not fiction.
VMware has already successfully demonstrated the smartphone test pattern with two operating systems to the journalists.
One was a modified Nokia N800 with a RAM memory of 128 MB, on which at the same time the aforementioned Windows Mobile and Android systems ran.
The development of the new technology for mobile phones is in full swing.
Now VMware is working together with the European and American operators on smartphone virtualization and the devices are to come to the customer within 2012.
Lack of snow on the mountains torments the hotel owners
Snowless slopes worry not only the Giant Mountains residents.
The lack of snow also discourages people from ordering ski stays in hotels and guesthouses.
That is where you can still get free rooms for all winter appointments including Christmas and New Year's Eve.
Our pages are frequently visited.
People are going through the offers, setting the prices, but meanwhile they are afraid to order bindingly.
And if they call us, they immediately ask if, in our opinion, this year's Christmas will be on snow or "on mud," the operator of the information portal Spindl.Info, Martin Jandura, cited.
If you want the New Year's Eve stay in the spindle mill exactly according to your ideas, you should no longer hesitate with the order.
If you prefer to save and where the quality of the stay comes second, you can try to wait a few more days.
In the meantime, the Spindelmühle hotel owners mainly only offer weekly stays for New Year's Eve.
I think they're going to make an effort to sell them for a while, but then they, too, will have to give in, and respond to shorter times.
And then the vacant rooms will quickly dwindle, Jandura estimated.
Free beds on all winter dates are always offered by the travel agency Ingtours in Vrchlabi.
Now we are most interested in the Christmas week, which is not yet sold out.
In our offer, about half of the seats are still available.
That’s why we’ve been preparing some lower-priced Christmas stays for the last moment, the director of the travel agency Ingtours, Petr Schiefert, cited.
The Vrchlabi travel agency can also offer New Year’s Eve day in Giant Mountains, but there are less vacancies here.
Good sales will also find the first fourteen days in February, the remaining winter dates are sold on average.
This year’s interest for winter stays is also negatively influenced by the fact that there is always no snow in Giant Mountains.
People are waiting to see if snow falls.
No one wants to spend the end of the year in the mountains
If the slopes are snowy within a week, Christmas will also be sold out, says Schiefert.
Conversely and slightly better than last year it manages to occupy the rooms at Hotel Horizont in Pecpod Snezkou.
We're about five percent better than last year.
On New Year's Eve and for Christmas we only have a few free rooms left.
There is a lot of interest during the Polish holidays in Janua, and in February the business will also run well, informed the director of the best Pec hotel, Karel Rada.
80 percent for New Year's Eve stays, the Hotel Omnia is also staffed at the central car park in Janske Lazne.
On the other hand, half of the beds remain free for Christmas at the latest hotel in Janske Lazne.
Pleasant surprised us this year January, for which we are currently already about 60 percent occupied.
February is now a bit weaker, but at the moment when the snow arrives, the interest in stays in the Giant Mountains certainly also increases, the director of the Hotel Omnia, Erik Sporysch.
In the meantime, the Giant Mountains hotel owners are waiting for a larger snout.
On Thursday it should cool down and if there is rainfall, this should be snow on the mountains.
But after that there will be a warming, informed Jiri Jakubsky from the location of the Czech weather office in Hradec Kralove.
USA: Repetition is mother of prudence
It's almost funny with what student acrimony Barack Obama, the president with his call of the smartest, repeats his predecessor's strategy on the Afghanistan issue, which he himself thought was the dumbest.
When he finally sweated out his long-awaited Afghanistan doctrine, it turned out that he wrote them off three years ago via the copy paper from the Busch-Iraq scenario.
Not only does he use the phrase "as in Iraq" several times in the text itself, he even used the name of the Bush Declaration of January 2007 as the name of his own declaration: "New Forward" without hesitation.
Three years ago, he himself criticized the Bush idea - sending out fresh troop contingents in Iraq plagued by the civil war - as an "irresponsible and karastrophic decision in its conclusions."
Subsequent months showed, however, that the Bush strategy based on recommendations of the military was the only possible and very successful in so far as it no longer speaks at all today in the news about Iraq.
Bush's reputation hasn't been improved by this, because objectively he has left his successor with a much more grateful initial situation than was to be expected.
In Afghanistan, Barack Obama hopes the miracle repeats itself.
In order to increase the troop contingent, he was once again asked by the military, especially the high commander of the Afghanistan operation General Stanley McCristal, who, even despite the subordination habits, harshly criticized the hesitant shooters in the White House.
He demanded 40,000 troops in order to be able to reverse the situation for the better.
The president hesitated for three months, but didn't come up with a better idea and so he betrayed the general by 10,000 troops.
He has squeezed the commitment for another 5,000 from Europe, even if he himself originally expected at least 10,000.
Highly problematic about the whole Obama strategy is his intention to withdraw the troops from Afghanistan in 18 months and complete this process within three years. The whole Obamas Doctrine leaves more questions than answers.
Some doubt that the halved increase will have the same effect as the measure in Iraq.
Even in spite of the fraternal war of recent years, the Iraq society is relatively well-structured, its leaders honor the values.
The Afghan situation is characterized by the contraindication - you cannot rely on anyone and no agreements are held.
In these conditions, it's hard to prepare the Afghan security forces who are accustomed to changing their mind every day.
The political institutions, even if they were formally founded correctly, remain only sham buildings behind which patriarchal tribal relations will continue to proliferate.
The balancing of interests between the different ethnic groups is unusually complicated, especially because of the incomprehensibility of the conditions that further complicate Pakistan and Iran influences.
The construction of a "capable state", which is the minimalist goal of the entire operation, is also very unrealistic.
Time horizon of Obama strategy alienated by its voluntarism
Over the past eight years, the situation in Afghanistan has deteriorated only further and now it is on the verge of the explosion.
Believe in the fact that the little soldiers, who have broken out into the cry of war over the "early return", create a miracule here, can only a truly romantic soul, which is not impressed by facts.
From a political point of view, the date setting is pure irresponsibility for the withdrawal of troops, because the Taliban will accept this as the indirect confession of American defeat.
The last NATO summit showed that the willingness of Europeans to participate in the battles in Afghanistan is running out.
Europe's politicians cannot and do not want to explain to their constituents how the security of Germany or Italy is related to the war in the Hindu Kush foreland.
Another possible factor that can radically influence the situation is the development of events in Iran.
If the situation continues to deteriorate and the increased sanctions do not affect the Iranians, there is a very serious question for the United States whether the problem cannot be solved with troops.
This question must be answered in the period in which America proceeds according to Obamia for the victorious withdrawal from Afghanistan.
However, each solution will also mean a dramatic turn of the Afghan situation.
The only consolantion for Barack Obama is the fact that there are still three years left until the presidential election, and thus there is still enough time for him to resort to a different strategy if it turned out to be a total failure.
The managers of Bank Goldman Sachs will no longer receive premium bonuses in cash
The senior executives of American bank Goldman Sachs will no longer receive premium bonuses in cash this year.
This was announced by the company, which is responding to the sharp criticism of its wage practices.
A group of 30 top executives receives shares instead of money, but you can’t sell it until five years from now.
In addition, the shares can be deprived of the managers if they act excessively riskily.
Goldman Sachs puts himself at the forefront's effort, according to the Reuters agency, to link Wall Street's premium bonuses with long-term performance.
I believe that Wall Street is aware of the far-reaching direction in which it has to go, the former banker of JPMorgan, Douglas Elliott.
The problem lies in the details, he added.
Goldman Sachs made itself the target of criticism after setting aside nearly 17 billion dollars for premium bonuses for the first three quarters of this year.
The total amount of premium bonuses over the course of this year is likely to exceed 20 billion dollars, according to the Reuters agency, despite the measure indicated today.
The high premium bonuses in the banking sector have become the hottest political issue in a number of countries at the beginning of the financial crisis.
Britain reported on Wednesday that it intends to charge the bonuses of bankers exceeding 25,000 pounds once with a 50 per cent tax.
France is also preparing similar steps.
This year, women have won the Nobel Prizes in all disciplines, except in physics.
Five record-breaking women took over this year’s Nobel Prizes in this year’s specialist categories and for literature in Stockholm from the hands of the King of Sweden Carl XVI Gustaf.
In addition to the four female scientists, among them was the German writer of Romanian destitution, Herta Müller, among them.
Two American biologists, Elizabeth Blackburn and Carol Greider, together with their compatriot Jack Szostak, received the Nobel Prize in medicine for research in the chromosone sector.
The prize for chemistry has been awarded by Israeli Ada Jonath, together with the Americans, Venkatraman Ramakrishnan and Thomas Steitz, for enlightening the ribosome structure and function.
The last laureatin awarded the Nobel Prize for economics, which the American Elinor Ostrom and her compatriot Oliver Williamson received for analyses of the economic report.
The onling category in which no woman received a Nobel Prize this year was physics.
Today, the scientists Charles Kao for research in the fiber glass sector and George Smith together with Willard Boyl for the CCD chip invention, which is the basis of all digital chamberas, fax machines or astronomical telescopes.
Each of these winners has received the diploma, Nobel Medail and certificate of acquisition of the cash prize.
This amounts to ten million Swedish crowns in each category.
If there are several prizewinners in each category, the cash prize is divided among them.
According to tradition, the American president, Barak Obama, has already received the most anticipated Nobel Peace Prize this afternoon in Oslo.
He has acknowledged the controversy of his appointment in his speech because he is only at the beginning of his path, and, moreover, is at the head of the country that wages two wars - in Iraq and Afghanistan.
In observing his policies, however, he added that wars in certain situations are necessary for fighting world peace, even if the price is high.
Minister Janota is considering resigning. Klaus has invited him to the castle
President Vaclav Klaus receives the finance minister, Eduard Janota, on Friday morning, who thinks about remaining in the government because he does not agree with the implementation of the state budget for the next year in the form, as it was released by the assembly of deputies on Wednesday.
It is believed that Janota's budget, and possibly the whereabouts in the cabinet, will become the topics of Friday's meeting.
The budget is also criticised by Klaus.
According to him, the budget deficit is too high, which also deepens the crisis of public finances.
About the meeting, the presidential spokesperson, Radim Ochvat, informed.
Janota plans to negotiate with the prime minister, Jan Fischer, about his further remaining in his capacity on Monday.
According to the budget law, which Klaus now receives for signature, the Czech Republic with a deficit of 163 billion crown should operate.
In parliament, however, the Left Party proposals on money circulation were voted on on Wednesday and the money for the state civil servant salaries, for the maintenance of social benefits and direct wages of the farmers was increased.
The government has resisted Social Democracy's proposals that they covertly increase the budget deficit.
Between the Left Party on the one hand and the government, Klaus and Rechtspartei, on the other hand, a sharp domestic political dispute has therefore arisen, because of which the question mark about the further work of the Fischer officials' cabinet arises, on whose design the ODS, CSSD and Green Party have agreed in the spring.
Critics claim that the budget, as adopted by parliament, practically liquidates the package of austerity measures enforced by the fishing government in the fall, and which, according to their understanding, is supposed to end public debt.
Klaus, who can sign the law on the state budget but doesn't have to, declared today at the discussion breakfast with the entrepreneurs that politicians have neglected the "serious problem" "the unsustainable deficit" of public finances for a long time.
In his opinion, there is no longer a good solution today.
An improvement could bring only a strong government, which could still build on a broader consensus of the political parties.
The ODS chairman, Mirek Topolanek, stated that the budget is horrific, and the government should weigh up its continued existence.
The ODS clearator, Petr Necas, told the CTK that the concept of the civil servicing cabinet obviously no longer works with the support of the CSSD, ODS and Green Party.
In the editorial board, Premier Fischer described similar words as "strong statements."
CSSD leader Jiri Paroubek reacted to Topolanek's words that the budget deficit was atrocious, that the former Topolanek government had prepared for this year, exceeding 200 billion crowns.
As early as Wednesday, the CSSD judged the approval of the state budget for next year to be a success.
The People's Party was also pleased.
Mandela has been played in Clint Eastwood's new film by Morgan Freeman
The South Africans claim that the world's new Hollywood film "Invictus: The Unbeaten" shows much of the country's struggle and victories despite some criticisms that the leading role has been cast by an American actor.
The story tells of sports, racial relations and about Nelson Mandela.
The tireless fighter against the racist apartheid government system in the South African Republic, as well as its first black president, Nelson Mandela, portrays in his life's story the American Oscar actor, Morgan Freeman.
Freeman said he was asked by ninety-on-one-year-old Mandela if he could portray him in the Eastwood film.
Matt Damon takes on another meaningful role.
I told him, "If I'm going to play you, I'll have to meet you.
The seventy-two-year-old actor, who had received the award for his role in the Eastwood drama from the boxing section "Million Dollar Baby", already represented the leader of the movement to abolish slavery, from which has finally become a slave owner, a fictional U.S. president, and also god in "Evan All-Mighty," but rarely someone who still lives and is as important to many people as Mandela.
The former South African statesman was in prison for 27 years for his actively working in ending apartheid in the South African Republic.
He was released in 1990 and became president of the country for four years.
In 1993 he was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize.
"Invictus" is the Latin word for "undefeated," and at the same time that's the name of a poem by English author William Ernest Henley, edited in 1875.
The requirement was how to speak
The film depicts a true incident in which Nelson Mandela joined forces with the captain of the South African ruby team, Francois Pienaar, in order to unite the country. The new president Mandela knows that even after apartheid, his people still remain racially as well as economically divided.
He thinks he can agree on his people through the sport, that's whhere he decides the rubby players, who are among the outsiders worldwide.
After all, the South African team rises until the World Cup in 1995.
Freeman worked for several years to bring the Mandela story to the cinema screen.
He had no other goal than to play this role in such a way in approaching reality as closely as possible, Freeman said.
Of course, the biggest challenge was to speak like he did.
The actor said that in case he and the world politician would be in the same country, he would try to meet with Mandela, have dinner with him and be behind the scenes in front of his speech.
The most important thing for him was to shake Mandela's hand.
I've found that if I shake your hand, I'll take care of your energy, it's transmitted, and I felt like I know what you're feeling, he said.
It's important for me to have become a different person.
Matt Damon represents François Pienaar, captain of the national rubgy team in which the white players outweighed.
The actor said that he had six months to prepare for the role in the tough rubby world.
It was a big surprise for him when he first met Pienaar at his home.
I remember when I rang the doorbell that he opened and the first thing I said to François Pienaar in my life was, 'In the film I look much bigger.'
Despite the obvious differences in the stature of the actors in comparison with their actual role models, he has received positive reviews under the direction of director Eastwood "Invictus: Unbeaten" and one also speaks of prospects for an Oscar.
The film critic of the Daily Variety, Todd McCarthy, summarized his evaluation in the following words: "This was very good history, very well executed film."
In the film reviews on the web pages rottentomatoes.com, Invictus has received 76 percent positive evaluations.
Light secrecy dissolved over Norway: The Russians have tested a missile
About Norway, a special lighting effect of unknown origin could be observed.
The Russian Ministry of Defense admitted yesterday that an intercontinental ballistic missile has been tested not far front of the Norwegian border.
However, the launch was again unsuccessful and the "Bulava" missile, which was originally supposed to be the pride of the Russian army, will slowly become the nightmare of the Russian generality as well as the prime minister Vladimir Putin.
His repeated on-the-scene attendance at the rocket tests also had no positive effect on the success of the trial flights.
Bulava has mostly not started at all or been damaged in the air.
In the Russian newspapers you already write about "Bulava" as about a "rocket that doesn't fly."
A rocket that doesn't fly but glows
This 13th attempt went normally at the beginning.
Only at the end of the flight, technical damage to the rocket occurred again.
Presumably, the engine exploded in the third rocket stage.
This time, "Bulava" departed from Dmitry Donsky nuclear submarine, which sailed under the water of the White Sea.
This type of missile allows you to launch from the submarine.
That's why it's probably that the mysterious light caused by an indefinite flying object over north trails was actually the damaged "Bulava."
In addition, the Norwegians have not doubted since the first moment that it is a Russian missile.
This time, the generality has defended itself against it and claims that one cannot clearly speak of a failure.
The first two stages of the rocket did not fail and only the third stage was affected by the accident.
In the previous cases, the engines in the first stage were immediately damaged.
Even if there was no test launch without errors, the management of the Ministry of Defense considers only six out of thirteen unsuccessful.
The almost fifty percent success therefore fulfills the generality with optimism and so it is still claimed that the "Bulava" will fly once without any problems and will even carry up to ten hypersonic nuclear warheads with a total weight of maximum 1.15 tons.
The legendary Fetisov has signed an annual contract with CSKA Moscow at the age of 51
The legendary defender Vjaceslav Fetisov introduces himself again in a professional hockey game at the age of 51.
Former world champion, Olympic champion and Stanley Cup owner is helping his team CSKA Moscow out in need, and is likely taking KHL against Petersburg as early as Friday's duel.
Fetisov, who graduated his career at the age of forty, in 1998, is currently president of the CSKA.
After Denis Kuljas injured, we urgently need another defensive player.
Fetisov has regularly trained and agreed that he will support the team.
We only need to clarify some remaining formalities, the agency AP cited the coach of Sergei Nemcinov's Moscow club.
Meanwhile, it's not clear whether the famous hockey player's comeback will affect just one game.
I mean Fetisov is one of those who can raise the morale of other teammates, pitched team-mate Nemcinov.
If Fetisov comes back to the ice surface, according to AP, he becomes the oldest Russian professional professional hockey game.
In the NHL, legendary striker Gordie Howe has played an entire season for Hartford at fifty-on-one and was able to take advantage of 15 hits and 26 assistants.
The oldest of the famous five, Fetisov, Kasatonov - Makarov, Larionov, Krutov, is one of the best and most successful hockey players in history.
In the dress of the Soviet Union, he has won two Olympic gold medals, seven world titles and he has also triumphed in Canada Cup as well as in the Junior World Cup.
In the second part of his career, he also accumulated successes in the NHL, where he twice overheld the Stanley Cup in the Detroit-Dress.
He has been a member of the hockey star room since 2001.
Hyundai gave up on the unions. All overtime will be lifted
The management of Nosovice automotive factory Hyundai agreed with the union on the lifting of all overtime hours.
Due to frequently ordered overtime, the union announced the willingness to strike on Monday.
This was confirmed by the spokesperson of the automotive factory Hyundai, Petr Vanek.
However, the management wants to agree with the trade unionists about a Saturday shift this week.
We must not give up our manufacturing plan, which is a principle, that's whhere voluntary overtime was suggested on Saturday, December 19, for the reason that two shifts of work would be worked, Vanek said.
Sedmicka.cz wrote that the company has offered the employees who would work the Saturday shift a bonus of 400 CZK.
Employees will also receive this in retrospect for the Saturday shift of 28 November.
According to Vanek, the company has decided to change the motivation of the employees for the proposed overtime.
Either they have the overtime surcharge paid or they can take replacement holidays on 28 December and thus extend the Christmas holidays, Vanek listed.
In addition, all employees of the Saturday shift will be paid the fare and lunch.
Bearing in mind that the overtime hours were the most urgent and burning point regarding the demands and complaints of the trade unionists, who had stopped production last week, the management decided yesterday afternoon with immediate effect on the lifting of all overtime hours for the month of December, Vaněk pointed out.
Employees at the automobile factory had been forced until last week to still work 2 overtimes virtually every day after the eight-hour shift.
From the signing of the agreement, the willingness to strike will be revoked by the trade unions.
Further negotiations between the management of the automobile factory and the trade unions are to come about on Friday afternoon.
The union leader, Petr Kuchar, said on Wednesday that if they can agree on fulfilling the demands and sign a joint document, they are prepared to lift the willingness to strike.
The situation in the automobile factory worsened last week, on Wednesday, when about 400 employees spontaneously went on strike due to constant overtime.
They also protested against the poor terms of payment and alleged harassment.
On the other hand, the management held that the employees have to do the overtime to meet the automotive delivery requirements.
The trade unionists also demanded in their Monday strike readiness proclamation that the employees, who had interrupted production the previous Wednesday, will not be subject to any sanctions.
In the area of overtime, the unions are calling for overtime to be reduced to a minimum.
In addition, they are demanding the payment of 5000 kroons to be paid this year.
Nosovice Automobilfabrik Hyundai now employs 2000 people.
Serial production was launched at the company in November last year.
By this September, about 80,000 vehicles had been produced here, the current production capacity is 200,000 vehicles per year.
Czech discovery: a substance that also works in ́steed ́ HIV viruses
The team of Czech and German scientists is testing new compounds designed to prevent the spread of HIV viruses in the organism.
The main advantage of the improved substance is that it also works on the viruses that are already resistant to drugs.
This fact predominates against the disadvantage that the compound does not act as strongly on conventional virus variants as one of the existing medicines.
The disease AIDS cannot be cured yet.
However, the composition of medication can prolong the patient’s life and prevent the multiplication of HIV viruses in the body.
However, their intake is accompanied by a number of side effects.
Incomplete suppression of virus propagation has also resulted in the development of sg. resistent viruses, against which drugs are no longer effective.
The work of the experts of three institutes of the Academy of CR, the VSCHT (Chemism-Technological University) Prague and the University in Heidelberg opens the way how to deal with virus resistance.
They have shown that the substances referred to as metalocarboranes act on the protein, which is responsible for propagating the HIV virus.
Metalocarborans are compounds of boron, hydrogen, carbon and cobalt.
These compounds block virus propagation in a different way than any medicines used today, so they can overcome the resistance problem.
In their work, the scientists arrive at new ́improved ́ compounds that have been prepared based on the knowledge of the molecular mechanism of their binding for the virus protein.
The metalocarborans have a unique three-dimensional structure: two multiple cages, which consist of boron, hydrogen and carbon atoms and are coupled by a metal atom, in this case cobalt.
Insidious and persistent virus
HIV protease is protein of the HIV virus, which is essential for the life cycle of the virus.
Withouth the HIV-virus division caused by HIV protease, no mature infection virus particles would arise.
If we turn off the HIV protease, we also eliminate the proliferation of the virus in the patient's body, the scientists explain in the press report, which has been published by the Institute of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry of the Academy of Sciences CR.
In the work, which was published in the Wissenschaftszeitung Journal of Medicinal Chemistry, the scientists describe entire ranks of connections in which two pairs of cages (see table) are coupled by a short organic chain, which is then systematically changed further.
Slightly weaker, but more reliable
The impact of this range of substances against HIV protease has been tested in the terrifying glass, also against its resistant (resistant) variants obtained from HIV-infected patients.
The effect of metalocarborans on the usual enzyme variant is not as strong as with the clinically used medicines, but it does not lose its effect on the resistant variants, against which the drugs used are often ineffective.
The unique mechanism of action as well as its further properties, such as biological and chemical stability, low toxicity and the possibility of other chemical designs, makes the metalocarboranes interesting compounds for further research aimed at the development of effective drugs against HIV, says Pavlina Rezacova, head of the Laboratory of Structural Biology at the Institutes of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry and the Institute of Molecular Genetics of the Academy of Sciences.
Dispute over budget. ODS went after fishermen, he's fighting back
ODS Chairman Mirek Topolanek described next year's approved budget as "horrific" and Jan Fischer's government should, in his opinion, consider its continued existence.
The prime minister described the ODS as "strong statements."
It's supposed to take time for analysis.
ODS deputy chairman Petr Necas said the concept of the civil servant cabinet, with the support of the CSSD, ODS and Green Party, was obviously no longer working.
According to Necas, the Prime Minister must decide whether he intends to rely on the minority in parliament when it comes to the fight against the rising deficit, or whether he is concerned about remaining in office with the help of the parliamentary left.
The approach of the ODS and the Social Democrats to the problem of public deficits is diametrically deviating, Necas pointed out.
On Wednesday, the parliament approved the budget for 2010, but with Left Party amendment proposals for more than 12 billion crowns.
Parliament has increased the money for state civil servant salaries, for the maintenance of social benefits and for direct payments to farmers.
The government has countered the social democracy’s proposals that they covertly increase the budget deficit.
The Czech Republic was supposed to grow with a backlog of 163 billion crowns.
The ODS has left the hall before the vote, so it has complied with the desire of the government, because even the amended budget is only a less bad variant of the budget provision.
Disappointment and meaninglessness
The finance minister, Eduard Janota, has talked about his disappointment and a sense of futility, whereas Prime Minister Jan Fischer has speaked that the government is still judging the current situation.
Necas is convinced that if Janota had seriously threatened his resignation in the event that the amendments were accepted, the situation would look different.
According to Czech television, Janota has registered with the Prime Minister for Monday after government business.
In doing so, he has hinted that he is considering his further remaining in office.
The reason for this is his disappointment at the negotiation of the budget.
According to Necas, the nominants of social democracy (CSSD), who had enforced the changes in the budget, have nothing to do in the government to the extent to which Fischer advocates the further struggle with the budget deficit.
The Premier should also clearly say that he will resign if Parliament continues to adopt deficit-enhancing laws, for example those involving sick pay and pensions, or the service law amendment.
Topolanek: Ministers nominated by ODS were not mistaken
This is a matter for Prime Minister Fischer and the entire government.
Ministers nominated by the ODS have not made any mistakes, Topolanek highlighted in a text message from the U.S.
It mentioned that in some ministries there are deputies who are professing for TOP 09 who voted against the budget.
Necas stressed that if Fischer decides to waive the fight against the deficit level of public finances, "it is perfectly logical and correct that he relies on the Left-wing majority, which is currently in parliament."
Then he allegedly can't reckon with the support and tolerance of the Civic Democrats.
I haven't thought about it, and if that's going to be current, then I'm going to deal with it, Defense Minister Martin Bartak, nominated by the Civic Democrats, told journalists in parliament.
In the afternoon, Prime Minister Fischer flew to Brussels at the European Council meeting.
The ODS resigned at the Prime Minister's request
Necas stressed at the same time that the ODS would probably vote against the amended budget in parliament on Wednesday, should the prime minister explicitly assert members of the civic Democrats to allow them to adopt the budget.
We don't act as orphans, we're not young children, so he then responded to the question of whether the ODS wasn't pushed back at the political negotiation on the budget through the absence of the party chairman in parliament as well as in the Czech Republic.
I didn't realise my voice would be missing.
Even when I renounced the mandate, I knew that in the next few months it would be mainly about Bolshevization of parliament and destruction of everything positive, Topolanek himself reacted.
According to the CD's (Czech Railway) schedule, fewer trains will run for about the same fare.
The Czech Railway terminates or restricts a number of less utilised fast and local trains from 13 December, while other connections will be intensified.
The entire volume of traffic will be reduced by two per cent from the validity date of the new timetable compared to today.
Most tariffs incl. basic and customer fares will not be changed.
For example, the residents of the Ostrava area receive several connection possibilities to the capital, and the railway uses Pendolino trains for this route.
The express train will no longer go to Bratislava (Pressburg).
Fewer express trains will also travel on the Prague - Pisek - Budweis route, in principle the direct connections from Prague to Letohrad will be reduced.
The largest reduction is planned by the railway in Hradec Kralove (Königsgraz), a total of eight percent, but Prague and the surrounding area can look forward to an increase.
Two night trains will also be lifted on the Prague - Tabor - Budweis route and for a few days a week the operation of some express trains on the Prague-Pisek-Budweis route will be reduced.
Among the biggest changes is the introduction of the new direct traffic route from Milovice to Prague, currently the inhabitants of Milovice have to switch to Lysa nad Labem.
It is the fifteenth trafficle of the Esko suburban transport system, the opening of which is possible thanks to railway electrification from Lysa nad Labem to Milovice.
The long-distance routes to the capital will end in the destination station Prague - Hauptbahnhof.
The CD introduces more connecting lines SC Pendolino between Prague and Ostrava and the trains to Sumperk/Jesenik will run at a time interval of two hours.
Passengers can no longer travel to Bratislava since December 13 with Pendolino.
This year's bill for regional railways: increase by 200 million
The express trains are ordered and paid for by the Ministry of Transport, in totally the railway will receive four billion kronors in order to settle the so-called liquidity loss in the next year, just like this year. The local trains supply the districts, in total they will pay eight billion kronor for it next year, with the state shooting three billions.
This year's regional trains are 200 million Czech crowns more expensive.
For the local trains as well as the express trains, the railway has concluded a ten-year contract, until now the contract has only ever been concluded for one year.
The railway praises the system, perhaps that is why it did not resort to an increase in prices for the vast majority of tickets.
Changes occur only with one-day network tickets CD Net and for cheap Internet tickets eLiska.
For passengers without the customer card, the CD Net price will be increased from 450 crowns to 600 crowns.
The price eLiska will then depend on the plug driven, until now it has been uniform for the journey between arbitrary district cities and cost 160 crowns.
Philippine millitants arrest more than 50 people, the children have been released.
Millitante today kidnapped 75 people in the south of the Philippines, including some elementary students and their teachers.
This was cited by AFP, which originally only informed more than 65 abductees.
All 17 detained children were released after eight hours, including a teacher.
Kidnapping occurred in Agusan del Sur province.
Approximately 19 millitants allegedly used the hostages as a live shield while escaping from police.
Local negotiators are now scrambling for the release of the rest of the hostage.
According to the AFP agency, millitants belong to the New People's Army (NPA), which is the gun faction of the Communist Party of the Philippines (CPP).
According to AP, police tracked the kidnappers because of the violence that prompted vengeances between the two local family clans.
In the south of the Philippines, in Maguindanao province, the standing right has been imposed because of the recent massacre in which 57 people were killed.
Among the casualties of the November 23 massacre, which was related to the election to the governor's office, were 30 journalists.
On suspicion of participating in the massacre, the governor of this southern Phillippine province and his father Andal Ampatuan, patriarch of the influential Ampatuan clan, were arrested.
Muslim separatists are also active on Mindanao Island.
Which, however, resumed the peace negotiations with the Phillippine government on Tuesday.
South Africa's history affects everyone, says Morgan Freeman aka Nelson Mandela
In the next few days, Clint Eastwood's new Hollywood movie comes to American cinemas "Invictus: the Unbeaten," which includes part of the life story of the former president of the Republic of South Africa, Nelsona Mandela.
The role of the world-famous fighter against apartheid has been given to Morgan Freeman, who had allegedly been voted out by Mandela himself a few years ago.
The supporting actor role of the legendary South African champion in rugby, Francois Pienaar, has received Matt Damon.
With the theatrical release of the film, reactions from critics as well as the public are also loud
Nelson Mandela
It is named Nelson Rolihlaha Mandela, born on 18 July 19118 in South African Mvez.
In 1988 he received the Sakhar Prize, in 1993 together with de Klerk the Nobel Peace Prize.
He was elected President of the Republic of South Africa in 1994-1999.
Great expectations are placed in the film because it comes to cinemas at a time when preparations for the World Cup are culminating in South Africa on the one hand, but at the same time the media shakes the world with frightening news about rising racial hatred and discrimination against the inhabitants on the part of the black ruling majority.
According to the AP news agency, the reactions to the new film in South Africa are consistently positive for the time being, despite numerous reservations against the occupation of the leading roles with American Hollywood stars.
Morgan Freeman is nevertheless proud of the new film.
I dreamed of this role for many years and prepared well for it.
Several times I also met Nelson Mandela personally, so that I can - as they say - receive his energy, he listed in the interview after the festival premiere of the film in Los Angeles.
I think it's good if with this film we can remember Mandela's personality and his message.
This is a history of South Africa, but it affects everyone, he added.
In rugbye national team's dress
The film is based on British writer John Carlin’s book template, freely translated ‘In the Role of the Enemy ́: Nelson Mandela, and the game united by the people portrays the South African advocate of human rights within his first presidential election period.
Morgan Freeman strives in the course of the film to bring together two racial and, for year and day, mutually delimited population groups, and like Nelson Mandela in 1995, he, too, is stepping to the World Cup field in his role in South Africa's national rubbydress, which South Africa won at the time.
Fourteen years ago, Mandela reaped great ovations for entering the lawn in the dress, which is almost like a holy act in South Africa, predominantly from the white audience and has thus won the recognition of the white population.
The rugby championship, perhaps, manages to unite the people into a single people in turn, the rubgy player Chester Williams, who was the only "blacker" on the national team in 1995.
Whether the tense situation in South Africa improves, that will only become apparent.
The white South Africans are skeptical, however, as, for example, in recent interview for LN the rugby fan Christopher Dawson, who fled from the country to Britain.
The championship begins on June 11, 2010.
In the Louny area, transformers burn for several 10 million kronors
The blaze broke out in the morning at the transformer of Ceps in the switchgear near the municipality of Vyskov in the Louny area.
According to Usti nad Labem's spokesman, Lukas Marvan, six units intervened in the fire.
Originally, the fire brigade wanted to burn out the transformers, but later it changed the view and now the fire is extinguished with a mixture of water foam and special extinguishers.
The cause was a technical defect, a transformer interior short circuit.
No one was injured, the accident did not jeopardize the security operation of the management system, according to the company spokeswoman, Pavla Mandatova.
The damage was estimated at several 10 million crowns, more precisely the damage can only be quantified after the fire.
According to Mandatova, the destroyed transformer must be replaced by a new one.
For over 10 years nothing similar has happened in the company Ceps, mistakes of this species are rare, she added.
According to the spokeswoman of the company CEZ for Northern Bohemia, Sona Holingerova, the power supply was not interrupted, because of the fire of the transformer station, the functionality of households, authorities and companies was not affected.
A.G. Ceps operates in the Czech territory as the sole operator of the line system, the electrical lines of 400 kV and 220 kV.
It maintains, refurbishes and develops 39 switchgears with 67 transformers, with which the electrical energy is transferred to the distribution system and line with a length of 4339 kilometers.
Jancura files criminal charges for defamation against three heads of the CSSD.
The owner of the Student Agency, Radim Jancura, today raises criminal charges for defamation against three social-democracy leaders.
Against Michal Hasek from the district of Südmähren, Radko Martinek from the Pardubice district and the head of Olomouc district, Martin Tesarik.
Jancura said this in a twenty-minute interview in the radio journal.
The lawsuit is related to the company's effort to operate regional train traffic in some districts.
However, all regions ultimately signed long-term contracts with the Czech Railways.
All three leaders attack us and say that we take out the raisins, but we only submitted the offer about the scope that was coordinated with them.
They claim that we are expensive than the Czech Railway because we would know the prices of the Czech Railway, which were completed last week.
So we have no choice but to defend ourselves.
Because of this slander of the company Student Agency, Jancura told the radio journal.
At the same time, he argued that he has evidence from all districts that they have taken their steps in accordance with European law.
That's why we're preparing an indictment for the European Commission.
Hasek considers the filing of the criminal complaint for the further continuation of Jancura's media show, which serves him as a free advertisement for his society as well as for his own person.
Of course, I am ready to defend my good name as well as the good reputation of my district, and I also call on other leaders to do so.
I assume that it will also become clear on this matter that it is Jancura who manipulates the public in the matter of regional railing traffic and uses lying and untrue arguments, Hasek said.
Jancura already reported last week that he had invested several million crowns in the advertisement, which will point to the detrimental step of the districts.
He told the radio journal that he interrupts the campaign after Christmas, and wants to continue it after New Year.
He clarified that he will lead the campaign against corruption as such.
Jancura initiated the campaign after the districts agreed with the Czech Railway at the instigation of regional railway transport and had not tendered tenders for the order in a height of 150 billion crowns.
According to Vancura, such conduct is unlawful and contrary to the requirements of the European Court of Justice.
Due to the criticism of train traffic in the districts, according to Jancura, he was already threatened by a deputy of the CSSD.
The only threat was if we don’t forego that we can also lose the bus licenses.
He introduced himself as a member of the CSSD, I know who it is, I don't say his name, Jancura told the radio journal today.
Vaccination against pneumococcals free of charge and voluntary
Immung against pneumococcals changes the usual from January.
She is said to be the first to be free of charge and voluntary for the families
But even now, voices are reporting that compulsory vaccination would be more effective.
The preparations for the introduction of free vaccination against pneumococcals culminate.
The pharmaceutical institute already determines the maximum prices of the vaccines that the health insurance companies have to pay.
The experts are also aware of the vaccination procedure.
New Vaccination
Vaccines free of charge for August children
From January, children can be vaccinated free of charge against pneumococcals, who were born on August 2 last year and later.
The condition is that they have not yet received a vaccine dose.
If you have already started vaccination last year or earlier, you also have to pay further repeat vaccination yourself.
The first vaccine dose must be given between the child’s 3 and 5 months of life.
Last year, a quarter of families vaccinated their children against pneumococci.
Parents paid 1600 crowns and more for one dose.
Four doses are required to vaccinate the youngest children.
As a whole, the professionals estimate that the health insurance companies issue 300,000 to 450,000 crowns per year for the vaccine doses.
However, you save on the treatment that the children would need without vaccination.
Until now, the state has spent about 500,000 per year on all children's land vaccinations.
Compulsory vaccination has been introduced with the so-called Janota package.
Pneumococci cause middle ear inflammation, but also serious infections including meningitis and blood poisoning.
Due to pneumococcal infections, up to 28 children aged up to 10 years died per year after examination in hospitals.
Outside the Czech Republic, the compulsory vaccination of children against pneumococcals has been introduced in about 40 countries.
Will vaccination be mandatory?
At the same time, it is said that this vaccination could be mandatory in the future.
From January, this should be paid for by the insurance companies and voluntary.
That compulsory vaccination would be better, mentioned, for example, the head of the Bürgervereinigung Nahlas, Rudolf Kalovsky.
His unification is already campaigning for pneumococcal vaccination in the long term.
The experts at the international conference, in which our association was involved, were not very satisfied with the fact that we have voluntary vaccination.
That's not entirely ideal.
On the other hand, we are happy that we have managed to enforce at least that.
I think that's the path we need to go now, Kalovsky cited.
However, the Ministry of Health does not want to change the vaccination procedure against pneumococcus.
At this now, we're not considering that at all, said his spokesman Vlastimil Sesen.
Voluntary vaccination has its disadvantages, the doctors claim.
Hana Cabrnochova, chairwoman of the specialist society of practical pediatricians, admits that voluntary vaccination has its disadvantages.
If all children were vaccinated, then one could think about the reduction of the repeat vaccination number "In a situation in which the vaccine is so expensive, we understand the political decision that this vaccination was started as a voluntary one," Cabrnochova says.
Experts expect more than 80 percent of parents to vaccinate their infants against pneumococcals starting next year.
Vaccination against pneumococcus is also groundbreaking in the fact that it is paid as the first area vaccination by the health insurance companies and not by the state.
Insurances should spend the money on prevention, saving them for further treatment, Cabrnochova explains.
Doctors have to buy the vaccines themselves.
This, however, entails a concern of practical pediatricians concerning the purchase of the vaccines.
According to the original project, the vaccines should be purchased by the insurance companies and the doctors should then receive them in the same way as other vaccines issued by the state to them.
Some MPs, however, feared that insurances "could put the money out of health insurance."
That's why doctors will have to buy the vaccines themselves and have to wait until the insurance companies pay them.
According to the estimates, the individual practical doctors will have to invest 40,000 krons per month, which is a significant sum.
It won't be easy, Cabrnochova says.
U.S. artist son involved in 'museum theft'
The son of famous American fantasy artist Frank Frazetta was accused of attempting to steal paintings worth 20 million dollars from his father's museum.
According to Pennsylvania police, Alfonso Frank Frazetta was trapped into his vehicle with a trailer while inviting 90 paintings.
It's said Frank Frazetta junior and another man had used an excavator to break into the museum in the Pocono mountains.
81-year-old Frank Frazetta Senior is famous for his depiction of figures such as Conan der Barbar and Tarzan.
Associated Press reported that the artist was staying in Florida at the time of the incident.
AP cited an unnamed police officer who said Frank Frazetta Junior may have been motivated by a family feud.
The agency reported, according to a policial affidavit, Frazetta Junior claimed he was instructed by his father "that he was supposed to enter the museum by all means to bring all the paintings into a camp."
The agency said Frank Frazetta senior denied granting any permission.
Labork defends budget and tax hikes
Ministers have defended the tax increases and spending freezes announced in the pre-budget report against criticism from the opposition, the economy and unions.
Conservatives said Alistair Darling had let an opportunity "elapse" to show that he was serious about reducing the deficit by sliding the decisions until after the election.
The chancellor-exchequer also came under fire for targeting low- and middle-income workers.
His main proposals include a 0.5 percent increase in Society and a 1-percent cap on public wage deals for 2011.
Society annoyance
The unions have protested that low-wage earners are penalised for a recession they have not caused and have warned of imminent "problems."
Raising social security - to roughly 3 billion British pounds a year - has angered the private sector, which says this is a tax on jobs, although attention should be on the economic recovery.
The increase, limited to those earning more than £20,000 a year, will affect roughly 10 million workers.
FOCULATIONS OF PRELIMINARY BUDGET REPORT
Increase social security by a further 0.5% from April 2011
Shrinking the economy by more than the expected 4.75% this year
New 50% task on banker bonuses
1 per cent increase in corporate tax discarded for small businesses
tax reductions for electric vehicles and wind turbines
Increasing state pensions by 2.5%
According to Treasury estimates, with an annual income of £30,000, will be poorer by £90 a year and someone at £40,000 per year will be £190 poorer, while a person with £10,000 annual income will be £110.
Ministers said their target of deficit halving by 2013 meant "difficult decisions," but they also said that 60% of the burden of supplementary taxes would be accounted for by the 5% of top earners.
Finance Minister Stephen Timms denied that the outlined tax hikes and spending freezes compared to the necessary measures would be "a drop on the hot stone."
"These are big numbers. They will bring us the deficit halving and this is also absolutely necessary over the period of the next four years," he told the BBC.
Timms said he was confident the economy would return to growth by year-end.
Predictions by the Labour Party on future economic growth – affecting the scope of re-indebtedness and the speediness of deficit-removal – have been questioned after the chancellorgy chancellor has been forced to revise previous figures.
He said in his statement that the economy is likely to shrink by 4.75% this year - far more than the 3.5% decline predicted in April - while £3 billion in debt would be estimated.
'Missed opportunity'
Conservatives, who want to launch a new advertising campaign on Thursday to warn against Labour's "debt crisis," said the planned indebtedness of 789 billion pounds over the next six years was unsustainable.
They added that ministers have failed to put forward a credible plan on how they will repay this and "cynically" press themselves "cynically" against difficult decisions until after the election - which must take place no later than June 2010.
"They didn't say anything meaningful about what they're going to do," shadow finance secretary Philip Hammond said. "They've had their opportunity to do so, and they've let them pass."
"The significant announcements will only be made after the election, whoever wins them."
Roger Bootle, Deloitte
Labour insists that schools, hospitals and the police will be saved from future cuts, unlike Conservatives' proposals, which see the deficit capping coming faster and sooner.
But the Liberal Democrats said the money gained through the tax hikes and spending freezes will be used to cover spending for next year, not to reduce the deficit, and therefore argue that the plans were "built on sand."
The BBC's political editor, Nick Robinson, said some at the timing of the announcements - such as the above inflativation adjustment among some disability pensions next April - would expose the chancellor of chancellors to the accusation of voting, since the money could simply be retrieved next year.
Labour insists on allocating extra aid when people need it most and that the situation will be re-examined in September 2010.
"Hohler budget"
Although ministers did not disclose budget details beyond 2011, economists say the pressure on public spending won't come to fruition untile in the medium term.
The recognized institute for tax investigations said the Chancellor's figures would mean hefty smears in many areas, possibly in the future also in the fields of transport, higher education, science and defense.
"It just shifts the pain to later," said its leader, Robert Chote.
"The significant announcements are not made until after the election, whoever wins them," added Roger Bootle, an economic adviser at accounting firm Deloitte.
"This was only the hollow advance budget report. The markets recognize that."
FBI investigates 'arrests of U.S. citizens in Pakistan'
The FBI is investigating the arrest of five reported U.S. citizens on suspicion of extremist ties in Pakistan.
The men were arrested during a raid on a house in Sarghoda, in the eastern Panjab province, the American embassy in Pakistan told the BBC.
The FBI said it's being investigated whether these are the same men who were reported missing last month in the U.S
The U.S. State Department said they are also looking for information about the men.
Three of them are said to be Pakistan ancestry, one is of Egyptian origin and the other has a Yemeni background.
"If they are American citizens, of course we are very interested in the charges they are accused of and under what circumstances they are being helded," Spokesman Ian Kelly said.
FBI spokeswoman Katherine Schweit said the agency was aware of the arrests and was linked to the families of the missing students.
"We are working with the Pakistani authorities to find out their identities and the purpose of their stay there, in case it's actually the missing students," she said.
The Pakistan Embassy in Washington said the men were arrested in a house belonging to the uncle of one of the students.
He said local police had already been interested in the house earlier and that no charges have yet been filed against the arrested men.
The Reuters news agency reported that Secretary of State Hillary Clinton declined to comment on the arrests, but she said the U.S. "must work more closely with Afghanistan and Pakistan to eradicate the infrastructure of terrorism that continues to recruit and train people."
The five students were reported missing by their families in northern Virginia at the end of November.
Amnesty denounces 'abuses' in Iran
According to a report by the human rights organization Amnesty International, human rights in Iran are still just as bad as in the past 20 years.
The report details "abuse patterns" by the regime, both before and after June's contested presidential elections.
One man cited in the report said he was beaten and burned with cigarettes. Another said he was threatened with rape.
Iran rejected earlier criticism of its human rights record.
Officials said such criticism was politically motivated.
After the contested election of President Mahmoud Ahmadinejad, thousands of people were arrested and dozens killed in Iran, resulting in the biggest street protests since the 1979 Islamic revolution.
Dozens were given prison sentences and accusers said at least five people were sentenced to death.
BBC Tehran correspondent Jon Leyne, who is now located in London, said that at the start of the protests, the supreme leader, Ayatollah Ali Khamenei, acknowledged some of the abuse claims and ordered the closure of the Kahrizak detention centre.
But since then there has been no tolerance on the part of the authorities for criticism, our correspondent said.
'Wrong Confession'
Amnesty International cited the report by 26-year-old computer science student Ebrahim Mehtari, who said he was charged with "working with Facebook networks" and for protesting the election results.
"They slammed me in the face many times," he was quoted as saying.
"I was burned with cigarettes under my eyes, in my neck, on my head . . . They threatened me with death and they humiliated me."
After five days, he signed a false confession and, still bleeding and half unconscious, was released on the street, Amnesty said in a statement.
Beaten presidential candidate Mehdi Karroubi said some protesters who had been arrested after the election were tortured to death in prison in August and raped others.
Iranian authorities denied rape allegations, but admitted that abuses took place.
Amnesty also cited the case of a former detainee who said he had been helded in a container in a Kahrizak detention centre at 75 others.
Amnesty acknowledged that Iran's parliament and judicial authorities had set up a committee to investigate post-election unrest and the government's responses to it, but according to Amnesty, the mandates and power of these bodies would be unclear and the results won by the parliamentary committees had not been released.
The group said at least 90 people have been arrested in the past three weeks to prevent further demonstrations.
Ski slope has been set up in the Stadtpark
More than 100 tons of artificial snow have been used to turn a landmarket in Bristol into a competition track.
The ski slope of this park event attracted 16 semi-professional skiers and snowboarders who fought for cash prizes.
On the top of the slope in Brandon-Hill-Park there was a short ski jump at the beginning of a 100-meter-long slope.
Student John Hickman, who is studying medicine for the fourth year, said there was a beautiful view from the top of the slope, but it was also quite intimidating.
It's a great view with all the lights of the city glistening below.
He added: "It's some of the best skiers and snowboarders in the country here - some real talents."
Prizes were awarded for the best tricks or tricks performed on the hillside.
After Thursday night's event, the snow will be left to melting.
Still her and us
Can Islam be french?
Pluralism and pragmatism in a secular state.
At a time when Swiss voters voted for a construction ban on minarets and there is widespread concern about the alleged Islamization of Europe, John Bowen, an American academic, has written a well-founded and well-considered account of whether Muslims can be integrated into one of Europe’s declared most secularized societies.
Some readers will read this new book out of admiration for the author's last work: "Why the French don't love headscarves" (original title: "Why the French Don't Like Headscarves") (2006), an elegant and densely arguing study of a quarrel that divided and occupied the country over a decade and a half and whose effects can still be perceived today.
Bowen's latest book offers a broader and bolder spectrum.
As a good anthropologist, he wants to know not only what the politicians and the mediaries say about Islam in France, but what really happens in the field.
He's spented months in mosques, schools and institutes, which now offer the 5 to 6 million Muslims in France, as Bowen calls "Islamic open spaces."
He's a good listener, he's reflecting debates between teachers and students about the questions they worry the most.
Should a Muslim get married (or both) in a mosque or town hall?
Should young Muslims be lectured on the evolution and rights of homosexuals?
Can a Muslim woman marry a non-Muslim man?
Is it permissible for a Muslim to use an interest-based banking system to get a mortgage?
It's these seemingly profane affairs, he claims, who make up daily life, quite in contrast to the political dramas that preoccupy the mediaries.
The author recognizes a new generation of imams, teachers and intellectuals, none of them with a well-known name, possibly with the exception of Tarik Ramadan, a Swiss-born Arab-Muslim scholar and academist.
This new generation seeks to open the debate about how it is possible, on the one hand, to be a good Muslim and, on the other hand, a good citizen in a modern secular society.
They don't have all the arguments on their side.
Conservatives are suspicious of the idea of French or European Islam.
The thinkers and activists Bowen surveyed are mostly divided with their Salafi counterparts—proponents of Puristic Sunni Islam associated with Saudi Arabia—that show a small but influential presence among Europe’s Muslims these days.
Bowen is of the opinion that Muslim values and French secularism could be compatible.
But an agreement requires giving and taking on both sides.
He calls into question the extent to which French policymakers (and the intellectual elite who so passionately guarden the separation of church and state) are truly committed to pluralism.
He hints that Muslims may experience harsher treatment than Catholics, Protestants and Jews, who in turn had to make their historical compromise with secular republicanism.
He recognizes a "tightening of value screws" rather than a growing pragmatism.
Can Islam be French? After reading this book, one is inclined to say "yes, but not yet".
The world's most bleak outlook
The Arctic is changing faster and more dramatically than any other environmental region on Earth.
The ice she labels melts at alarming speed and takes life with it that can't survive anywhere else.
Oil, gas, shipping and fishing interests have entered the recently open water, with diplomats, lawyers and now also authors in their keel waters.
Richard Ellis, an author and illustrator, in "On Thin Ice," draws a natural history of the icon of the north, the polar bear.
Proclaimed in the complicated history and politics of whaling, he describes the long tradition of Arctic explorers, who proved themselves hunting the polar bears.
Admiral Nelson's encounter with a polar bear as a brave 14-year-old enshrine to the sea, who fought only with his muskete's rifle buttock, is certainly a legend, but others are true.
Young bears who were caught swam behind the bodies of their recently slain mothers were taken to zoos and circuses.
A circus performance at the beginning of the 20th century showed 75 polar bears at a performance.
Even today, zoo facilities still typically own a millionth of the habitat of a wild old animal. The climate is rarely suitable: the polar bear in Singapore became quite green from the algae growing in the hollow hairs of its fur.
Click here to find out more!
Ellis refers to the accounts of other authors and historians and often returns to the hustler for the survival of the species.
Although banned in Norway, America and Russia, succumbance continues in Greenland and Canada, where hunters in helicopters and motor sled-driving Inuit use powerful assault rifles to track down their prey.
Ironically, in the waters in the north of these two hunting nations, the ice will last the longest.
In his new book "After the Ice," Alun Anderson, a former editor of New Scientist's science magazine, offers a clear and disturbing description of the Arctic's science and a gripping insight into how the future might develop.
It’s not that scientists all have answers.
Neither atmospheric scientists nor oceanographers can adequately explain the speed of the changes.
However, this is not due to a lack of explanation attempts.
Fridtjof Nansen’s pioneering journeys at the end of the 19th and the beginning of the 20th-century gave an idea of the swirling of the packet ice.
Submarines in the Cold War measured the shifting burrs while undercoating.
A useful addition are satellite surveys and the large-scale application of measuring probes on icebergs, but still a lot is achieved by intrepid people who camp outside in the cold.
Anderson takes a look into the peculiar, tiny world of the inflow system within the Arctic ice formed by saltwater rinnsals squeezed during freezing.
But, starting from the bear on the ice to the microscopically small miracles in the ice, everyone is threatened by it when the summer ice once passes, which is expected in the period between 2013 and 2050.
"In a few months ... this could be us"
Naval infantries were among the 6,000 who laid wreaths in Arlington
The upper-free De'Angello Robinson traveled by bus for seven hours to put a single wreath on the grave of a soldier he had never met.
For him and the other naval infantrymen, who took the trip from Camp Johnson in Jacksonville, North Carolina, to Arlington National Cemetery to decorate graves, the day was a tribute to the men and women in whose footsteps they followed.
In a few months, from wherever we're brought back from, we could be, Robinson said of the fallen soldiers.
If that was us, I'd wish someone was doing the same for me, so I'm trying to show my respect like that.
He was one of more than 6,000 volunteers who had gathered on Saturday morning to place wreaths on the graves of veterans in several sections of the Arlington cemetery.
Morrill Worcester, owner of the glurine manufacturer Worcester Wreath Company in Harrington, Maine, established this tradition in 1992, when he and several others decided to decorate several hundred graves at the cemetery in Arlington.
It is now an 18-year-old train and Morrill and his wife Karen, who undertake this journey every year, stop in different cities along the way to hold events dedicated to people in the military and victims of terrorism.
The Worcesters also formed a non-proficiency organization, Wreath's Across America (wreaths throughout America), which extended the event to other states as well.
This year, volunteers placed more than 16,000 wreaths on the tombers at the Arlington Cemetery, in areas at the Pentagon, on tombs at the Fayetteville National Cemetery in Arkansas, at Battery Park in New York and at the memorial site for United Airlines flight 93 in Shanksville, Pennsylvania.
The Walmart Foundation donated more than 150,000 dollars for buying and transporting the wreaths for the second time.
Charleen Hunt, 70, from Westminster, said she made the trip to Arlington in memory of her husband, a deceased professional soldier.
Although her husband isn't buried in Arlington, Hunt said, this is her way to give something back to those who made sacrifices.
She said this is a small contribution on how we civilians can support our soldiers.
It's just like visiting a family burial site.
Packed in layers of warm clothes, the volunteers wandered across the extensive lawns and hills of the cemetery.
The volunteers had older tombs from World War 1 and 2 and the Vietnam War as a destination, as these are not visited as often as the newer graves, according to the organizers.
The mood was cheerful, children played and couples held by the hand as they wore the fresh pine twigs to the greying tombstones.
Each round wreath was dark green with a small red loop attached to the upper part.
Section 60, which is located nearby and where mainly veterans of the wars are buried in Iraq and Afghanistan, remained almost quiet.
The United Service Organizations donated 1,000 wreaths to adorn this section, but the gloomy mood of the current wars fulfilled this area.
Individuals wept openly, others prayed and families and friends stuck to each other in the icy air.
Some read the names carved in stone, while others bent into small black-and-white place cards that had been put into the damp mud for those whose tombstones were not yet finished.
Sandra Lockwood was one of the many mothers shedding bitter tears in front of her son's tomb.
She drove 8 hours from Zanesfield, Ohio, to visit the tomb of naval infrantry non-commissioned officer David Shane Spicer, who fell in battle in July.
No one should ever forget why we're free ..., my son paid for it, she said.
“If I’ve not been for a long time, I want someone to remember him.”
U.S. government contractor arrested by Cuban authorities
The Cuban government has arrested a U.S. government contractor who sold mobile phones and notebook computers in the country, State Department officials said Saturday.
The contractor, who has not yet been identified, works for Development Alternatives Inc., headquartered in Bethesda.
The company works on projects for clients, such as USAID, the U.S. agency for international development and for the World Bank.
U.S. advocacieties in Havana are trying to gain access to the detainee arrested on December 5.
The special charges have not yet been released, although under Cuban law a Cubanian citizen or a foreign visitor can be arrested for almost anything, under the allegation of "endangerance."
All so-called counter-revolutionary activities, which include light protests and critical writings, carry a risk of arrest.
Anti-government graffiti and statements are considered a serious crime.
Cuba has a growing blogging community led by popular reporter Yoani Sánchez, who often describes how she and her husband are persecuted and attacked by government officials.
Sánchez has repeatedly asked for a permit to leave the country to accept prizes, but this has always been rejected.
Arresting an American contractor is likely to increase tensions between the Castro-brother's communist administration in Cuba and the Obama administration that had sought a "slow" rapprochement to improve relations with the island.
News of the arrest was first reported Friday night by The New York Times.
The new U.S. policy puts emphasis on ensuring that the United States provides a return if the Cuban government takes concrete steps in that direction, such as the release of political prisoners and creating more space for opposition.
Mobile phones and notebooks are legal in Cuba, even though they are new and sought-after products in a country where the average merit of a government employee is 15 dollars a month.
The Cuban government has only allowed ordinary citizens to buy a mobile phone since this year, they are mainly used for sending text messages, since a 15-minute telephone call would cost a daily wage.
Internet use is extremely restricted on the island.
It is accessible in expensive hotels and there to foreign visitors and at some government agencies, such as universities.
Cubans who wish to log in often have to report their names to the government.
Access to many websites is restricted.
In Cuba, the arrest of an American is rare.
Most of the few U.S. citizens who are imprisoned in Cuba for crimes such as drug smuggling, said Gloria Berbena, the U.S
Berbena said she couldn't provide further information on the arrest.
Arrest and imprisonment are clearly wrong.
An activity that would be legal in any other free society - levying free mobile phones - is a crime in Cuba, said Jose Miguel Vivanco, head of the American section of the Human Rights Watch group, which recently released a harsh report on freedom in Cuba titled "New Cas Castro," a nod to the inauguration of Raul Castro as the country's leader who replaced the sick older
Vivanco said the defendants would very often be arrested, convicted and imprisoned within a day.
He said that probably any solution would be political, and that the Cuban government often provokes a negative reaction in the United States, just when both countries are moving towards each other for more willingness to engage in dialogue.
Pakistani government officials uncover plan to send men to Afghanistan
Pakistani authorities on Saturday discovered an alleged leading head of a conspiracy aimed at sending five men from northern Virginia to Afghanistan to kill U.S. soldiers, and said they hope this case could help uncover an extensive network of terrorist recruiters searching the Internet for radicalized young men.
Investigators said they had sought an opaque insurgent known as Saifullah, who invited the men to Pakistan after picking them because one of them on the Internet video site YouTube had made approving comments on terrorist attacks.
Saifullah became the leader of these men after their arrival in Pakistan and tried to help them reach the remote area in Pakistan's tribal belt, which is home to Al Qaeda and its terrorist training camps.
But a representative of Pakistan's intelligence service who was briefed on the matter said on Saturday Saifullah could not convince al-Qaeda leaders that the men aren't part of a CIA conspiracy to submerge the terrorist network.
As a result, they were held for days in the eastern city of Sargodha, far from the inhospitable mountains in the northwest, which have become a terrorist refuge.
They were seen as part of a covert operation.
That's why they were rejected," said the government official, who wanted to remain anonymous due to the sensitivity of the matter.
The government official said that the men were by no means discouraged and continued to try to get the right recommendations to gain access to the al-Qaeda camps when they were detained by Pakistani penitential authorities.
The fall of the five men - who continue to be in Pakistan and are questioned by the FBI - underscores the pivotal role of recruiters in identifying future terrorists and possibly even more important in deciding who is trustworthy.
Since the September 11, 2001 attacks, U.S. intelligence has made it a matter of utmost urgency to accommodate human assets within Al Qaeda.
The recruiters of the organization act as gatekeepers who don't let in those whose commitment to Holy War isn't serious enough and those who could be spies.
American would-be recruits are subjected to a special examination by A Qaeda, analysts said.
However, due to their potential access to U.S. goals and their propaganda value, they are also seen as tremendously attractive to the group.
Evan Kohlmann, highest-ranking analyst at the NEFA Foundation based in the U.S., said terror groups have also become much more cautious in recent years in terms of who they allow access, because U.S. intelligence agencies have become experts in their recruitment methods.
If one tries to smuggle someone into these groups, what better path could one go than to follow the recruitment model that so many have already followed?, Kohlmann said.
The model is now much more based on the internet.
Tens of thousands of protesters demand climate "action" in Copenhagen
Tens of thousands of protesters marched through the streets here on Saturday, demanding bolder action on climate protection from the negotiators at the city's Bella convention center.
Protesters said 100,000 people had joined the event, while the police estimates were more likely to come from about 25,000.
The event was relatively peaceful, although a handful of masked activists detonated small explosive devices near some downtown government buildings.
One day, when little happened in the UN-funded climate negotiations, thousands of activists migrated through the city with banners that said "There is no Planet B" and one in Spanish that declared "The earth says "enough."
Some stars joined the protest, for example the Danish model, photographer Helena Christensen, who said the trips to Peru, her mother's country of birth, make her aware of what heartbreaking problems the country is struggling with due to the already noticeable effects of climate change.
That's part of the motumes why I chose to take part in the big demonstration - to pass on the word and appeal to the world leaders to bring about a fair, ambitious and binding agreement, she said.
It’s not an easy task, but it has to be done now and there’s no longer a way around it.
Police stopped the protesters from getting too close to the Bella centre and said they had arrested 19 people, mainly due to the fact that they were either masked or carrying pocket knives.
According to Danish law, these actions are prohibited during demonstrations.
According to a bystander who did not want to be named since he is involved in the climate negotiations, masked protesters in black clothing detonated several explosive devices near Copenhagen's main canal, near which are several ministries.
They threw them near the buildings, he said, adding that they began as luminous missiles, but were then followed by "a few big explosions."
Inside the convention center, people gathered in front of television screens to watch the demonstration all afternoon.
But the protest has apparently not reached the consciousness of the main officials, such as Su Wie, China's chief climate negotiator.
When asked if he thought that the demonstration had a constructive effect on international considerations, he replied in English: "This is something I wasn't aware of."
He then proceeded in Mandarin and said: "Because the venue is big, I can't hear what's happening outside."
He noted that it depended on the individual's perspective on whether the demonstration would harm or help.
It demonstrates the focus of the general public and various sectors on the issue of climate change, he said.
On the other hand, "it can also be said that they disturb the negotiations or the freedom of other people."
Examination data shows stubborn breed gap
Testimonies on math performance were welcome good news last week for the public schools in Washington D.C.
Although the Washington district is still far behind the country's schools that produce peak performances, testimonies show that the fourth- and eighth-graders have made faster progress over the past two years than cities enclosing Atlanta, Chicago and New York.
But what is included in the latest National Assessment of Educational Progress (NAEP) is the stubborn performance gap between African-american and white students, both at the local and national levels.
In the last two years, the average scoring number of white fourth graders from Washington D.C. rose from 262 to 270 (on a scale of 500), but that of their African-American peers rose by only three points from 209 to 212.
In reality, the performance gap rose from 53 to 58 points between 2007 and 2009.
The progress of African-American eighth graders basically remained the same, falling by a statistically negligible point from 245 to 244.
The average score of white students was not included in the test data because the sample size was not large enough.
The picture over a six-year period is also not more encouraging.
The chasm that separated white and black fourth graders in 2003, when the first NAEP was captured in this district, amounted to 60 scale points (262 to 202).
And although the score that children of both groups have reached has increased in this period of time, the difference has hardly decreased with 58 points now.
Some education supporters of the district last week expressed their concern that the results celebrated by school chancellor Michelle A. Rhee and Democratic mayor Adrian M. Fenty were driven forward for the most part by white pupils, who are already high-performing children anyway.
This suggests that we raised the overall rating by treating those at the upper end of the scale, which is problematic and difficult, said Jeff Smith, board member of D.C. Voice, a non-profit group advocating for educational equality in Washington D.C.
I don't jump up and down for a span of two or three points, said Democratic Washington City Council member Kwame R. Brown, who has a daughter in fourth-grade Eaton Elementary School.
Brown, a common critic of Rhee's management style, said the message of exam scores is that the city's middle schools desperately need attention.
Of course, you always want to see the positive signs, I respect that.
But what frightens me is that we don’t spend nearly the time and energy that we would have to spend on our middle school.
Rhee said the district needs to continue to find better ways to meet the needs of low-performing pupils.
This fall, for example, some teachers are trained to use a new reading curriculum, the Wilson reading system, aimed at pupils in the upper grades of elementary and middle school, who have not mastered basic skills of reading at the beginning of their school career and which are significantly behind their classmates of the same age.
Everyday math math, a curriculum that comes to the fore from kindergarten to sixth-grade games and lifelike experiences, has been brought to Washington under former school inspector Clifford Janey and this curriculum is attributed to some of the NAEP progress reported last week.
I mean that we must continue to work to let the best measures benefit the pupils who are below the grade level, Rhee said.
Others say that the NAEP results highlight the question of whether Rhee can proceed to improve the overall performance of the system, while continuing to provide the extra money and resources for the low-performing schools that are necessary to reduce the gap.
This is a serious dilemma for Michelle Rhee, said Bruce Fuller, professor of education at the University of California at Berkeley who examines urban exam results.
The conventional remedy is to focus its resources and management reforms on the schools located in the poorest areas of Washington D.C.
The objective could lead to political after-effects working against this important item.
The weak point is, of course, as with other urban school inspectors, that she's trying to hold the white and black middle class.
Our focus is to ensure that we build a system of great schools.
We need to strengthen our schools in the neighborhoods so that all families, regardless of where they reside, can be sure that their children can receive excellent public education.
Janey, who is now school inspector in Newark, said in an interview this week that one of the most significant elements in closing the performance divide is to extend the traditional public school year, which is currently 180 days in Washington, to compete with charter schools.
You have to raise the school year to nearly 200 days to have the strength to change, said Janey, who came to an agreement with his teachers' union last year for an agreement on 185 days a year, which he regards as "a milestone" on the way to a greater increase in the next contract.
Higher education for more people than future prospects
The coach arrives at Gettysburg College with a loud whistle.
Graciela Rodriguez, 12, gets out and blinks for a moment at the sight of the white columns, brick facades and emerald green lawn.
Graciela's parents barely graduated high school in El Salvador.
Until recently, Graciela, who was rescued at Silver Spring and now lives in Riverdale, had never set foot on a college campus.
Their stance and that of the group's other eighth graders exploring Gettysburg, where teaching costs 38,690 dollars a year, was that of an apprehentic visitor rather than that of an enthusiastic future student.
"Yeah, I'll be here soon!" exclaimed Graciela, who would like to study medicine when the guide proclaimed that they had now entered the natural sciences building.
"Wow, really?" she said thoughtfully when she was told the school's low professor-to-student ratio.
The college in Pennsylvania was the 7) that the children visited on their three-day tour and now they've fully recorded the intended message: the question isn't whether you go to college.
The question is where.
On the one hand, there is a growing concern about the large number of U.S.-born children of Hispanic immigrants who don’t finish high school or get pregnant as teenagers, but on the other hand, there are also hundreds of thousands who receive college education needed to move up to the middle class.
In truth, one in five of those "second generation" of Hispano-Americans closes college - which is a remarkable accomplishment since many of their immigrated parents, mostly from Mexico or Central America, came to the U.S. without having graduated from high school.
Their success stories are important, researchers say, because for a generation that will play a huge role in the country's workforce, show the way forward.
Those who examine high-performing children say these often have a natural affinity for the school and an inherent drive for success.
Many of them also have parents who set their children’s lofty goals and find ways to compensate for their lack of familiarity with American schools.
But counseling programs also play a big role by helping Graciela and millions of children like her get to college, especially if those efforts persist over an extended period of time.
If you look at low-income children whose parents don't have the experience and skills to help them find their way through the system, then at some point a single measure won't solve the problem either, said Patricia Gándara, a researcher at the University of California at Davis, who studied Hispan-American students.
We need to think about providing a supportive network for these children from pre-school to high school.
The federal program, funded by Graciela's college tour, is a useful example.
It's known as GEAR UP − Turning up a gear - and provides more than 300 million dollars a year for local school systems to start college preparatory programs that start when low-income students are in middle school and keep running until they've graduated high school.
According to the U.S. Department of Education, the program has helped more than 10 million pupils since 1999, more than 60% of whom have gone to college.
Iraqi Oil Ministry concludes treaties with 10 foreign oil companies
Despite concerns about violence and political instability, at two auctions concluded on Saturday, the Iraqi government has been able to get larger oil companies to rebuild its damaged infrastructure.
The 10 contracts concluded with foreign oil companies of the Iraqi Oil Ministry suggest that China, Russia and European oil companies are willing to play an important role in renovating the Iraqi oil industry that has been paralyzed by war and economic sanctions for decades.
American companies were only able to record shares in two of the 10 auctioned fields for themselves.
Seven American companies had paid for participation in the second auction, which began on Friday.
The only company that made a bid has pulled the shorter.
Two American companies were able to conclude contracts for fields auctioned in June.
Thin-sown representatives of American oil multinationals in the opening of Iraq's oil industry surprised analysts.
Iraq has finally opened its gates after 6 years of war and, instead of U.S. corporations, Asians and Europeans are leading the way, Ruba Husari, the editor of Iraq Oil Forum, told an online news service.
There will be no further offers in Iraq for a long time.
Security concerns still underlined by massively coordinated bombardment on Tuesday and the political instability caused by the withdrawal of U.S. military have likely prevented American oil companies from doing so, according to analysts said they were getting more involved in Iraq, which possesses the world's third-largest proven crude reserves.
U.S. firms were disadvantaged in some cases because competitors, especially the Chinese and other state-controlled energy companies, have significantly lower wage costs and are more likely to take risks because they don't have to account to shareholders.
Exxon-Mobil and Occidental Petroleum Inc. were the only U.S. companies that were able to enter into contracts with the ministry.
Larger U.S. companies, such as Chevron and ConocoPhillips, who maintain close ties with Iraq's oil ministry and have stood by technical advice in recent years, have gone blank.
Russian companies Lukoil and Gazprom were the key players in two of the contracts awarded this weekend.
The state-owned China National Petroleum Corporation has bid for more contracts than any other company and was able to go home with large packages of contracts for two larger fields.
We all know, oil ministry spokesman Assam Jihad said China is on its way to becoming one of the greats of the global economy as well as a technology power.
We are convinced that the Chinese company can compete with its co-bidders and fulfill its commitments to Iraq.
Companies pre-selected forbidding submitted bids, which were then compared to the fee per barrel of oil that the ministry was willing to pay in order to increase the output of each field beyond the current level.
Al Qaeda's top planner apparently killed in Pakistan
In an apparent U.S. missile strike along the Pakistani border with Afghanistan, a top Al Qaeda deployment planner is said to have been killed this week, U.S
This, if it were confirmed, would be the second deadly attack on a high-in-leader from the terrorist organization this autumn.
Saleh al-Somali was one of the two Arabs believed to have been killed by a number of missiles that hit their vehicle on Tuesday near the town of Miran Shah in North Waziristan province, according to U.S
Local authorities reported that the missiles were fired by a type of drone used by the CIA in Pakistan's lawless tribal belt.
They were driving in a white vehicle towards the Afghan border when the car was hit, an official from a Pakistani civilian intelligence agency from Miran Shah said by phone.
Local residents suspected of belonging to the militants ran to the spot and quickly took all that was left of the "fully destroyed bodies."
Local authorities were unable to confirm the identities of the victims, but two U.S. anti-terrorism officials cited indefinite evidence that Somali was among the dead.
Somali has been described as al-Qaeda's leading operations by terrorist groups outside the Afghanistan-Pakistan region.
He was involved in conspiracies around the world, noted a senior official who wished to remain anonymous and referred to the delicate nature of U.S. air strikes on Pakistani soil.
Assuming its central role as a given also included the planning of attacks against the U.S. and Europe.
He accepted strategic instructions from Al Qaeda's top leaders and converted them into operational plans for possible terrorist attacks.
The second U.S. official said Somali had risen rapidly in the al-Qaeda hierarchy and maintained best ties with other extremist groups in the region.
He may not be known to some Americans, the second official said, but this by no means reduces the danger he posed to us and our allies.
If his death were confirmed, Somali would be the second leading Al Qaeda or Taliban leader who had been killed since September when Najmuddin Jalolov, the leader of a militant faction in the tribal area and three other top activists were killed.
The pace of attacks by CIA drones has declined from an average of six operations a month to two since the summer, according to a summary of the Long War Journal, a website maintained by a non-profit organization.
The decline may be based on improved tactics by the terrorist groups that have taken steps to reduce their vulnerability while brutally killing suspected informants at the same time.
Leading U.N. envoyant in Kabul will step down
U.N. envoyance Kai Eide said on Friday that he would step down from office in March, ending a stormy term that has been tarnished by claims of widespread corruption in the U.N.-based Afghan presidential election.
Swore departure occurs when the Obama administration has decided to send another 30,000 U.S. troops to Afghanistan.
The U.N. envoy said he supports the troop increase, but he also expressed his concerns that the U.S. timetable for military withdrawal, which is scheduled to begin in 18 months, could prompt other NATO administrations to withdraw their armed forces.
We need to speed up the build-up of Afghan security forces and thereby send the right signal to Afghans that they can trust the international community, Eide said in a telephone interview from Kabul.
Commitment must be in the long term.
The Norwegian diplomat also urged the United States and other military powers to increase the number of international civilian aides designed to assist in Afghanistan's political shift.
The troop increase on the military side must correspond to an increase on the civilian side, he said.
Eide said that he did not resign from office, but only fulfilled an agreement made to his family in March 2008 to remain in Kabul for only two years.
He said that he wanted the notification to the UN
Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon is now moving forward so that he has time to arrange for a replacement.
What I've said is he'd better start looking for a successor, Eide said.
When I came here, there was a two-month vacuum between my predecessor's departure and my arrival.
Ban has begun the search for a replacement, according to U.N. representatives.
U.N. officials say he has the Swedish-Italian-born Staffan di Mistura, who recently led the U.N. mission in Baghdad and considered Frenchman Jean-Marie Guéhenno, who has previously led U.N. peace operations.
Oath's position in Afghanistan was put to the test after his former deputy, Peter W. Galbraith, accused him in September of favoring President Hamid Karzai in the country's presidential election and covering up evidence of massive voter fraud.
Eide has denied all accusations, but he also said that the accusations of Galbraith - which has been fired - "undoubtedly caused harm to the mission because there has already been a great deal of skepticism in terms of international interference in the election."
Eide said that he recommended the appointment of a leading civilian representative to coordinate the relief efforts of American-leaded forces in Afghanistan.
He also insisted that the U.N. leadership allow his successor to hire more personnel from the United States and other Western countries donating to the Afghan mission, saying that this would boost their confidence in getting their money used properly.
Eide expressed his disappointment in restricting his forces in Afghanistan, saying the cumbersome U.N. cessation settlements have undermined his ability to bring in capable people.
The U.N. rules are designed in such a way that I have been able to hire only one person since May, he said.
That's catastrophic and mustn't go on like this.
U.S. Secretary of Defense Gates: Iran must face additional sanctions
Defense Secretary Robert M. Gates said Friday that world powers will soon impose "significant additional sanctions" on Iran for failing to participate in talks on its nuclear plans.
Gates spoke during a 7-day trip through the war zones in Afghanistan and Iraq to a group of about 300 U.S. soldiers in northern Iraq and downplayed the prospect of military action against the Islamic Republic of Iran.
There are no good options for election in Iran, he said in response to a soldier's question about the likelihood of such a development.
"One of the things that lie on my soul is if we have learned something from Iraq over the last six years, the unpredictable to war."
The Obama administration is considering a sanctions package aimed at Iran's military and political elite, but Gates has announced that some sanctions could also affect the average Iranian.
He said that a "package of positive and negative incentives" is needed "to convince the Iranian government that it is in reality less protected with nuclear weapons," because "their people will suffer tremendously" from the sanctions.
On Friday, in a statement from White House spokesman Robert Gibbs, the American government joined top European politicians in the warning that Iran must face "believable" consequences if it fails to comply with its nuclear programent with the demands of the U.N. Security Council and its nuclear monitoring organization, the International Atomic Energy Agency.
Iran continues to claim that the knowledge of nuclear technology is to be gained only for peaceful purposes.
During talks in Geneva on October 1, Iran said it would resume talks restricting its nuclear program and would forego a substantial portion of its inventory of enriched uranium in exchange for much-needed nuclear fuels for its medical research reactor.
The U.S. government has pushed for this kind of agreement to build trust between the two parties and gain time for negotiations.
Iran, however, appears to have left negotiations on the tentative agreements since that time -- in part, experts say, because Iran's leadership disagrees over whether it should commit to the United States.
That Iran simply left the international community in terms of some proposals they had already agreed to in early October has, frankly, brought together the international community, including the Russians and Chinese, in a manner like they have never been in connection with key additional sanctions for Iran, Gates said.
President Obama has set the Dec. 31 as a deadline for Iran to respond to those proposals before he will pursue other options, including those that Secretary of State Hillary Rodham once once called "suppressive sanctions."
In a statement issued in New York, the Iranian Mission to the United Nations has condemned what it cited "baseless and unfounded assertions" by some Security Council members on Iran's nuclear activities on Thursday, saying they are ready to continue talks with the United States and five other world powers "to achieve an appropriate long-term resolution."
Gates, who was scheduled to return to Washington last Friday, met Iraqi Prime Minister Nuri al-Maliki in the morning before fleeing to the oil-rich Kurdish region in Iraq for meetings with the soldiers in Kirkuk and with Kurdish officials in Irbil.
Tensions between the Kurds and the Arab majority in Iraq remain high, in particular in terms of borders, property rights and the division of public revenues.
Gates urged both sides to lower the potential for conflict to prevent delays in U.S. plans to cut American troop levels from 115,000 to 50,000 by the end of August.
Gates also tried to disperse Kurdish concerns about the imminent troop withdrawal. U.S. representatives quoted Gates, who told Masud Barzani, president of the Kurdish regional government: "We're going to maintain your security, your prosperity and your autonomy within a united Iraq.
We won’t leave you.”
A change of course in aging China
Wang Weijia and her husband grew up surrounded by propaganda posters who gave them lectures that "Mother earth is too tired to entertain more children" and "A baby more means a grave more."
These lessons they have learned so well that, as government officials in Shanghai who, due to the low birth rate and aging population this summer, suddenly began to change course and encouraged young couples to have more than one child, their response was immediate and definitively: "No way."
We’ve already invested all our time and energy in a child.
There's nothing left for a second child, Wang, 31, a human resources manager with an 8-month-old son.
More than 30 years after the introduction of one-and-a-kind politics in China, which has spawned two generations of notoriously thick, warped single children affectionately called "little emperors," a populous crisis in the country draws up.
The average birth rate has fallen to 1.8 children per couple compared to 6 at the time this policy came into effect, the UN-
Department of Populous Issues fixed, while the number of the population of 60 years and older is expected to rise explosively from 16.7 percent of the population by 2050 to 31.1 percent. This is far above the global average of about 20 percent.
The imbalance is stronger in rich coastal cities with educated populations, such as Shanghai.
Last year, 60-year-olds and the elderly accounted for almost 22 percent of Shanghai's reported residents, while the birth rate has been below one child per couple.
Xie Lingli, head of the Shanghai City Population and Family Planning Commission, has said that couples of procrastinatable age must have babies "to help reduce the ratio of aging population proportions and alleviate the labor shortages of the future."
Shanghai will soon be "as old -- but not as rich -- as the industrialized countries, such as Japan and Sweden," she said.
A gradual relaxation
Enshrined in the country's constitution in 1978, China's one-child policy is likely to be arguably the most controversial injunction introduced by the ruling Communist Party to date.
Married couples who violate this policy must face enormous fines -- up to their three-fold annual salary in some regions -- - and discrimination in their workplace.
Chinese officials have credited it to this policy that the critical suspense of natural land resources could be avoided, whereas human rights representatives have condemned abusions in enforcing politics.
In rural regions, some civil servants have forced pregnant women who already had a child to abort.
Many couples had also performed gender-selective abortions, creating an unnaturally high relationship between men and women.
In recent years, government representatives for the population have gradually weakened their stances on one-child policies.
In 2004, they allowed more exceptions of the rule --- including city dwellers, members of ethnic minorities, and cases where both husband and wife have been solitary children -- and in 2007 the tone of many tough slogans has been mitigated.
Qiao Xiaochun, a professor at Beijing University's Institute of Population Research, said that government officials recently even discussed more radical changes, for example, allowing couples of two children where a partner is an only child.
Shanghai became China's first Chinese city in July to launch an aggressive campaign to promote more births.
Posters instructing families to have only one child have been replaced almost overnight by directives detailing who is eligible for a second child and how to apply for approval.
The city administration has sent out municipal employees and volunteers for family planning in order to visit couples at home and push through information sheets under the doors.
She also asked to provide psychological and financial advice for those who have more than one child.
The response wasn't exactly encouraging, family planning officials said.
Disappointing reaction
Although civil servants from a small rural town in the outdoor areas of Shanghai said an upswing can be seen in applications from couples who want a second child after the launch of the campaign, the urban districts, on the other hand, report no change.
At the municipality of Huinan with a population of 115,000, only four to five applications are still received as an example per month.
Disappointed Shanghai officials say the number of births in the city expected for 2010 will still be around 165,000 despite the campaign -- slightly higher than 2009, but lower than 2008.
Feng Juying, head of the family planning committee of the Shanghai municipality of Caolu, said that financial considerations were probably the main reason why many people don't want to have more children.
They want to give their first the best, she said.
Yang Jiawei, 27, and his wife, Liu Juanjuan, 26, said that they would like to have two children and this was also legally permitted.
But they, like many Chinese, have only minor government health and life insurance.
Without a social network, they say, that decision would be irresponsible.
People in the West misreasonably rate the one-child policy as a human rights issue, said Yang, a civil engineer whose woman is seven months pregnant with her first child.
Yes, we are robbed of the opportunity to have more than one child.
However, the problem isn’t simply in politics.
The problem is money.
Other couples state psychological reasons for their hesitancy.
Wang, the human resources manager, said she only wants a child because she was also an only child. "We were the focal point of the family and accustomed that everyone took care of us.
We're not used to taking care of others and we don't really want to."
Chen Zijian, a 42-year-old who owns a translation company, said it bluntly.
For parents from medium-sized companies with a double career, through which the birth rate decreases, he said, it revolves around being successful and you have to be selfish for that.
The age group of 20- to 39-year-old-year-olds grew up to see their parents fight during the early days of Chinese capitalism experiment and they don't want such a life for themselves," he said.
Even a single child, he said, places huge demands on parents' time.
A mother has to give up her social life for at least two years.
Then there's the spare issue -- "You have to rebuild your apartment" -- and life planning -- "If the child is 9 months old, you already have to have a CV ready for the best pre-schools."
Most of his friends are willing to go through this once, Chen said, but not twice.
Our generation is the first to achieve a higher standard of living, he said.
We don't want to make too many sacrifices.
U.N. group drafts plan to reduce emissions
The U.N.- funded climate conference -- which until now has been characterized by wild poses and reciprocative accusations -- once again set a focus on Friday with the release of a document that marks an ambitious reduction in greenhouse gases for the next 40 years, with industrialized nations having to shoulder the greatest burdens in the near future.
The text, which could provide the basis for a definitive political agreement to regulate greenhouse gases, has shown both the remaining obstacles, as well as the way forward.
But he has been seen as an important step forward in a negotiation that is running out of time as more than 100 top politicians from all over the world will arrive in Copenhagen next week.
The text, which was formulated by a specially created U.N. working group, is silent about the amount of money that rich countries should give to the poor countries so that they can master global warming in the short and long term.
It also offers a range of options for the key issues, including how industrialized and large emerging economies can limit their carbon emissions and how high the upper limit of global temperature rise that policymakers would support.
The trial is given great flexibility, according to John Coequyt, senior representative of the Sierra Club in Washington.
Michael Zammit Cutajar, who drafted the six-page document, has summarized a 180-page negotiating text to focus on what the UN's leading climate protection representative, Yvo de Boer, has called "the big picture."
It shows the layout of a possible agreement, in which the industrialized nations would jointly reduce their emissions by 25 to 45 percent by 2020, in comparison with the limits of 1990, while larger developing countries would reduce their emissions by 15 to 30 percent in the same period.
Together, countries could cut emissions by 50 to 95 percent by 2050.
The European Community also boosted Friday's talks through its assurances to allocate US$3.6 billion per year over the next three years in order to support poorer countries in adapting to the effects of climate change - from mastering floods and droughts to preventing deforestation.
Friday nevertheless brought the same verbal fireworks that had determined the conversations in the last week.
U.S. Climate Commissioner Todd Stern has rejected formulations calling for binding reductions in greenhouse gas emissions for industrialized countries, compared with voluntary reductions for larger emerging economies, if these aren't funded by industrialized nations.
This train announced that the Obama administration is running a tougher line with China as a representative of the Bush administration not even two years ago.
The United States won't sign an agreement without larger emerging economies standing up and taking action, according to Stern, who also complained that the text doesn't go far enough to ensure that the reductions could also be confirmed by outsiders.
Stern issued his statement an hour after the statement of Chinese Deputy Foreign Minister He Yafei, who said it was either lacking "of sanity" to the leading American climate negotiator or it was "highly irresponsible" from him that earlier in the week he said that the United States would not financially help China navigate global warming.
Due to the fact that the future economic direction of the major world powers is at stake, fault lines have opened both within the industrialized nations as well as between the industrialized nations and the emerging economies.
The current fight relates equally to the saving of individual economies, as well as to saving the planet, with China and the United States commanding themselves because of their commitments, while poorer countries claim that the two dozen most influential countries would ignore the scientific need to take bolder measures.
Ricardo Ulate, a delegate from Costa Rica, said that it is not surprising that the major powers are fighting over who should bear the cost of braking greenhouse gas emissions, as even affected countries have moved to act more aggressively in holding countries with very high greenhouse gas levies accountable for their actions.
This is clearly a game from which a new economic supremacy will develop, according to Ulate, who is also active as a regional climate change adviser for Conservation International in Mexico and Central America.
Some of the countries most heavily exposed to the impacts of climate change said they would continue to fight for a legally binding treaty in Copenhagen, although the leading attendees say the talks will lead to a political deal at best.
The Alliance of Small Island States, with 43 members, presented a 24-page draft treaty early Friday morning.
Artur Runge-Metzger, who is leading the international climate negotiations for the European Commission, said that the small island states' offensive puts "political pressure on the entire political process," partly because they are now unified and are calling for measures from emerging markets, such as China and India.
The talks got a new urgency as the delegates focused on the fact that most of the outstanding issues need to be clarified even before the arrival of government leaders to come to an agreement.
High-ranking representatives, such as Indian Environment Minister Jairam Ramesh and China's vice-minister escaped their planes and raced through the Bella Center's aisles to meetings behind closed doors and press conferences to stake out claims being negotiated during next week.
The mere scale of the assembly -- 13,000 people go in and out daily at the convention center, guitarry-playing activists host nocturnal shows in which they make fun of the countries that they think betray and sell, and contract drafts are passed on from hand-to-hand rather than by e-mail -- already poses a challenge.
Intensity is only just growing: almost all major ministers are here now and from Wednesday 60 government leaders will be in Copenhagen.
We're coming to the upper league, said Carlos Manuel Rodriguez, vice president for global politics at the organization Conservation International.
The heavyweights are coming.
Britain's first 225 km/h fast train redefines Monday's feeling
Monday morning at 5 a.m. 13, a whistle will permeate the darkness to announce a train departure representing a revolution in traffic in Britain.
The first high-speed commuter train in the country will reach 225 km/h on its lightning ride from Ashfort, Kent, to London St Pancras and travel 93 km in 38 minutes.
To Lord Adonis, the transport minister, Dame Kelly Holmes will then board the first Javelin commuter service from London.
He hopes the UK's new service will eventually connect to an international rail network of faster, more reliable trains and will connect the major metropolitan areas of the Midlands, the north and Scotland to London.
The neglected province could become vibrant commuter cities, work journey times would be radically reduced and the switch from plane and car to train would reduce national carbon emissions and traffic jams on the roads.
In theory, the three major parties believe in this vision.
But the many billions of British pounds needed for the construction of a new national rail network, the unavoidable planning battle and fears about environmental damage could still pull the brakes on the UK's high-speed tracks.
High Speed Two (HS2), a company founded in January designed to submit a feasibility study to the government, Lord Adonis will present the report on December 30, according to the Times.
As a result, he receives a detailed route map of the next planned stage of the high-speed rail network.
The new railway line, designed to connect London with the West Midlands, will lead past urban areas and places where the impact on the environment is controversial.
In open terrain, the plan will reveal the concept within a distance of 25 meters from the final track.
HS2 is also expected to create three options for a larger, north-by-the-winding high-speed rail network.
Lord Adonis will respond in the spring.
The preferred proposed option is a Y-shaped route in which a single high-speed route would lead to the West Midlands.
The track would divide in or near Birmingham and a turn-off would lead west of the Pennines to Manchester and Scotland and another north-east towards Sheffield, Leedes and Newcastle upon Tyne.
A single line would lead to Scotland.
"It seems to be the best-functioning option," said one railway insider.
Upon completion, travel time between London and Edinburgh would be reduced to 2 hours 40 minutes.
The first section, which would not open before 2025, would allow travellers to travel the London-Birmingham route in 49 minutes, compared to something over an hour today.
The designs offer trains the ability to drive at a speed of 400 km/h, making the British rail network the fastest in Europe.
The first route was planned in such a way that 400-meter-long trains with a maximum capacity of 1,100 persons can run on it.
Up to 18 trains could be in operation per hour on the London-Birmingham route.
This, in turn, would mean that a terminus would be necessary in London where 20,000 travellers per hour could be handled.
Given the spatial restrictions in the capital, an existing station is expected to be upgraded to accommodate the high-speed network.
Railway experts say that only St Pancras International or Euston have this potential.
Lord Adonis will announce whether, in his opinion, this plan should be continued or not, but the lengthy public advisory and planning measures mean that a final decision will not be taken until after the general election.
But even if he puts the wheels in motion, the rail network has to be built in sections.
Trains would first have to switch from the high-speed lines to existing rail lines in the north of Birmingham, which would continue to put the travel time between London and Scotland at over three hours.
This is regarded as a decisive sticking point, so that a change from the aircraft to the railway can be ensured, a benchmark for the rail revolution.
Taxpayers come up for mortgage for 75,000 pound orangery of a Conservative
Alan Duncan, the Conservative's leading lower house member, has charged the taxpayer thousands of pounds a year for the cost of an orangery that he could build in his apartment building in his constituency.
The party's prison spokesman, who was demoted by David Cameron for complaining that lower house members must live "of meager rations," has raised his mortgage by 75,000 pounds to build an oak-wood half-timbered extension to his second-house in Rutland.
He was allowed to charge taxpayer the extra interest income, a sum of hundreds of pounds a month.
Those in charge of the lower house approved the motions at the time and they were also not questioned by Sir Thomas Legg, who is carrying out a review of the sub-house members' expenses. Last night, Duncan said the motions "couldn't have been even cleaner or more understandable."
Duncan, one of the richest lower house members, owns a two-storey house in a village in his Rutland and Melton constituency.
Neighbouring houses have achieved a purchase price of almost one million pounds (1.1 million euros).
The ground floor includes kitchen, living and dining room, but the Fees Office, a spending checkpoint of the House of Commons, has agreed that Duncan needs more space.
Last year, he attached a conservatory called an "orangery" in the plans.
Neighbors described him as a "glass house for entertainment."
Duncan has also not been urged to comment on increased borrowing during a recent investigation into his previous mortgage contracts by the Committee on Standards and Privileges.
The committee acquitted him of breaking the regulations last month after an investigation into his expenses.
In 2001, Duncan transferred the collateral for a mortgage over 271,406 pounds (300,000 euros) from his London house to his constituency house, which he had bought in 1991, a year before his election as a member of parliament.
Documents released this week show Duncan was refunded about 1,400 pounds (1,500 euros) a month as a mortgage rate by March last year.
In April, his claims rose to more than 1,800 pounds (2,000 euros) a month.
Duncan's RBS mortgage interest rate hasn't changed during this period, suggesting that the total increase was used to financing 75,000 pounds (85,000 euros).
The normal stepless RBS mortgage rate fell from 7.94 percent in December 2007 to 4 percent in March this year, where it still stands.
The latest documents released by the lower house show Duncan continued to assert its right to 1,250 pounds (1,400 euros) a month this May.
Duncan lost his post as opposition leader of the House of Commons in September after an undercover reporter filmed him in the process when he said after the spending scandal that parliament members would live "of meager rations."
"In essence, when you work for the state, it means that you have to live by meager rations and be treated like s*****s."
He said: "I've spent my money on my garden and claim a vanishingly small part measured by what belongs.
And I could've demanded all the damn sum, but I don't.'
The MP, a millionaire from his previous career as an oil wholesaler, was attacked for the first time in May because of his expenses when it became clear that he had demanded thousands of pounds for his garden before clarifying with the Fees Office that this "could be seen as an exaggerated demand."
An activist has dug a hole in the shape of a pound sign as a protest in Duncan's lawn, after it became known that he had demanded 4,000 pounds (4,500 euros) over three years.
When questioned last night about the increased mortgage, Duncan said: "This had been approved both by the Fees Office and subsequently by Legg to increase value."
He added: "There is no debate about dodging and meandering or bending the rules or the like.
Everything is completely transparent and moves strictly within the rules, is approved and everything.
It couldn’t be cleaner and clearer.”
Comes all of her believers to admire Brighton's beach hut advent calendar
Beach huts in Brighton have found a new seat of life for the winter months as an interactive advent calendar.
"Beyond," an alternative church group spurring to spiritual discoveries through creativity, has invited 24 owners to decorate their huts for each December day with Christmas carols as theme.
The event kicked off on 1 December and visitors were allowed to visit the latest hut every day from 17:30 to 18:30 with free mulled wine and traditional mince pies, pastries stuffed with dried fruits.
The Christmas carols selected so far so far are "I saw three ships", "O come, o come, Emmanuel" and "The first Noël", where the smelter decoration is the work of Janette Tozer, a local artist.
Martin Poole, 50, a TV marketing assistant from Hove, is an honorary clergyman of the Chichester diocese and head of Beyond.
He said: "We want to make religion more important to the people in a post-Christian society again.
Why, as a church, do we expect people to get into a strange old building? I think the church should go to the people and host thrilling celebrations.
The idea for the beach hut Advent calendar began as a conversation with some friends at dinner.
Brighton is such a fantastically creative and vibrant place and we are trying to express this through our spirituality."
Strong mobilization in Copenhagen and the world for the climate.
Tens of thousands of people demonstrated worldwide and in Copenhagen on Saturday to call for an ambitious and obligatory agreement on the fight against global warming, where the police made several hundred arrests.
At least thirty thousand people, according to police, a hundred thousand according to organisers, have marched in the cold of the Danish capital, on the sidelines of the United Nations climate conference, which was to lead to a conclusion by Friday, which could come into force from 2013.
For Connie Hedegaard, Danish chairman of the climate conference, the increasing mobilization in the world for the climate contributes to what is illustrated by these demonstrations that the "political price" of failure in Copenhagen would be very high.
The incidents in the Danish capital took place a short time after the train passed, when a group of 300 protesters dressed entirely in black had smashed shop windows with paving stones and hammers, a journalist from the AFP noted.
Anti-unresting police officers intervened immediately and without regard.
The police have proclaimed that 600 to 700 people, according to their estimation mainly members "of Blacks Blocs", this extremely violent splinter groups, which have already made themselves famous at the Nato Summit in Strasbourg, were arrested in the east of France, in April.
The Coalition Climate Justice Action (CJA), one of the organizers of the demonstration, has sharply criticized the conditions under which hundreds of militant supporters had been arrested "without distinction", and has underlined that about a hundred of them "were still being held in the streets on Saturday evening," despite the extreme cold with handcuffs and sitting position.
The rather peaceful train has gone from parliament in the direction of the Bella Center, where the negotiations took place.
Demonstrators have stopped about 500 yards from the building without trying to get in.
A stage has been installed to welcome the designated speakers for an evening with candlelight, with the participation of former South African archbishop, Desmond Tutu.
The majority of the demonstrators have arrived with pees and trains from the major European cities, but also many Asians, including some Chinese and Koreans were present, as well as some Africans.
Approximately 3,000 people, most of them with sky-blee rain coats, made a first casserole in the morning in Copenhagen, as you have responded to the call of the friends of the earth to form "blue tides" for "climate justice."
Today we are taking to the streets to demand reparation of ecological guilt in favour of the south, explained Lidy Nacpil, Filipino activist of the Jubilee South Coalition in Copenhagen.
We can't go on like this and tell each other: we have time, said Angelique Kidjo, singer from Benin.
There are rivers in Africa that are drying up, watercourses where you can walk, just as it has never been the case in the past.
For the first time in the history of climatic diplomacy, founded in 1992 with the adoption of the U.N. agreement, the movement of globalization critics has approached environmental organizations.
José Bové, Member of the Merchandism of Globalisation, announced that he came to Copenhagen to link "climate justice and social justice: "Today there is no difference between the fight against global warming and the struggle for another world."
The Asia-Pacific region, where there are many islands that are particularly at risk as a result of warming, has given the go-ahead for the demonstrations.
About 50,000 people have taken to the streets, according to organizers in Australia.
There have been a few hundreds of people in Manila, mostly students who have marched in red and hoisted bandanas where you have praised solar energy.
In Hong-Kong or Djakarta, but also in Canada, marches of several hundred demonstrators took place to demand an energetic approach against climate change.
In France, the demonstrations, organized by the "350" network, have gathered a few hundred people, namely in Paris, in Marseille (south), in Lille (north), in Bordeaux (west) and in Lyon (east).
Some people have gathered in Geneva.
The separatist Abkhazia has elected its president, Georgia denounces that this is a "prank".
Abkhazia elected its president on Saturday, which happened a bit more than a year after Moscow recognised this pro-Russian separatist Georgian territory as self-employed, Tbilisi denounced the "prank."
Approximately 131,000 people were called to participate in the first election after the Russian-Georgian war of August 2008 for control of the South Ossetic area, another Georgian secessionist area recognized by Russia.
Polling stations closed at 5 p.m. GMT.
Voter turnout amounted to approximately 58% a few hours prior to the polling state closure, according to Abkhazian Electoral Commissioner.
The first preliminary results should be announced during the night from Saturday to Sunday.
The five candidates, including the previous president, Serguei Bagapch, categorically reject the idea of reunification with Georgia condemning the election.
These elections are a farce.
For his part, the Georgian president, Mikheil Saakachvili, regrets that Abkhazia is now under Russia's total occupation, his press spokeswoman, Manana Manjgaladze, has explained.
In addition to Russia, only Nicaragua and Venezuela have recognized the self-employment of this area with 216,000 inhabitants, while the rest of the world perceives it as a part of Georgia that is illegally occupied by Russia.
Having separated from Georgia during the 1992-1993 conflict, with several thousand people dying, Abkhazia wants to prove through this election that it is ready to join the international community.
This process is a stage in our new life, a new era, as an independent state, Svetlana Kvartchia, a 54-year-old historian who claims to have voted for Mr Bagapch.
She was one of the electors who went to a polling station, located in a school in the capital Soukhoumi, a beautiful white building with a few palm trees in front of the entrance.
The issue of Russian dominance is the heart of this presidential election, in a region with beautiful landscapes on the Black Sea.
Large signs on which Bagapch can be seen alongside the Russian president and the head of government, Dmitri Medvedev and Vladimir Putin, are everywhere in this small area that has 216,000 inhabitants.
In Gali, which is located in the east of the country, the district chief, Beslan Archba, has prepared a warm-hearted welcome to the Russian representatives who have come to supervise the election.
We, the Abkhazian people, are grateful, he said at a small celebration, as a reference to the support of Moscow, in particular with regard to the decision to recognize Abkhazia’s self-employmeness.
I hope that your country will soon be recognized by the United Nations, replied Alexei Ostrovski, a member of the Russian parliament and raised a glass of vodka in the process.
The opposition, for its parage, represented by the former vice-president and two Russian businessmen, Zaous Ardzinba and Beslan Boutba, has criticised the agreements reached with Moscow the previous year, as the one in which Abkhazia leaves Russia to control the railway for 10 years.
The fifth candidate, Vitali Banba, claims not to support either the current government or the opposition.
A majority of 50% plus one vote is necessary to win the election.
If none of the candidates reaches this limit, a second round of voting must be organized within two weeks.
Climate: Demos Worldwide, Incidenties in Copenhagen
Tens of thousands of people demonstrated all over the world on Saturday to demand an ambitious and binding contract from the Copenhagen climate conference, where the police, ready for action at any time, arrested about 700 people on the sidelines of an important demonstration.
Thirty thousand people, according to the police, according to the organizers, have demonstrated in the afternoon cold in the Danish capital, which until December 18 welcomes delegates from 193 countries looking for an agreement to enter into force on 1 January 2013.
From parliament, the peaceful train marched in the direction of the Bella Center, where the negotiations are taking place.
Protesters have stopped about 500 yards from the centre and have not tried to invade.
A stage has been set up to welcome the designated speakers for an evening with candlelight, with the participation of former South African archbishop, Desmond Tutu.
A few moments after the train had passed, a group of protesters dressed entirely in black and armed with bricks and hammers has begun smashining discs, an AFP journalist noted.
Anti-unrestorie police officers immediately encircled you and intervened without regard by throwing some of them on the ground.
The police have announced that 600 to 700 people have been arrested and have made it clear that they are mainly "members of "the Blacks Blocs", these extremely violent splinter groups, which have already made themselves famous at the Nato Summit in Strasbourg, in the east of France, in April.
Towards the evening, a police officer suffered an injury from a paving stone during the incidents, according to police, in which, according to police, about twenty people were provisionally arrested. Four vehicles burned near an occupied house.
"We have the extreme groups under control," police spokesman Henrik Jakobsen explained, as the helicopters circled in the air.
Most demonstrators who arrived by train or with their own vehicle from major German cities, from London, Amsterdam or Milan were Europeans.
However, there were also many Asians, including Chinese and Koreans, and Africans.
Nearly 3,000 people, mostly in the sky-blue raincoat, formed in the morning in Copenhagen after the call of the "Friends of the Earth" to a first group, which should represent the "blue seas" for "climate justice".
"Today we are taking to the streets to demand that the ecological debt be repaid to the people of the South," activist Lidy Nacpil told Jubilee South Coalition of the Philippines in Copenhagen.
"We can't tell each other anymore : We still have time," says singer Angélique Kidjo from Benin.
"In Africa there are rivers drying up, there are watercourses through which you can walk, which was never the case before."
For the first time in the history of climate diplomacy launched in 1992 with the adoption of the U.N. Convention, the anti-globalization movement has approached environmental organizations.
The French MEP of the European Parliament and globalisation critic José Bové, told AFP that he had come to Copenhagen "to combine climate justice and social justice." "There is no difference today between the fight against global warming and the fight for another world."
Oxfam has mobilized several personalities, such as Danish model Helena Christensen or former U.N. High Commissioner for Human Rights Mary Robinson, who are reaching out to the masses.
The Asia-Pacific region, which is home to many islands particularly vulnerable to global warming, has given impetus to the demonstrations.
According to the organizers, about 50,000 people took to the streets in Australia.
In Manila, a few hundred people, the majority of students dressed in red with bandanas, in front of the city administration of the Philippine capital have advertised solar energy.
Hundreds of demonstrators have also gathered in Hong Kong and Djakarta in an action against climate change.
In France, several hundred people joined together during the demonstrations organized by the "Network of 350", especially in Paris, Marseille in the south, Lille in the north, Bordeaux in the west and in Lyon in the east.
Several people gathered in Geneva.
According to Chatel, the subjects of geography and history emerge stronger from the school reform of the Lycées.
In an interview, the National Minister of Education Luc Chatel explained in an interview, which appeared in the Sunday newspaper "Journal du dimanche" that the subjects of geography and history emerge stronger from the school reform of the lycées, even if they are not taught compulsory in the terminal (12th school year).
"The pupils of Première (11th school year) now have all four hours per week and the same programme," confirms Mr Chatel.
For the minister, "this hypocrisy must be overcome," because according to him "the scientific profile is no longer a scientific profile," but, by and large, a concentration of "the best students."
"The economic-social and the literary profile (ES and L) must not become a sub-profile of the scientific," he continued. He underlined: "I have not yet heard many historians and intellectuals who would have been outraged by the lack of historical-geographical education in the terminals on technical lyceas."
On Saturday, an IFOP survey published in the Humanité revealed that almost seven out of ten French (69%) criticize the state project of the deletion of obligatory teaching in history and geography in Terminal S (12th school year with a scientific profile). Many teachers and intellectuals also criticized this project, as well as the left and some representatives of the right.
The minister, on the other hand, replied that there was no question with regard to restricting the philosophy of Terminal S programme.
Luc Chatel also defended the education reform for teachers, while 16 organizations (students and trade unions) had called for Tuesday to protest against the project, which in their interest falls victim to the educational training of future teachers.
The Minister stated that in Master 2 (M2) at the beginning of the year, they undergo a subject-specific aptitude test and, at the end of the year, an admissions examination in which their teaching skills are examined.
In addition, they carry out internships in the M2 during the year and then teach one year before the students as teachers in the internship.
He continued: “If you add all the internships offered before the performance exam, you’ll reach even more hours you’ve spent in front of the students, i.e. more than 300 hours.”
The minister finally stated that he would like to offer "the individual right to education" - a "thing that doesn't exist in popular education."
He confirmed again that he would like to abolish the school card "until 2012".
15 minutes for the climate at "Ecole Normale Supérieure"
Fifteen minutes to talk about the deployment at the Copenhagen summit : this is the challenge Jean Jouzel, vice-president of the "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change," had to overcome on Saturday morning at the Ecole Normale Supérieure at Rue d'Ulm in Paris.
As a journalist of the AFP noted, the Nobel Peace Prize winner Jean Jouzel opened the first of the "Ernest" conferences, at which experts turn to the audience fifteen minutes with filmed contributions.
You want to do something with which you reach the people and awaken in them the feeling of wanting to learn more, Edouard Hannezo, 22 years old and one of the organizers of that day, explained on the sidelines of the conference. The designation of this event goes back to the name that the students gave to the fishes in the basin of the school.
"Among the experts, around twenty panelists were selected, even according to their charisma," he clarifies.
"For climate warming, you can talk for hours, but the core goals can also be outlined in a few minutes," Jean Jouzel told AFP, who spoke in front of about 50 people only with the help of slides.
"This is part of our role as a scientist," he underlined in the hope that listeners may draw from the fact that "the Copenhagen conference relies on an absolutely serious scientific dossier."
The videos made by these conferences will be available online on the school's website from December 15.
The next "Ernest" day is scheduled for February and will take place every two months afterwards.
Default of payment : angry prison guards, actions planned
The unions of correctional officers on Friday called for a general day of action to ensure that the premiums and overtime, whose pay was deferred for budget reasons until January, will be transferred before the Christmas holidays.
The 2nd union of correctional officers ("FO-Pénitentiaire"), urged the employees who have no serve to demonstrate in front of the correctional facility on Tuesday morning and block them to draw attention to the "theft of what they are entitled to".
The 3rd Union of Pennalists ("CGT-Pénitentiaire") calls for "unification" for a strong mobilization in the various prisons on which the non-paying of the overtime, night surcharges incurred in October, as well as Sunday and holiday surcharges in December is marketed as "disgraceful and unacceptable."
The first union of penitentiary officers, the Autonomous Federal Detention Union (UFAP/UNSA), called for "general mobilizations (...) to show dissatisfaction and angery."
The management of the penitentiary and the Ministry of Justice stated that overtime increased sharply in 2009 and not all were envisaged in the budget. However, they guarantee that the remaining claims will be paid in January.
As the Ministry of Justice announced in the evening, Michèle Alliot-Marie had submitted a letter to the unions of the penality officers, in which it is guaranteed that the payment will be made next month.
"I am well aware of the efforts of the staff to keep our prison system functioning and modernising," the justice minister said in this letter.
"I have instructed the management of the prison to take immediate action so that such a situation, which has arisen due to a lack of assessment of overtime, will not be repeated in the future," Ms Alliot-Marie added.
The December wage is therefore meager, except for the management staff, who benefit from high service and target premiums, explains the UFAP/UNSA, for which the "household constraints" have to bear.
"This is an absolute scandal," outrages the interregional union FO in Marseille, announcing plans to boycott "all gatherings and convocations" up to new instructions.
"The staff does not go to work on Sundays and public holidays, if then the hours from October in December are still not paid. This also applies to their overtime hours," threatens FO Rhône-Alpes-Auvergne as well.
"At a time when more and more tasks are being imposed on us without hiring more staff, trying to enrich themselves with the funds of the staff is more than inappropriate," according to Bordeaux's CGT union.
According to the trade unions, the situation is not the same in all regions. Some are less affected than others.
In any case, however, it is wrong, according to Jean-François Forget, the secretary-general of UFAP.
Obama thinks he's always handing over better gifts to Christmas than he gets himself.
The American president, Barack Obama, who will spend Christmas for the first time with his family in the White House, has lashed out at TV talkmaster Oprah Winfrey that he is always giving better gifts than he himself gets.
On a special broadcast that aired Sunday evening via the channel ABC, the president and his wife Michelle gave insight into how they will spend the holidays in their new illustrious home.
Even Bo, the family's little four-legged companion, will be part of the game.
"Santa Claus also loves Bo," affirms Michelle Obama, adding that the dog, of course, gets a gift, too.
Michelle Obama firmly rejected her husband's claims on the quality of the gifts. "I've given you beautiful gifts over the past year," she neglects the president.
"So please," he summoned her mischievously, pointing to the first-lady's pearl necklace: "And who gave it to you?"
During this hour-long broadcast in a casual atmosphere with the well-known TV presenter Barack Obama so vehemently supported during his election campaign, the president and his wife also talked about gifts they had loved most as a child.
Barack Obama recalled a bicycle with ten gears and also a basketball that his father, whom he very rarely saw, had brought him from Kenya for Christmas.
"I remember when my father visited us once Christmas and brought me a basketball," the president said.
"It wasn't until much later in my life that I understood I had that ball from him."
The first lady, in turn, remembered her puppet house.
“I didn’t even know how to arrange the furniture in a house, so I put them all on the wall instead of grouping them around the fireplace. But I loved this little doll house,” Michelle Obama recounted.
Johnny Hallyday operates again, RTL confirms that he has been put into an artificial coma
Johnny Hallyday again underwent surgery in Los Angeles for significant "damages" following surgery in France. According to the people surrounding him, the situation is "under control," even though RTL confirms that the singer has been put into an artificial coma to avoid pain and complications.
The 66-year-old rock musician underwent re-operated on the night from Wednesday to Thursday at Cedars-Sinaï hospital in Los Angeles after American doctors found "damage as a result of the surgery of a herniated disc" that he had suffered in Paris on November 26, according to his production firm in Paris, which added that these damages would have necessitated re-operation.
Nevertheless, the singer's production company wanted to clarify that the situation was "under control" and announced that a new medical opinion would be available no later than 48 hours.
According to RTL, the partner during the singer's last tour, however, Johnny Hallyday has been put into an artificial coma to avoid "pain and complications."
However, it wasn't until after 48 hours that there was a little more certainty about the patient's condition when he opened his eyes, RTL added.
At the request of AFP, there was neither confirmation nor comment from the production company.
RTL also convinces that "from certain sources emerges" that after his first operation in Paris, "nothing drainage" was laid.
The rocker had been operated on in the international clinic of Parc Monceau because of a herniated disc.
The procedure planned for several days was carried out by neurosurgeon Stéphane Delajoux, a well-known doctor who had already treated many stars.
This was not available on Thursday evening for comments to RTL.
Following Johnny Hallyday's surgery, the singer was admitted Monday at Cedars-Sinaï hospital in Los Angeles due to a consequential infection following the procedure.
Singer Eddy Mitchell, who is very close to Johnny Hallyday, told RTL on Thursday that his friend was going through a "deep valley" right now. However, he assured that you don't need "worry" about the rock musician, as he was "a good fighter."
"He'd called me to Los Angeles even before his departure. I told him it's nonsense to put himself on a plane for twelve hours after such an operation, that doesn't work like that," Eddy Mitchell said.
On Wednesday evening, the singer's producer, Jean-Claude Camus, confirmed that Johnny Hallyday "responds well to the antibiotics" and that "the infection was contained."
Mr Camus also declared to have spoken to Laeticia, the singer's wife, and he felt no particular concern in the process.
Nevertheless, the concern for the singer's health had become ever greater since he spent nine days at a hospital in July after he crashed on his yacht.
Two months later, to the general amazement, there was talk that Johnny Hallyday had a "small colorectal cancer surgery," which led to a mild infection.
At the end of September, the singer recorded his long "Tour 66" as a farewell tour.
Since then, however, every health problem has been commented extensively. Fans and journalists have not escaped the fact that the singer on stage struggled to move.
Only in mid-November, the people surrounding him had to deny a new admission to the hospital.
Euro-2009/Short track: First place for Bousquet over his favorite distance
Frenchman Frédérick Bousquet, three-time medal winner at the 2009 swimming world championships won an international title on Thursday over his favorite distance, 50m freestyle, at the Euro-2009 on the short track in Istanbul.
On the first day of this competition, when for the last time the polyurethane suits were allowed, there were only three world records, while one had expected a real record flood.
A record has been improved by Hungarian Evelyn Verraszto over 4 x 200 m layers and the other two by Russia at 4 x 50m layers of the men.
It's a shame for Bousquet, who wanted to reach for the world record at the 50m freestyle, the record of which he holds in the big basin (20.94).
At one of the two royal distances in swimming, 50m freestyle, the 28-year-old Frenchman asserted himself in 20.53 sek, in a finale with ten swimmers at the start (usually it's eight) - a premiere at international level.
Bousquet defeated Croat Duje Draganja (20.70) and Russian Sergey Fesikov (20.84), but failed to break the world record (20.30) as planned.
"You can believe in always bigger things and in this case I've said that if the world record is in my reach, I'll try."
Like Bousquet, almost all swimmers launched in polyurethane suits, which are banned from 1 January 2010.
With 238 world records since February 2008, these combinations end their epos in Istanbul, which began for the first time in the same competition in 1999.
The use of the combination this time for the German Paul Biedermann, two-time world champion in the cymbal this summer in Rome and world recorder, did not pay off in the end.
The young swimmer failed to improve his best time over 400m freestyle (3:32.77), yet he prevailed with the second-bestest time in his career (3:34.55), ahead of Russian Nikita Lobintsev (3:35.75) and Dane Mads Glaesner (3:36.82).
"I didn't give everything this time.
I knew I couldn't achieve a world record today, but I've defended my title and done my task.'
"Lobintsev will be a serious opponent in the future," Biedermann explained.
U.S : Grave slab at Zola estimated at US$1.5 million
A Roman tomb slab, which appeared with the French writer Emile Zola, was estimated at 1.5 million US dollars over its value on Thursday, according to the art dealer Sotheby's.
This slab is two meters long and 63 cm high. It dates back to the IIIst century AD and has always been valued at 150,000 to 250,000 dollars.
The marble, on which "four scenes of the Dionysus are depicted in a sophistic architectural decor, surrounded by satyrs and bacchus figures, is a rare piece. There are only four or five of them in the world," Florent Heintz, vice president for the austagion of Roman and Egyptian antiquities at Sotheby's, told AFP on Tuesday.
It was sold under hundreds of pieces.
"Sales are still ongoing, but this part is certainly what the buyers have offered most of what buyers so far so far," Sotheby's spokesman Dan Abernethy told AFP.
The discovery of this purchase of the marble part by Emile Zola goes back a long way.
The record was at home in Rome for almost 300 years in the Borghese family's famous collection, after which she dived piece by bit with French actress Cécile Sorel, who was used for it in her bathroom of her Paris hotel and with Paul Reynaud, minister in the Third French Republic and president of the Council before Marshal Pétain came to power.
The next fifty years have been in the dark.
While searching the Louvre's database, Florent Heintz discovered that this record was part of a "heir of Emile Zola," which opened in 1903, a year after the death of the author of the "Thérèse Raquin" and the fresco "Rougon-Macquart."
Zelaya's exit attempt from Honduras towards Mexico has been foiled
On Thursday, Mexico had declared the conditions for the transfer of ousted Honduran President Manuel Zelaya on home soil to be incompatible, after the latter had rejected passage by the coup government as unacceptable.
After a telephone conversation with President Zelaya that took place during the night, everything seems to indicate at the moment that there is disagreement on the possibilities of carrying out this transfer, explained the Mexican Minister for Foreign Affairs Patricia Espinosa.
According to the immigration authorities, the aircraft provided by the Mexican government, which had arrived in Salvador on Wednesday for the purpose of M. Zelayas, had returned to its starting point.
The coup government wanted to force M. Zelaya to "abdication" and ask him abroad for political asylum under escort.
The ousted president, however, declined because he wished to return to Mexico as a welcoming incumbent head of state.
The coup regime "has suffered a renewed failure by wanting to persuade me to give up at my expense (...), they wanted me to abdicate," M. Zelaya told Radio Globo on Thursday, in which he has frequently spoken out since the beginning of the political crisis, which lasts five months in the Central American country.
I can stay here for ten years, here I have my guitar, the ousted president, who had escaped two and a half months ago into the Brazilian embassy of Tegucigalpa, added under attacks of some chords.
The Mexican embassy has presented us with a request for guidance, which we unfortunately cannot accept, since it does not include a request for asylum, Oscar Raul Matute, interior minister of the coup government, told the Honduran radio HRN on Wednesday.
I'm not requesting asylum in any country at all, Manuel Zelaya had responded in an interview with Channel Telesur, based in Caracas.
A possible departure from Honduras, he had underlined, he would only undertake as president of the Honduran people.
The rumours of an exit M. Zelayas had mobilized on Wednesday the supporters of the ousted president, who had opened up to the Brazilian embassy and gathered around them, whereupon the local military had stepped up its deployment.
Brazilian Foreign Minister Celso Amorim criticized the coup government's position.
According to his assessment, this reveals the marginal nature of this government in the face of international norms.
Zelaya, who had approached Socialist Venezuelan President Hugo Chavez, was overthrown on June 28, on the day of the people's interrogation organised by him in preparation for his re-election, contrary to the advice of the Supreme Court, the army and Congress.
He had unexpectedly returned from his exile in Costa Rica on September 21 and had escaped to the Brazilian embassy.
If he leaves these, he is imprisoned for treason, of which he is accused by the judiciary, according to the coup government.
Coup president Roberto Micheletti has succeeded in organising a presidential election on November 29 to replace M. Zelaya.
Porfirio Lobo had been recognized by the United States in the course of the controversial election, but was rejected by Latin American countries such as Venezuela, Argeninia or Brazil.
Three days later, the Congress had voted massively against M. Zelaya's return to office until the expiration of his mandate on January 27.
Wall Street upswing, U.S. trade balance fuels optimism
The New York Stock Exchange closed on Thursday with a high, the figures of the American trade balance testify to a price recovery of the shares of the United States as well as its partners: the Dow Jones increased by 0.67%, the Nasdaq by 0.33%.
According to the figures at stock market close, the Dow Jones Industrial Average has risen from 68.78 points to 10,405.83 points, the Nasdaq in turn, which is dominated by technology, has risen from 7.13 points to 2,190.06 points.
The extended index Standard & Poor ́s 500 was written at 0.58% (6.40 points) to 1,102.35 points.
There is good news from various economic sectors, said Lindsey Piegza of FTN Financial.
This, however, remains anecdotal under the premise that the market focuses only on "half-full glasses," she moderated.
According to Avalon Partners' Peter Cardillo, investors are mainly fixated on the figures from America's trade balance, "which shows an improvement in activity in the fourth quarter."
The U.S. trade deficit felt by 7.6% to 32.9 billion dollars in October compared to September.
The analysts, on the other hand, had expected a deficit increase.
Both imports and exports have increased.
The big news is the increase in the total volume of trade, especially in freight exports, which reflects the economic growth of the main trading partners of the United States, commented Christopher Cornell of the enterprise research Economy.com.
In return, unemployment reports in the United States have risen against all expectations in the last week and have now reached 474,000 inquiries.
However, the average of new job seekers during the last four weeks "fell to the low point of the year," emphasized Scott Marcouiller of Wells Fargo Advisors.
The obligation market has fallen.
Profitability of government bonds increased to 10 years to 3,482 % compared to 3,432 % on Tuesday evening, the profitability of bonds increased to 30 years to 4.492 % compared to the 4,408 % from the previous evening.
Cuba: Hundreds of partisans in power whistle "Ladies in White"
A few hundred partisans standing by Cuban power whistled the wives of political detainees gathered in Havana for the purpose of World Human Rights Day on Thursday and also thwarted another rally, due to which a good dozen dissidents were temporarily arrested.
Around forty "ladies in white", wives or related political prisoners, were whistled on the open street by partisans of the government under Raul Castro, just as they found themselves on the footmasket in the center of the capital to demand the liberation of the political detainees, confirmed a journalist of the AFP.
Down with the Yankies!, "Mercenaries!", "the street belongs to the revolutionaries!", "Living Fidel and Raul!" the empowered partisans chanted the dissidents with a raised fist.
The latter were escorted by civil agents who had appeared to "ensure their protection."
A similar demonstration against the "Ladies in Weiss" had taken place the night before in Havana, in which there were neither injuries nor arrests.
In Havann's Vedado district, on the other hand, a dozen dissidents, who also set offf for the demonstration taking place on Thursday, were temporarily detained by plainclothes police officers, an AFP reporter confirmed.
According to this, the policemen, according to this journalists, the dissidents booed by the partisans of the government forced to enter civilian vehicles.
The temporary detention of dissidents under such circumstances is usually limited only to a few hours.
As contenders for the Sakharov Prize awarded by the European Parliament in 2005, the "Ladies in Weiss" have organized an unauthorised march for the purpose of "please for the release of all political prisoners," the number of which, according to the dissident's list, has around 200 people, Laura Pollan, one of the leaders whose husband Hector Maceda has a prison sentence of 20 years.
M. Maceda belongs to the group of 75 Cuban dissidents imprisoned in March 2003, of whom 53 have been behind bars to date.
The Cuban authorities perceive the regime opponents as "mercenaries" or "agents" in the service of the declared arch-enemy, the United States.
A rarity in itself is that Cuban television reported on the demonstration events taking place on Thursday between militant pro-government fighters and "countra-revolutionaries."
Cuba, led by a unity party, the Communist Party, is regularly blamed by European or American NGOs for denigrating rights and freedoms, in particular freedom of expression.
Insane kills eighty-year-old with the knife and leaves 5 injured in Clichy-la-Garenne
An insane man armed with two knives killed an eighty-year-old and injured five other people on Friday against late afternoon at full-time in downtown Clichy-la-Garenne (Hauts-de-Seine).
According to the initial information of the interrogation, the 28-year-old attacker had injured the eighty-year-old from previously unexplained cause in or in front of a pharmacy on Jean-Jaures Boulevard with two knives and this, according to police, in the presence of witnesses.
The old man, born in 1929, according to a police syndicate Alliance, has succumbed to the consequences of his injuries at the hospital.
In or in front of the same pharmacy, he pre-buttoned a couple with a pregnant woman.
The man, roughly in the Thirties, intervened and was injured.
The first two victims were hospitalized, where the eighty-year-old died.
The other injured person is in danger of his life, because according to police sources, apparently vital organs were affected.
The pregnant woman has become nauseous as a result of the events and she is said to have been hospitalized in a "shock state" according to a police source.
The armed man, who, according to polices, appeared to be under the influence of alcohol or drugs, is said to have immediately escaped to the street, the same boulard Jean Jaures.
He would have injured two other passers-by, apparently by accident: a thirty-year-old and a forty-year-old, the latter of both severely.
The scenario remained in the dark in the evening.
He had been armed with two butcher's knives, which he carried crossed behind his back, explained on the spot Arnaud Pressé, a person in charge of the syndicate alliance of the Hauts-de-Seine area.
Several passers-by, including teenagers, are said to have tried to tame the mad, according to witness reports ascertained by AFP.
I've seened a guy in the thirties with a green vest.
Three teenagers tried to intervene.
They didn't make it because the guy was well-built, Alberto, a waiter of an obvious bar, reported.
A horde fell over him, he added, including a city police officer who broke his wrist in the process.
Still on Boulevard Jean-Jaures, at number 61, two city police officers, including their supervisor, tried to arrest the armed man and were injured with stabbings, according to polices.
The action has taken place in one of Clichy-la-Garenne's main arteries, where a number of shops are located.
Passers-by have approached me to tell me that there is an injured person outside.
I've gone out and seen a 70-year-old person lying on the ground, I put them in first aid position and noticed a gaping wound in the stomach area, Frédéric Allione, a 35-year-old pharmacist, told AFP.
Five minutes later, a second, about thirty-year-old person came, injured by lacerations.
Forces of the state police have finally arrested the man, who carried no identification documents with him.
Nanterre's public prosecutor's office has tasked the district department of the Hauts-de Seine criminal police (SDPJ 92) with clearing the case.
The insane was hospitalized on Thursday evening.
In view of his condition, he has not yet been questioned in court.
One police source doesn't rule out that he's being taken to a mental hospital.
According to another police source, it required six police officers to tame him at the police commissioner's office in Clichy, where he had been taken away.
He found himself "in a state of overexcitation" such as "all mentally confused people of this kind, in whom the forces release themselves during the course of the crime."
U.S.: Polanski's lawyer again calls for cessation of
The lawyer of the French-Polish filmmaker Roman Polanski once again demanded on Thursday in Los Angeles (California, Western United States) to drop the prosecution against his client, who is currently on trial at his place of residence in Switzerland due to a mortal violation of more than 30 years ago.
Chad Hummel, a lawyer for the "pianist's director," denounced gross procedural errors before three judges of the Court of Appeal in the second district of California at the time of the trial in 1977.
Immediately, I demand sentence, explained M. Hummel, before he added that the conduct of Judge Laurence Rittenband, who was in charge at the timing of the trial of the case, "let it run down your back cold."
According to him, the - meanwhile defuncted - judge Rittenband, before he fell his judge's verdict, had discussed with a public prosecutor, who is said to have told him that Roman Polanski deserved prison - which was a serious procedural error.
This discussion had been thrown up in the documentary "Roman Polanski: wanted and desired", especially by the invoking prosecutor David Wells.
At the end of September, M. Wells had declared to have "lied" to the authors of the documentary.
Should the Court refuse to suspend the criminal proceedings, Mr Hummel would require the conduct of a trial that would enable him to submit the evidence for his reasoning.
The prosecution, for its part, has repeated the evidence already explained in the first instance, i.e. that a discussion on the annulment of the facts charged to the defendant cannot be seriously considered as long as Roman Polanski does not appear in court.
The 76-year-old director had fled the U.S. in 1978, even before proclamating the sentence for "illegal sexual relations" with a minor of 13 years.
He hasn't traveled to America since that time.
Los Angeles County deputy prosecutor Phyllis Asayama had stated that Roman Polanski "must be there to attend the trial."
If one wants to spread the message in this matter, not only to (Roman Polanski) but also to other defendants, the escape is a way out of the way out?, asks Ms Asayma.
In their opinion, "the filmmaker asks the court for a favor and thus despises his authority."
One of the three judges – who now have 90 days to announce their decision – even emphasized “that there are other escape alternatives.”
Hummel wasn't the only one who had requested the cessation of prosecution against Roman Polanski.
Samantha Geimer's lawyer, the victim, once again demanded the abolition of criminal proceedings.
She recalled that her client would no longer bear to be harassed by any resurgence of Affaire Polanski, adding: "None in this room can say that this procedure was equitable.
Thirty-two years is enough.”
Roman Polanski was arrested on September 26 in Switzerland at the instigation of an American arrest warrant.
After spending two months behind bars, he was then convicted in anticipation of possible extradition to house arrest in his chalet in Gstaad.
Wall Street is slowed by the dollar's rise and concludes without price.
The New York Stock Exchange closed without price on Friday in a market that was torn between better indicators than in the U.S. and an increase in the dollar: The Dow Jones gained 0.63%, while the Nasdaq lost 0.03%.
Based on the definitive figures available at closing, the Dow Jones Industrial Average has risen by 65.67 points to 10,471.50 points, while the technologically dominated Nasdaq has surrendered 0.55 points to 2,190.31 points.
The extended index Standard & Poor's 500 has risen 0.37% (4.06 points) to 1,106.41 points.
"Honestly, the market is pretty quiet," Mace Blicksilver of Marblehead Asset Management noted.
"It remains tightly limited, the trading volume has become very small and I think this will remain so until the end of the year."
The trend was supported by the publication of better economic indicators than envisaged, which "helped improve the market view to the soundness of the economy's resurgency," the analysts from Charles Schwab summarized.
In the U.S
In addition, American consumer confidence improved markedly in December to 73.4 versus 67.4 the previous month, according to index preview by the University of Michigan.
However, the indexes were curbed by a new rise in the dollar exchange rate, which has reached the highest level against the euro for two months, pushing 'the sectors vulnerable to the economic situation down,' explained Owen Fitzpatrick, Deutsche Bank.
As a result, technological values have lost some ground.
The obligation market has declined.
The yield on the 10-year Treasury bond rose by 3.540% on Wednesday evening, up from 3.482%, and that of the 30-year bond to 4.497% compared to 4.492% of the previous day.
Miss Worlding in South Africa again
After a month's round trip through South Africa, the 112 contestants will cross the final hurdle to the title of Miss World on Saturday in order to realize their dream: to become the most beautiful woman in the world, in front of a billion TV viewers.
During this large beauty fair, which is held for the second time in a roward around Johannesburg, five of them have already grabbed their ticket to the semi-finals.
Miss Japan Eruza Sasaki, Perla Beltran Acosta from Mexico, Mariatu Kargbo from Sierra Leone, Kaiane Aldorino from Gilbratar and Yanbing Ma from Canada were crowned Miss Sportive or Miss Topmodel during their African stay.
This was a true swirl of pleasures.
Nice towns, such as Port Elizabeth, Durban and Cape Hoorn, was simply extraordinary.
I've seen a lot of things and had a lot of experiences!" said Miss Sierra Leone with full enthusiasm.
This competition will take place for the ninth time in South Africa and will be broadcast live on Saturday, from 5 p.m
I couldn't think of a nicer country to hand over my crown.
This experience was simply fascinating.
I'm happy to have had that experience twice, underlined Miss World Ksenia Sukhinova in the heat of the South African summer.
For the young Russian, her second stay in South Africa was characterized by numerous exchanges of experiences with candidates and the rediscovery of "stunning landscapes".
After a month of galas, rehearsals, encounters with the press and safari, the Miss are now focusing on the competition.
Indian Pooja Chopra hopes to follow in the footsteps of Priyanka Chopra, who was elected in 2000 and will also be present at this election, as well as emulating Aishwarya Rai, who was elected in 1994 and both became Bollywoodstars.
It is, of course, every woman's dream to be crowned once as the most beautiful woman in the world, but this isn't just about appearance.
We are ambassadors of our country and I want to come home with the crown, interspecting Miss India with a big smile.
According to the organizers, this 59th edition is underlined with music and dance.
The contestants defile in traditional costumes and not to forget, in a swimsuit.
The performance is presented by a television presenter, Chinese Angela Chow, the ex-beauty queen from South Africa, Michelle McLean, and by a leader of the Miss World committee, Steve Douglas.
South Africa has shown that it knows how to receive the world witness and prideness, assures Julia Morley, chairwoman of Miss World.
The contestants, like last year, made a safari, visited the famous township in Soweto, but this time tasted by bike and local cuisine.
Quite extraordinary, however, was the meeting with South African President Jaboc Zuma, who gave them his hand.
Not common for a head of state, noted Miss Sierra Leone.
Their most beautiful memory, however, undoubtedly remains the draw in Cape Town (south-west) for the World Cup in June 2010, a kind of the country to advertise for this event a little more.
I'm upset with people I've probably never approached otherwise.
I'm sure I'll come back to the 2010 World Cup, enthusiastically promising Miss Italy, Alice Taticchi.
The news of Johnny Hallyday is "very positive," according to his son David
News to Johnny Hallyday is "very positive," his son David insured Friday upon his arrival at Los Angeles airport, where he visited his father, who has been in hospital there for several days.
The news is very positive.
He is very strong, it's all going very well, David Hallyday explained to the press on the exit from the plane, in which he arrived shortly after 3 p.m. local time (21 p.m. CET) from Paris.
The singer's son, who made a relaxed impression, assured that he would "quickly" go to Cedars-Sinaï hospital, into which Johnny Hallyday was admitted Monday because of a bacterial follow-up infection following surgery on a herniated disc on November 26 in Paris.
The singer was "put into an artificial coma this night in Los Angeles to ease his severe pain and carry out his treatment, the press service announced.
Johnny Hallyday has been put into an artificial coma by the doctors of the Cedars-Sinaï hospital in Los Angeles, into which he was admitted on Monday because of a bacterial consequential infection after a herniated disc surgery on November 26 in Paris.
The singer had to undergo surgery again in the night from Wednesday to Thursday due to the morbid changes caused by the last intervention.
His wife Laeticia took part in a school eventing event at France's Los Angeles high school on Friday morning where her daughter Jade goes to school, an AFP photographer noted.
The head of orthopaedic and traumatological surgery at Hôpital Pitié-Salpêtrière, Yves Catoné, announced that he would visit the singer at hospital in Los Angeles on Monday.
The producer severely burdened neurosurgeon Dr. Stéphane Delajoux, who operated the singer on November 26 at Clinique internationale du parc Monceau in Paris.
We were told that he had wreaked the purest massacre.
Los Angeles surgeons said they were completely indignant, Mr Camus explained.
I've heared that you're potentially going to initiate legal proceedings," he said.
Jean-Claude Camus is of the opinion that Johnny Hallyday "traveled far too fast and this with the permission of the Parisian surgeon to Los Angeles."
I spoke personally with this doctor, who assured me that he sees this trip as completely unproblematic, which had surprised me very much, since I myself already have two herniated disc surgeries behind me, he added.
Shortly before, he announced on RTL that the singer had apparently left the hospital "without drainage, without everything" after the operation.
Dr Stéphane Delajoux is very well-known in the Künstlermilieu.
He had the actress Marie Trintignant , operated a few days before her death in Vilnius, at a clinic in Neuilly-sur-Seine where he practiced at that time.
In 2007, he also operated on actress Charlotte Gainsbourg to reabsorb a hematoma after a brain haemorrhage.
However, he is also a doctor with a dubious reputation who has already been convicted several times due to unsuccessful operations and not to forget about tax deprivation and fraud.
Me Olivier Metzner, lawyer of the Medical Association, has described him as "a man without scruples" who "regularily" has to answer before the chamber board and is "anything but recommended".
After a few days of silence, Dr Stéphane Delajoux issued a statement Friday through his lawyer, Me David Koubbi.
He explained that the intervention was "perfect" and the post-operational examinations would have been "normal."
The operation "did not evoke any bleeding and therefore requires no drainage," he added.
In any case, the tour of the singer, whose health problems have accumulated since last summer, seems to be questioned.
It's clearly obvious that it's a little too close for a continuation of the tour on January 8 (in Amiens), Mr Camus explained.
The party alliance UDF (Union for French democracy) becomes energetic and calls on the Nouveau Centre party to no longer use its seal
The party alliance, overseen by François Bayrou’s MoDem party, reacted violently on Friday to a takeover attempt of the seal, the party once founded by Valéry Gismard d’Estaing, on the side of the chairman of the Nouveau Centre party, Hervé Morin, and urged him under the threat of a judicial prosecution to no longer use that seal on any document.
Hervé Morin publicly announces his intention to adopt this UDF seal, as he felt that François Bayrou would not be worthy of defending this political legacy after reaching out to the left.
By the way, the Minister of Defense, whose party partner is the UMP, wanted to reaffirm this strategy on Saturday in front of the National Council of the NC.
To emphasise his efforts, he had received the support of Mr Hervé de Charette (formerly UMP, now at NC), who claims the ownership of UDF, since he was the first to apply for this trade mark at the "Institut national de la propriété industrielle" in 2004.
However, the office of François Bayrous's Friends-aligned UDF broke the silence and made it clear that they could not be expropriated and set a deadline for the minister as well as the deputie under the threat of a judicial prosecution to no longer use this seal and to transfer the property rights of the trademark to it.
In recent days, you have made numerous declarations that you would propose your political movement to take over the UDF-Siegelzu+, wrote the party alliance in a letter signed by 19 of the 24 office members, including François Bayrou and Marielle de Sarnez, the MoDem party, as well as Senator Jean Arthuis of the centrist alliance.
You probably also believed that you had the right to print the name of this movement +Nouveau center+, the slogan + of today’s UDF +, on their political material as well as on the home page of your website.
As you know, you are not entitled to the use of this seal, the UDF office stated, prompting the party NC to "immediatelyly remove the mention of the name UDF from all material as well as from (its) website."
In addition, Hervé de Charette was asked "to transfer the property rights of the trade mark to its legal claimants".
If you deny this, we will file a lawsuit against you in order to respect the law and to defend the legitimate rights of UDF members, the signatories warned.
In their letter, they emphasize that "UDF" has been a full-fledged political movement since 1978 and are reminiscent that Morin and his friends "made a choice" to abandon these in 2007 to start a competing movement, as others had already done in 2002 to form the "UMP" party.
Therefore, according to the undersigners, the Nouveau center party has no rights whatsoever to use the UDF (Union pour la démocratie française).
They continue to recall that in 2007, UDF's last congression passed the three-year accession to the MoDem party, as well as the establishment of an office in defense "of material and moral interests" of the movement founded by Valéry Giscard d'Estaing.
As far as Hervé de Charette is concerned, the office continues, "at the time when he applied for the brand name, he was no longer a member of the UDF for three years," but the UMP.
He benefits in a scandalous and fraudulent way, from the lack of deposit of the acronym UDF to carry this out for himself.
Furthermore, you point out that this recklessness is in the best way to turn into a criminal offense, and ironize the strategy of the NC: "It seems dignificant in politics as well as in life to make a name for ourselves instead of trying to get out of anonymity by fraudulently acquiring a name to which you have no legal claims."
Affaire Liliane Bettencourt : François-Marie Banier is convicted of exploration of weakness
Nanterre's court on Friday decided to judge the artist François-Marie Banier to determine whether, as the billionaire's daughter Liliane Bettencourt claims, he exploited a weak moment of the eighty-year-old to sneak some of her assets.
The 62-year-old photographer, who is known for his star photos, will have to answer in court on 15 and 16 April 2010 for exploiting weakness, an offence punishable by a maximum sentence of three years imprisonment and a fine of 375,000 euros.
This decision means that, in fact, there will be a trial in this affair, worthy of the saga of one of France's richest families, in which mother and daughter shred each other, and at the same time have the reins of the L'Oréal beauty empire.
Likewise, however, the 15th Criminal Chamber ordered a medical examination of 87-year-old Liliane Bettencourt, which is carried out jointly by three doctors.
L'Oréal's heiress and main shareholder has so far refused to undergo such an investigation, especially among the conditions put forward during investigations by Nanterre's public prosecutor's office.
Doctors appointed on Friday are due to present a report before March 10, 20010, in which you will clearly announce whether and at what time Liliane Bettencourt may have been in a state of weakness.
I think she'll undergo these investigations.
I'm going to talk to her, Ms Bettencourt's lawyer, Me Georges Kiejman, explained after the trial.
As far as Fran�çoise Bettencourt-Meyers' lawyer, Me Olivier Metzner is concerned, he is very pleased with the court's decision.
This is an excellent news (...). The report will prove that he (Banier) manipulated this old lady, which must be protected, he affirmed.
For two years, Liliane Bettencourt's only daughter has launched a criminal lawsuit against François-Marie Banier, photographer and all-round artist, accusing him of exploiting her mother's weakness for years to steal from donations totaling one billion euros in the form of bank cheques, insurance policies or paintings.
For her part, Liliane Bettencourt had always refuted being manipulated by the photographer, whom she sees as a longtime friend.
Although the court would have to examine the admissibility of the lawsuit initiated by Ms Bettencourt-Meyers, the lawyers' plaidoys led to a major family debacle on Friday.
Liliane Bettencourt's lawyer invoked three letters sent by his client to her notar (1999, 2003 and 2005), in which she wrote that she distrusted the husband of Françoise Bettencourt-Meyers.
According to her lawyer, Liliane Bettencourt added: "Could you please explain to my daughter that I have been writing to Baniers for fifteen years, once or twice a daily.
Françoise has never been able to speak since their marriage.”
Nanterre's public prosecutor's office is again of the opinion that the plaintiff had no right to respond in this way, especially because in recent days a guardianship judge has rejected her request to initiate judicial protection proceedings against her mother.
Liliane Bettencourt "is fully justifiable, is not under protection and has no guardian.
No one has the right to act in their place," Marie-Christine Daubigney, the prosecutor's representative, affirmed.
The court has now decided to deliver a verdict after the admissibility trial of Mme Bettencourt-Meyers took place.
manager revolution or the end of Strategic Early Enlightenment?
If nothing changes in the leadership of the great French companies, our struggle for the development of economic intelligence has been in vain ...
For over a decade, the attitude of the management of large companies in relation to its employees, said the position of the companies vis-à-vis the consumers before the marketing revolution: It is for the staff to join "values" to impose the best, as it used to be designed on the consumer products by the technicians in the factory.
By placing the customer at the center of the companies, the marketing revolution has given the strategies of conquering the markets in the place ... although the authoritarian temptation products separately impose factors preferences of the customers again regularly!
Demobilization of employees can lead to deadly demoralization.
The suicides at France Telecom, PSA or Renault are just at the forefront of a much larger phenomenon that touches employees in particular.
This demobilization has a negative effect on the development of all the intellectual functions of the company, in particular those of R&D, which is even more crucial, to those of economic intelligence.
In fact, the IE (Intelligence Economique = economic intelligence) "demands from all parties involved (...) a proactive approach and a future-oriented view"; it claims from the employees a commitment to which the instructions of authoritarian management completely contradict.
That is why we call for an operational revolution, which would be to place the employees at the center of the company’s concerns on an equal footing alongside customers.
The very conditional benefits of internal opinion surveys
Will the internal opinion polls, such as Orange's questionnaire submitted to its wage earners, stimulate this revolution in terms of management practice?
I'm afraid, sadly not.
- First of all, because I was able to determine that in the face of a new situation after many detours, the whole structure finally begins to do what it has always done.
Also because authoritarianism - one of the pathologies of power - increases proportionally to the significance of its own failures.
And finally, because most executives have a greatly simplified idea of human behavior.
Emerging from the major engineering schools their interpretation of psychology is mechanistic.
Through intellectual comfort, they adopt the old behaviorist theory, which has recently been reactivated and unknowingly widely circulated by research institutes and the media.
Thus and by suggesting that it is possible to operate the "levers" (sic) on the commitment of employees, a recent Le Figaro.fr survey by Ipsos encourages executives in an attitude towards a mechanistic control system that is unsuitable for today's world.
Identify and measure mobilization factors
Instead, an operational revolution necessary for the proper functioning of modern companies presupposes the adoption of a definitive view of human behavior.
It consists in incorporating a basic protocol into any consideration or practice of governance: for wage earners, commitment to the company is only possible if it clearly appears as a means of realizing part of their dreams, their ambition and projects.
At the moment, only the studies from the multifunctional analyss take over this concept of the motivation of the employees.
These studies have shown that they assess their company on the basis of a predetermined dox that, through their perceived image of the company, allows them to estimate what it offers them to achieve their life goals.
Thanks to the identification and measurement of these mobilization factors, the executives of the companies can control their "social performance", predict and anticipate and place their human resource management in the right place again.
In this way, they create fertile ground that is favorable for the coordinated development of robust and efficient economic intelligence.
Oslo's speech: the 'fair war' earns Obama praise from the right
The American right on Friday greeted Barack Obama's speech the previous evening in Oslo and was satisfied that the 2009 Nobel Peace Prize defended the notion of "righteous war" against the enemies of the United States.
The Conservatives, who mocked the allocation of the allotment prize to Obama on October 9, this time applauded the president's speech, following the example of the former vice-presidential nominee in the 2008 election Sarah Palin.
I liked what he said Palin explained the face of the right wing of the Republican party in the daily USA Today, adding that she herself raised the issue of a "sometimes-necessary" war in her just-published memoir.
War is, of course, the last thing I think Americans want to do, but it's necessary.
We'll have to stop the terrorists down there, she says.
Obama, who inherited two wars from his predecessor George W. Bush, the wars in Iraq and Afghanistan, made an effort Thursday in Oslo to justify the use of force; a way of declaring his decision nine days ago to send 30,000 additional troops to Afghanistan.
Another opponent of Obama, Nevat Gingrich, argued that the president had done a "very good job" in Oslo.
I found that the speech was really very good, rejoiced the former president of the House of Representatives, who especially acknowledged the reference to "the existence of evil in the world" in Obama's words.
In some parties, I found this speech to be entirely historical, explained Ginrich, who was interviewed on the radio show "The Takeaway."
The previous lawmaker, seen by some as a possible nominee for the Republican nomination to the 2012 presidential election, said he was delighted to have seen a "president of the left," reminding members of the Nobel committee that they "weren't free and couldn't hand out an award for peace if the use of force didn't exist."
For Michael Gerson, former writer of George W. Bush and now editor at The Washington Post, Obama gave "a very American speech."
In addition to portraying himself as a "cosmopolitan," as "he always does," he "spoke like an American president" when he pointed out that Europeans "live like in a security bubble that they didn't create themselves and don't do much for their protection," Gerson said.
Among the few unconvinced right-wingers, former U.N. ambassador John Bolton, a falcon of the Bush era, described the speech on the National Review Online's website as "boring, lofty and inspirationless."
Commenting on the president's left, Democratic representative Dennis Kucinich expresses concern about how Barack Obama is defending its resceing to the armed forces.
If you package the war under the guise of the judiciary, the massacre of the innocent can be easily justified, he warns.
However, the press of the moderate leftists remains praised alongside the president.
The Los Angeles Times, which is still of the view that the Nobel Prize was awarded prematurely to Obama, referred to the address itself for a speaker like him known for his talent as a "monumental work."
The speech in Oslo "provides a schedule for the management of international decisions against conflicts, poverty and repression," according to the California newspaper enthusiastically.
The New York Times is of the view that Obama "proclaimed the necessary discourse" and describes his speech as "bleak and exemplary at the same time."
Long-distancer: Three marathons for an agreement that secures supply for Christmas
After a three-week negotiating marathon, the trade unions of the road association and the TLF - the only employer organization that remained until the end - came to an agreement on Friday evening putting an end to the strike order and the looming severe supply bottleneck.
At the signature meeting on Friday evening, Secretary of State Dominique Bussereau, in charge of transport, "welcomed" the agreement, which was initiated on Wednesday morning after several weeks of negotiations.
After a second negotiation night, the negotiators separated early Friday morning in the hopes of quickly signing the agreement late in the morning.
And this is despite the fact that the three employer associations (FNTR, Unostra and OTRE) had left the meeting.
However, more than six hours went from the beginning of the meeting at 11:00 a.m. to the conclusion of the agreement and the agreement was not signed until 6:00 p.m.
The conflict "would have caused a great deal of damage to the companies" and "it was to be feared that it would affect the holidays at the end of the year," according to Bussereau.
The agreement envisages a 4% increase in wages for the lowest wages (local and regional road transport) and 2.9% for the highest wage groups (international transport).
The hourly rate for employees with the lowest wages thus rises from 8.72 euros to 9.06 euros.
"That's not much, but we're starting small," says Patrice Clos (FO).
Activists are now instructed to no longer block the supply centres.
"However, there will probably always be some unruly, which are not satisfied with the agreement," he adds.
TLF Chairman Philippe Grillot spoke of a "good agreement," though it was "difficult to admit these wage increases in the face of international competition."
According to him, the increase is 170 euros per month for the low and 100 to 120 euros for the high wage groups.
"This agreement is based on common sense and respect for the employees," said Maxime Dumont (CFDT), while Thierry Douine (CFTC) spoke of "a historic agreement."
Richard Jaubert (CGT) commented on "an acceptable compromise" in this regard.
The agreement also provides for an increase in travel costs by 3% and the maintenance of the total burial for road transport in the first half of 2010, which will, in particular, make it possible to address the 13th monthly salary and the modernisation of collective agreements.
M. Bussereau recalled that "the sinking of levies for employers in the amount of 100 million euros" was the subject of an amendment by the government to the draft law of corrected financies for 2009, which was passed by the National Assembly on the night from Thursday to Friday.
The government is calling for "an identical" vote from the Senate.
The signature of the TLF allows the agreement to initially apply to the companies affiliated to this organization and that, in the event of an extension by the Minister of Labour, it will then apply to the entire profession.
This will be the case "in the shortest possible time," Bussereau assures.
The opposition of the employers' associations can only delay the procedure.
The FNTR association denounced a "fraudulent trade" because the 100 million euros "had already been promised to the sector to offset part of the carbon tax."
The secretary of state for transport forced us to negotiate as soon as possible, but not on the basis of a joint analysis, but in order not to annoy the goose eaters on the approaching Christmas days, laments the Unostra.
For the association Otre, the agreement threatens "to shovel the small and medium-sized companies of the industry a large grave in the coming 12 months."
Line RER A: Disruption this weekend, trade unions dissatisfied with RATP's proposals
The disturbances on the RER A line, France's largest high-speed rail network for the Île-de-France area, continued over the weekend, while a meeting continued on Friday, the 2nd day of strikes between the unions and RATP's (Independent Paris passenger traffic administration), which will keep strikers waiting with their demands for wage surcharges.
There were no trains on this important shopping weekend before Christmas between 10:00 a.m. and 8:00 p.m. On the central line (between La Défense and Nation) only one of two trains and only one of four trains was available.
The connection to SNCF's trains was disrupted in Nanterre Préfecture, forcing travellers to change trains.
The department store chain Galeries Lafayette, which counts 200,000 customers every day, welcomed the fact that "RATP strives to connect the big shops in terms of traffic."
The RER A line permeates the metropolitan area from west to east.
The section of RATP connects Saint-Germain-en-Laye and Nanterre as well as Boissy-Saint-Léger and Marne-La-Vallée.
On Friday, about one in two trains operated as scheduled at rush hour, and almost none during the low-traffic period.
As on Thursday, there was no rage of people on the platforms, as the residents of Île-de-France had adjusted to the situation.
So early in the morning, Saint-Germain-en-Laye (Yvelines) station was far less crowded than usual, mainly because of the lack of high school students.
In Boissy-Saint-Léger (Val-de-Marne), Jack Nbakina, an engineer (29), declared that he would have gotten up extra "an hour and a half earlier to get to work on time."
The SNCF, which had planned to take care of the passengers by servants and police officers, pointed out that there were no bottlenecks at Saint-Lazare station.
The trains operated by the SNCF on line RER A or in the direction of Cergy-Le-Haut arrived or departed via Saint-Lazare station in order to ensure a connection to the metros.
On Saturday and Sunday there were again general meetings of the strikers, during which the results were reviewed, which the associations had classified as "meager" after the meeting with the leadership on Friday afternoon.
The RER Managing Director proposed the specific drafting of a protocol for the RER A line with regard to all relevant aspects, working hours, professional careers, etc.
However, that's not what the drivers want. According to Thierry Garon (Sud), they're demanding a long-due wage surcharge.
The CGT was outraged that the RATP itself puts its wage surcharge proposals in brackets and emphasized that the leadership "is not able to solve a problem, while this was also possible with the long-distance drivers and the SNCF."
RATP clarified that a new meeting was planned for Monday's "comprehensive examination of working conditions."
This also shows the associations that the company "ignores the weekend drivers."
In the morning, about 150 drivers visited the branch of a committee in Paris.
Again, it was the leadership who to date rejected any discussion during the strike, and suggested a meeting after a reassuration of the situation.
On Tuesday, the conflict loomed to extend to the RER B line after an advance announcement by Unsa and Indépendants.
The associations demand a wage surcharge of 120 (Unsa) to 150 euros, of which 30 euros are variable (CGT, FO, CFDT, Sud, Indépendants). In addition, they lament the deterioration of working conditions on this route, on which millions of travelers are travelling every day.
The drivers of the RER line, who have many years of professional experience, receive a gross wage of 2,200 to 2,700 euros as well as a wage surcharge of 600 to 650 euros for shift services as well as Sundays and holidays.
Turkish Constitutional Court dissolves pro-Kurdish party. Decision with serious consequences
Turkey's Constitutional Court on Friday announced the dissolution of the country's pro-Kurdish main party; a decision that was immediately followed by angry demonstrations in the Kurdish community in the country's south-east and in Istanbul.
This decision threatens to enormously complicate the work of the government, which strives to shake hands with the Kurdish community in order to put an end to the conflicts that have lasted for 25 years.
The "party for a democratic society" (DTP) was dissolved because it had become a "site for activities detrimental to the independence of the state and its indivisible unity," the president of the Hasim Kiliç Constitutional Court told the press four days after the decision.
According to Kiliç, the decision would have been taken unanimously by 11 judges, requiring a majority of seven votes to impose dissolution. He also pointed out that 37 of the party’s leaders, including Chairman Ahmet Türk and parliamentarian Aysel Tugluk, have been excluded from political events for a period of five years.
Furthermore, the judge promulgated the abolition of parliamentary immunity enjoyed by Türk and Tugluk, as well as the confiscation of the party’s goods.
The DTP now has 21 seats in parliament (out of 550).
Ahead of the verdict, the party leaders commented on the fact that the parliamentarians would rather leave parliament rather than meet like non-partisan parliamentarians.
The decision followed proceedings initiated in 2007 by the prosecutor of Turkey's Abdurrahman Yalçinkaya federal court. He accused the DTP of following the "instructions" of the Kurdistan Workers' Party (PKK), which is considered a terrorist organization by Ankara and numerous other countries.
Numerous observers are of the opinion that the DTP is the legal political showcase of the PKK rebels.
The party assures to have "no connection" to the PKK, but refuses to designate it as a terrorist organization and calls on the government to negotiate.
The verdict of the Constitutional Court spokes the government in between, which strives to accommodate the Kurdish community and proposes a series of measures to strengthen the rights of Kurds in order to allow the support of the PKK by the Kurds to dry up and put an end to the conflicts.
The leaders of the DTP reaffirm that a dissolution could lead to a re-eruption of tensions in Southeast Anatolia, where numerous demonstrations against the detention conditions of PKK chief Abdullah Öcalan had led to clashes with the police in recent weeks.
Since the court's announcement, thousands of protesters have gathered outside DTP's premises in the multimillion-dollar city of Diyarbakir in the south-east, predominantly populated by Kurds.
Police deployed tear gas grenades and water cannons as protesters began throwing Molotov cocktails and stones.
Hundreds of people also demonstrated in Istanbul.
After the verdict, Türk assumed the court's decision "will deepen desperation even further."
"Turkey cannot clarify this (Kurdish, ed.) question on the basis of a party's ban," he told the press.
The government, consisting of a party emerging from the Islamist movement, also regretted the decision.
"We're opposed to the closure of the parties out of principle.
That doesn't solve the problems," according to Energy Minister Taner Yildiz.
"This decision is a torpedoing of the democratic opening promoted by the government," according to political market observer Ahmet Insel.
"You can obviously prepare for a reaction from the PKK," he adds.
On Thursday, the PKK pleaded guilty to an attack in which seven soldiers died three days earlier in northern Turkey, one of the most brutal attacks in recent months.
Snowfall can come
It's a really crisp winter, that's what Wolfgang Brauburger wants.
Not because he wants to harass the citizens, says the winter service manager of the FES.
But so that its employees can try out and master the entire vehicle technology.
At the weekend, according to meteorologists, it could be ready for the first time.
More than 330 employees and 120 vehicles are available to take care of snow and ice.
The FES proceeds according to a precise plan.
As soon as it snows nationwide in Frankfurt and the temperatures are below zero degrees, 20 so-called A routes with large scattering vehicles are driven off, explains Brauburger.
These include, for example, main thoroughfares such as the Neue Weg and the Hanauer Landstraße.
But also fire brigade access roads, public transport routes, pedestrian zones and routes with gradients, such as Atzelbergstraße.
FES service begins at three in the morning
The approximately 1040 kilometers would have to be scattered if necessary in a maximum of three hours with moist salt, which adheres better.
That's why the FES starts at three o'clock at night, "so that the routes are free until the professional traffic starts".
It will be difficult if it does not snow until 7.30 a.m.
Then we have a problem, says the winter service manager, "because then no one is willing to make room for the scattering vehicles".
Only then, if it does not snow any further, it is the turn of feeder and access roads, which also include Rat-Beil-Straße.
The last priority is side and side streets.
There will be complaints there, Brauburger knows.
At five o'clock in the morning, the footy people of the FES make their way, danger points and transitions are primarily cleared.
The FES can usually not take care of all cycle paths.
There is a special service in Frankfurt that keeps an eye on the bridges from 22 to 4.30 a.m., says Brauburger.
Since there is smoothness on these, as soon as the temperature drops below two degrees, the spreader is on the way.
If necessary, it will also be called by the police.
Cradle of dinosaurs
Tawa hallae looked like many other carnivorous dinosaurs: The two-meter-long and 1.50-meter-tall animal ran on two powerful hind legs, had an elongated skull with sharp, curved teeth, short arms with sickle claws and a long tail.
Had the species lived a few million years later, it wouldn't be anything special.
Yet Tawa hallae was one of the first dinosaurs with these body traits, report Sterling Nesbitt of the University of Texas at Austin and colleagues in Thursday's journal Science.
The animal lived about 215 million years ago in what is now New Mexico, which at that time was located in a dry zone of the supercontinent Pangea at the equator.
Tawa is opening a new window for the early evolution of dinosaurs, Nesbitt says.
It shows us a lot about the kinship, the spread and the features of the early dinosaurs.
Nesbitt and other paleontologists began excavating at Ghost Ranch in northern New Mexico in 2006.
We've sometimes found a dozen dinosaur bones in one day, that's unprecedented for debris from the Triassia, reports team member Randall Irmis of the Utah Museum of Natural History.
Some of the bones the researchers quickly identified as a new species.
During the excavation, they gathered the remains of a total of five individuals.
Fund confirms theories about Dino-early history
The name "Tawa" comes from the sun god of the Hopi Indians.
For Sterling Nesbitt, it's an extraordinary find: "Dinosaur fossils from the Triassic are extremely rare, usually you only find individual fragments."
Other dinosaur experts don't necessarily share the enthusiasm.
This find is not particularly spectacular, but it confirms some theories about the early history of dinosaurs, says Martin Sander from the University of Bonn.
According to the current doctrine, the dinosaurs first appeared about 230 million years ago.
However, many of the oldest fossils are incomplete, which is why the assignment of these primal dinosaurs is usually controversial.
For example, the experts debate when the lizards split into their three most important lines of development: the carnivorous theropods, which later included the Tyrannosaurus rex, the Velociraptor and also the birds, the four-legged sauropods known as "long necks" and their relatives, and the plant-eating Ornithischia (Bird Basin dinosaurs)
According to Nesbitt's analysis and his colleagues, Tawa hallae is an original theropody.
It has an interesting combination of primitive and advanced properties, says Nesbitt.
On the skull of Tawa hallae, for example, the researchers found clues to aerial sacks, such as the descendants of theropods, the birds.
Thanks to the aerial sacks, the breathing of the birds is more efficient than that of the mammals.
At Tawa hallae, the bones of the spine were also apparently partially filled with air.
Tawa is the oldest and most primitive theropod with aerial sacks, Randall Irmis says.
Since the related line of the giant sauropods later also possessed airbags and lightweight bones, probably the common ancestor of both groups had already produced this useful innovation.
At Least Three Waves of Migration to North America
Because the most primitive dinosaurs lived in South America, the researchers conclude that the lizards appeared there for the first time.
Accordingly, the three lines of development separated early on and spread from South America across all continents, which at that time still formed a single landmass as supercontinent Pangaea.
This is supported by the fact that the team, together with Tawa hallae, found the remains of two other, not particularly closely related theropods.
The three can't derive from a common, immigrated ancestor, the researchers argue.
We believe there were at least three waves of migration to North America, says Alan Turner of the American Museum of Natural History in New York, a co-author of the science study.
Of course, there are also contradictions.
An even older species, Silesaurus, which belongs to a sister group of dinosaurs, lived in today's Poland, says the Bonn paleontologist Martin Sander.
In his opinion, a distorted image could emerge, because in South America especially many primitive dino fossils have been preserved, while on other continents hardly anything can be found.
How the dinosaurs managed to evolve from a relatively rare sideline of the reptile trunk to the dominant land vertebrates of the Medieval Earth is still puzzling.
During the Triassics, the dinosaurs shared their habitat with many other reptiles, for example with the ancestors of today's crocodiles and the flying dinosaurs and with numerous other lizards.
Towards the end of the Triassic, dinosaurs spread around the world and developed many new species, while kinship died out on the border from the Triassia to the Jura.
Success story didn’t last forever
There are two hypotheses for explaining this change, says Martin Sander.
There may have been a mass extinction that the dinosaurs accidentally survived, but the other reptiles didn't.
Or the dinosaurs were better adapted, allowing them to knock the competition out of the field.
In any case, the success story didn’t last forever.
150 million years later, a meteorite impact of a group of animals for flowering, which had also developed in the Triassic and since then had a shadowy existence: the mammals.
Solidarity from Vienna to Sao Paulo
The list is long and the signatories are international.
Professors at the University of Toronto stand on it, the University of Cambridge, the University of Vienna or Berkeley in California.
Lecturers from Naples are present, from Quebec, Edinburgh, New York, Sao Paulo, Berlin or Bremen.
The police eviction of the occupied casino of Frankfurt Goethe University is making waves worldwide.
278 professors and lecturers of Goethe University, but also of universities throughout Germany, Europe and North and South America, show solidarity with the protesting students on the Westend campus.
In a statement written in German and English, they "disapproved" the police operation prompted by the Bureau.
University President Werner Müller-Estler had the building occupied by students and lecturers vacated on an educational strike last week because walls and works of art had been smeared.
Five students were wounded.
The property damages, the signatories judge, did not justify "the violent dissolution of self-organized courses of students and lecturers by a police squad."
Eviction constituted an "unacceptable intervention" in the freedom of research and teaching.
The exclusive focus on vandalism serves "the deligitimization and criminalization of the protest" and distracts from the contents.
The Bureau evaded the necessary discourse.
The lecturers call for the immediate cessation of criminal prosecution.
The majority of occupiers did not support the property damage, it said.
Protest legitimized
The youth of the Verdi trade union and the German Trade Union Confederation also show solidarity with the students.
The protest is a legitimate expression of a growing concern about one's own future, says Verdi state youth secretary Alexander Klein.
Hesse's Minister of Science Eva Kühne-Hörmann (CDU) defended the actions of university president and the police.
We are thankful to the university president.
Destruction and occupation are completely unacceptable means, she said in a current hour on Thursday in the Wiesbaden state parliament.
We understand the protest, but no understanding on vandalism, according to CDU state MP Peter Beuth.
This was seen differently by the opposition factions SPD, Greens and the left.
The CDU takes painted walls as a pretext to criminalize the protest, Janine Wissler (Left) said.
Instead of dealing with the arguments and demands of the students, they have vacated.
The left has tabled a motion calling on the Home Secretary to give reports on the police operation.
New Silicon-Valley
For many years it was considered a secret city, inaccessible to Western visitors.
Selenograd, one of ten boroughs of the capital, Moscow, was a tightly guarded site for secret armaments research in the Soviet Union.
Today, the commune, with its 216 000 inhabitants, 37 kilometers from Moscow's center, is considered "Russian Silicon Valley."
Significant research and production sites for micro and nanoelectronics are located here.
Since September 2000, representatives of Selenograd had established contacts with the Riedberg science location in Frankfurt - with the university institutes and the companies of the Frankfurt Innovation Center (FIZ).
There were several mutual visits and on Wednesday Selenograd and Riedberg signed a "protocologue for closer cooperation" in Frankfurt.
The Moscow vice-mayor Yuri Rosljak and prefect Anatoly Smirnov of Selenograd led the Russian delegation, for Frankfurt signed planning director Edwin Schwarz.
In 2010, representatives of FIZ and small and medium-sized enterprises of the scientific research focus for a seminar will travel to Selenograd.
Scientific Exchange
It's about continuous scientific exchange.
But it's also about economic relations.
Companies of the FIZ are enabled to gain a foothold in the growing Russian market.
Conversely, Russian science companies want to secure orders in Germany.
Selenograd is the headquarters of major Russian research institutes and companies.
These include, for example, the Moscow Institute of Electron Technology, the large corporations Sitronics and Rossijskaya Elektronika.
The commune was founded in 1958 as a so-called "sociast city" for armaments research.
Selenograd (literally translated "Green Town") got its name from the wooded hilly landscape in which the research institutes are embedded.
Stuttgart brake pad
The dimension of Stuttgart 21 cannot be set high enough.
For the proponents of the project, it is simply about "the new heart of Europe."
S21 is much more than lowering a station, more than a monstrous construction project that will plunge the city of Baden-Württemberg’s state capital into helpless construction site chaos for a decade.
Stuttgart 21 originated from the spirit of the 1980s.
Head stations with tracks that end in the middle of the city were at that time regarded as transport-technical fossils of the 19th century, which acted like brake pads of a modern rail traffic, since it once took an enormous amount of time to span locomotives from back to front.
Drive-in, keep short, keep driving - that's how it's supposed to go.
And that's where the head stations should disappear: in Frankfurt and Munich.
And in Stuttgart: a transit station that is located about halfway on the trans-European rail magistrale between Paris and Budapest/Bratislava - that's why the slogan from the "Heart of Europe".
S21 is now actually addressed, even though the visions of the 1980s are no longer compatible with reality.
Even very ambitious planners have recognized that it is almost priceless in the Hessian and the Bavarian city to implement the lowering.
Head stations, however, no longer have an entry and exit problem since there are modern reversible trains with trains and control cars at both ends.
Nevertheless: Stuttgart 21 together with the ICE new construction line to Ulm is now being approached.
State and local politicians enforced it.
The railway participates, also because the risks of the largest individual infrastructure project of the Republic bear taxpayers and not the state corporation.
In terms of urban planning, the project can still be somewhat justified.
New urban space is being created, which - believes the computer-generated graphics of the planners - is equipped with a lot of greenery.
S21 is harmful to the rail transport system.
The through station and the extension of the route are intended for passenger traffic, to which - typically 80s - huge growth is attributed.
At that time, there was no competition from low-cost airlines.
However, modern transport policy must first be about strengthening freight transport, as transport by rail is considerably more environmentally friendly than by truck on the road.
But the new line is not suitable for freight trains because of violent gradients.
It is even more serious that S21 indirectly affects many other railway projects.
A good six billion euros are to be digged up in Swabian.
That's the official number.
Experts consider ten to 15 billion to be realistic.
What is certain is that in the next decade the money will be missing at other junctions and bottlenecks on the net.
A total of sixty larger projects are on the list of the Ministry of Transport.
One of the most important ones is the expansion of the 180-kilometre route through the Rhine Valley.
Since 1987 it has been built on the route at slow motion.
It would be urgent to press the tube properly here.
But S21 will act as a brake pad.
The Rhein valley line becomes a bottleneck for international freight transport: right in the heart of Europe.
Government considers verdict on airport
The state government has left open in the Wiesbaden state parliament whether it will appeal against the judgment of the Hessian Administrative Court (VGH) on the night flight ban.
The CDU/FDP coalition has commissioned experts to have the more than 400-page VGH verdict legally examined, said Economy Minister Dieter Posch (FDP).
The VGH in Kassel had justified in writing on these days that, in the event of the construction of the planned north-west runway at Frankfurt Airport, the country would have to reorganize the night flight ban.
The approval of 17 flights between 23 and 5 o'clock, as the state government envisages, should not be compatible with the legally required protection of the population from nocturnal aircraft noise.
Coalition must decide by January 4 whether to appeal the verdict.
The opposition criticized this postponement and urged the representatives of the government to waive a revision at the Federal Administrative Court in Leipzig.
SPD parliamentary group leader Torsten Schäfer-Gümbel urged CDU and FDP to return to their pre-election promises on a night flight ban. Otherwise, this should be regarded as a "breach of words".
It's damn heavy if you're allowed to keep your word, Greens leader Tarek Al-Wazir said in reference to the FDP election slogan "We're keeping word."
Minister Posch rejected the accusation of Schäfer-Gümbel that the state government is trying to come with "legal twisting and trickery" for their night flights.
A detailed legal examination takes time.
The coalition does not want to operate on the basis of a "quick first impression".
We need a legally compliant decision, said Stefan Müller (FDP).
Just as the opposition represents, his group's statements on the night flight ban were never.
U.S. banks repay aid
The crisis-ridden U.S. major banks roam their state shackles.
The Bank of America on Wednesday repaid the aid from the bank bailout fund Tarp in a total of 45 billion dollars (a good 30 billion euros) in one fell swoop.
Citigroup is also planning to refund state aid, according to a reportation by U.S. broadcaster CNBC.
The institutions want to get rid of the restrictions that have come with the acceptance of the money – such as the limitation in the payment of manager bonuses as well as the payment of high fees.
Bank of America had raised 19 billion-dollar fresh money from investors via convertible bonds.
She took the rest of the sum from her own cash register, which, among other things, was well filled by branch sales.
On Wednesday evening, bank chief Kenneth Lewis explicitly thanked American taxpayers: The cash injection had helped over a very difficult time.
At the height of the financial crisis at the height of the financial crisis, the money house had withdrew with the takeover of investment bank Merrill Lynch.
Citigroup also wants to procure money on the capital market for exemption from its liabilities to the state.
Already on Thursday, the U.S. bank could announce a roughly 20 billion-dollar increase in its capital, CNBC reported, citing circles.
A Citigroup spokesperson declined to comment.
In June, Goldman Sachs, JP Morgan Chase and Morgan Stanley had already repaided their aid.
Fast Choice
After all, everything went faster than expected.
Barely two weeks after the ZDF administrative board forced the incumbent editor-in-chief Nikolaus Brender out of office, the broadcaster can present a successor.
Last night, the panel wanted to vote on personnel.
Peter Frey, head of the ZDF capital studio in Berlin, was regarded as the favorite.
There was, however, no confirmation from the broadcaster - officially, so as not to pre-empt the procedure.
However, it should also have played a role not to further damage the ZDF intendant Markus Schächter, should this proposal also not come through with the board of directors.
Frey, Schächter's candidate, needs the support of nine of the 14 panel members.
The result of the vote had not yet been determined at the end of this issue.
Two weeks ago, Schächter had failed on the board of directors with his proposal to extend the contract with Nikolaus Brender for another five years.
Only seven members spoke out in favour of Brender, seven voted against him. Brender’s deselection was a bitter defeat for Schächter, but an expectable one.
Because the Union majority in the Board of Directors had already signaled weeks before the election that Brender did not want to confirm in office.
Hessian Prime Minister Roland Koch had organised the resistance against the editor-in-chief.
With the argument that under Brender, the news broadcasts of the ZDF had lost ratings, he rejected another term of office of the highly respected editor-in-chief.
Until shortly before the election, there were therefore protests in which the political influence on the ZDF was scourged.
Most recently, even 35 state lawyers had interfered in the debate, which assessed Koch’s approach as unconstitutional.
It's used nothing.
to the person
Peter Frey was born in Bingen on August 4, 1957.
He studied politics, pedagogy and Romance studies.
Afterwards he worked at Südwestfunk and the Frankfurter Rundschau.
Frey moved to ZDF in 1983, first to "heute-journal".
Subsequent stations: 1988 to 1990 personal speaker of the editor-in-chief; 1991/92 correspondent in Washington; until 1998 head and moderator of the "morning magazine"; until 2001 head of ZDF foreign policy; ever since head of the ZDF capital studio.
The Greens announced a constitutional lawsuit after Brender's deselection, which was still being voted journalist of the year by the industry newspaper Medium magazine on Wednesday.
Koch's opposition on the Board of Directors, the Rhineland-Palatinate Prime Minister Kurt Beck, declared last week that the countries should change the ZDF state treaty in such a way that personnel proposals of the director cannot be blocked by a politically motivated majority.
However, Beck also ruled out an excerpt of party politicians from ZDF's supervisory bodies.
Brender's expected successor Peter Frey had recommended himself to the board of directors with a smart move - by heavily criticising the panel immediately after Brender deselection.
Thanks to this evidence of journalistic independence, he was able to nominate Schächter without loss of face for the post of editor-in-chief as well as the board of directors, without being suspected of bringing a partygoer into office.
Frey is already attributed to the left-liberals in party arithmetics.
Study is decluttered
Partial success for the students: After a year of the educational strike, the ministers of culture of the countries finally intervened on Thursday: At a meeting of the Conference of Ministers of Education and Higher Education, both sides agreed to improve the study conditions at bachelor’s and master’s.
In the future, the students should also be able to manage their studies.
In detail, they agreed to reduce the examination loads: In principle, each study module should only be completed with a single examination.
In addition, the workload of the students is to be checked and "a realistic and justifiable measure" is to be guaranteed.
Politicians and universities also want to simplify the recognition of examinations between universities within and outside Germany.
The countries committed themselves to making common structural requirements more flexible for bachelor’s and master’s degree programmes.
In addition, there should not be any deviating country regulations.
Fewer Examinations
Since the beginning of the student protests in June, ministers of culture and university rectors have been wearing the winged word of better studability in their mouth.
Now, for the first time, it has been filled with a concrete content.
The so-called accreditation council decided to drastically reduce the number of examinations in part: "Each module usually concludes with only one exam," according to a 30-page paper, which is intended to partially reorganize the admission of degree programmes.
With the "significant reduction in the burden of testing," accreditation council chairman Reinhold Grimm explained, a "misdevelopment should be stopped, which significantly impaired studyability."
In addition, "studyability" should be "the decisive criterion" in the design in the future.
Modules such as content should be "strictly checked" whether "unnecessary restrictions of the students in the choice of
Events "would be avoided.
The students had "claims to obtain leeway that enable and promote self-initiative."
The rules should apply to the admission of new, but also in the review of existing courses of study.
These have to be "reaccredited" every five years – but this means that it will take until 2015 for all degree programmes to be reviewed.
Ten accreditation agencies are responsible for this nationwide, which are subject to the accreditation council established by the countries.
The ministers of culture had urged the countries "emphatically" in October to influence the agencies in such a way that the abundance of substances is not too large and the examination regime is not too strict.
In individual federal states, universities and technical colleges have already agreed on appropriate measures with the state ministries.
Rhineland-Palatinate was the only state to have pledged additional funds for the further development of the Bologna process.
Of the money, the universities are primarily intended to set up tutor programs and strengthen study advice as well as examination and foreign offices.
Land and universities want to spend ten million euros on this.
At the beginning of 2010, the government intends to introduce an amendment to the Higher Education Act in parliament.
According to this, only a single examination should be necessary for the completion of a study model.
The design of studies is to become more flexible and the recognition of certificates of achievement at other universities is to be ensured and access to the master’s degree is to be facilitated.
By the end of next year, all of the country’s degree programmes are to be checked for their studyability.
The government and the opposition argued again on Thursday about the study conditions in the country.
In addition to praise, there was also criticism of the decisions.
The Federal Executive Board of the Juso Higher Education Groups demanded that the design of education policy should no longer take place "behind closed doors" of ministers and university rectors in the future.
At the latest after the educational strikes, the students should now also "have a say" as those affected.
The future academics received support from the president of the university rectors' conference (HRK), Margret Wintermantel: "We need the experiences of the students," she said on Thursday at a meeting with the ministers of culture with whom the HRK discussed a joint reform paper.
The Bonn decisions did not go far enough for the trade union Education and Academia (GEW) as a whole: it calls for a legal entitlement, which allows every graduate with a bachelor’s degree access to a further master’s degree program.
On the other hand, countries must ensure that today's generation of bachelor's students will have sufficient master's degree places with the best possible study conditions, according to GEW board member Andreas Keller.
Countries need to hire significantly more lecturers.
However, the ministers of culture did not comment on freer access to the master.
More than a footnote to Herta Müller
Entering into Germany and out is beautiful almost no scrutiny..." is the starting line of a poem by the lyricist Werner Söllner, in which he addresses in disturbing fragranism the arrival experience of a dissident who gained freedom, but at the same time lost his language space.
Werner Söllner, who was born in Banat, Romania in 1951, first worked as a teacher of German and English in Bucharest and later as a lectorator in a children’s book publishing house before moving to Frankfurt am Main in 1982.
Söllner has been awarded various literary prizes, in 1993 he gave the legendary Frankfurt poetry lectures at Goethe University, and since 2002 he has been head of the Hessian Literature Forum at the Mouson Tower in Frankfurt.
He caused a significant extra-literary sensation last Tuesday during a conference in Munich, which dealt with "German literature in Romania in the mirror and distortion mirrors of their securitate acts".
Visibly unsettled and embarrassed, Söllner read out a statement in which he admitted his cooperation as IM for the Romanian secret service Securitate in front of Romanian writer colleagues, including Richard Wagner, William Totok, Franz Hodjak, and Helmuth Frauendorfer.
Together with some of those present, Söllner had received the German Language Prize in 1989.
The recruitment attempts would have started creepingly, first in 1971.
I'm, Söllner concluded, "so someone who hasn't resisted enought intimidation attempts by Securitates."
Lyricist Werner Söllner is IM Walter.
Almost at the same time as the Nobel Prize ceremony for Herta Müller and her unprecedented literary examination of the mechanisms of dictatorial domination, the literary scene and in particular the community of German authors originating from Romania is shaken by a case that identifies the abysses and violation of a political system based on surveillance and spying.
Werner Söllner is, as far as one can say according to previous knowledge, not only perpetrators, but also victims.
In his Securitate file, IM Walter played a role, said after Söllner’s confession of the literary scholar Michael Markel and referred to the positive influence of IM Walter.
He "smashed it out" in all difficult points for him at that time, according to Markel.
It is a mortal need to make this remark.
Söllner's explanation was unexpected in form and in front of this audience, but the confession of a former dissident and his cooperation with the Romanian secret secry is not completely surprising.
Germany had been a cozy reserve for Securitate-Spitzel, Herta Müller had already written in July at the time, after she had read her own file, 914 pages.
Those affected, one may assume, are speculated and in some cases have probably also known which clear name hid behind the alias Sorin, Voicu and Walter.
In contrast than in this country, the handling of the legacies of Securitate monitoring is still very diffuse.
In recent years, a systematic enlightenment has been buoyed and hindered.
A National Council for the Reappraisal of Securitate Acts (CNSAS), corresponding to the Birthler authority, was only founded in 19990, but the release of the files has been rather sluggish ever since.
Only since 2005 has there been a law that regulates the management of the files.
The fact that the influence of former Securitate members is still very great in Romania was evidenced by an interview in which a former Securitate boss from Temesvar commented derogatoryly about the laureate.
She has psychosis, after all, she has only been interrogated once.
The infamy of power thought to be able to express itself blatantly here in the form of freedom of expression.
Surely there are unavoidable questions attached to the Söllner case, as so often in connection with concealed guilt.
Why only now? Who has been harmed? The prudent reaction of listeners to Söllner’s confession gives hope that the continuation of this chapter will be determined by urge for knowledge rather than blame and shame.
It is more than a footnote about the genesis of Herta Müller’s work, which received the Nobel Prize yesterday.
Saturation Leaque Champions League
It seemed as if there was just an elegantly dressed salesman from a very nice Christmas party, on which a sporting goods manufacturer had distributed goodies when the new VfB trainer Christian Gross climbed into a summoned C-Class a quarter of an hour after midnight accompanied by an orange plastic bag of the supplier.
The man with the distinctive baldness was chauffeured because of the city still foreign to him.
Otherwise, he could also have driven himself - alcohol wasn't in the game after all, and the 55-year-old was just as unlucky.
Had been a pleasant evening, but tomorrow the work just goes on.
Or just really go.
In the words of the only three days earlier committed Swiss: "While we have reached the eighth final", namely in the Champions League thanks to the 3:1 success against Unirea Urziceni from Romania, "but we must not be blinded by the success: It will be a brutally tough fight against relegation."
"The "much more important task" is imminent on Sunday.
Then it is important to repeat the king-class performance in everyday league life in Mainz.
And to put off the well-revealed weaknesses again after the fast three goals by Marica (5.), Träsch (8.) and Pogrebnyak (11.).
Drawing
The eighth final of the Champions League (run-off matches 16./17. + 23./24 February / rematches 9/10 + 16/17 March) will be drawn in Nyon on 18 December.
So the joy over the fuminant admission of the Babbel successor remained subdued.
Not only with the supposed bringer of salvation, who had conveyed self-confidence, commitment and playing pleasure to the unsettled Stuttgart professionals in the short time.
I approached the players and told them to remember their strengths and urged them to be more courageous, Seelenklempner Gross explained the so simple-sounding recipe for success, which under therapist Babbel had no more effect.
He has kindled some magic, Babbel proponent Jens Lehmann admitted, "when a new impetus comes in football, sometimes things happen that you wouldn't have thought possible."
And Sami Khedira, who became the best player on the pitch after weeks of injury time thanks to his dynamism, tried to describe the determined "phenomenon" with the proverbial "fresh wind" and the workings of the new supervisor: "He's a man who cracks down and demands discipline.
That's what we're needing in the current situation.'
Under Babbel it had obviously lacked it.
VfB Stuttgart is not yet over the mountain, warns sports director Horst Heldt
VfB Stuttgart, above all, needs consistency.
Like Gross, players and managers were behaved of overestimating the victory against a substitute-weakened and completely overwhelmed defense of the Romanian guests.
They had also won in Glasgow, before four days later in Leverkusen at the 0:4 the evil awakening, Khedira said.
And sports director Horst Heldt warned that although a goal for the season was achieved, the other was still in the distance.
We'ren't over the mountain yet.
The fact that Heldt on his 40th birthday, even after the first home win in three months in three months, was due to the events of the weekend, which revieled in the form of a television interview of goalkeeper Jens Lehmann broadcast immediately before the start of the game.
There are decisions that others make and not the Jens, Heldt countered Lehmann's harsh criticism.
He had accused the board of leadership weakness and had decided to change the coach under the pressure of adolescent fans.
Lehmann also regarded the fact that his day-free had been canceled as a sign of a lack of sovereignty and resentance.
Lehmann's statements were "shaped by pure selfishness," Heldt wedged back, announcing consequences for the closing man.
I don't think you're going to be friends for life anymore, the two forty-year-olds.
After all, however, they agreed with everyone else that without the preparatory work by the replaced coaching staff, the playfully and combatively strong performance would not have been possible.
Babbel and his assistants had "a large share of the success," Heldt and Lehmann said in unison - and the man with the plastic bag also agreed.
World Cup fight at heavyweight
Heated duel in Bern: Again and again challenger Kevin Johnson provoked world champion Vitali Klitschko with words and gestures.
But after twelve tough rounds, the Ukrainian defended his heavyweight title according to the WBC version.
The judgment of the judgment judges was unanimous.
Vitali Klitschko remains a heavyweight boxing world champion.
The 38-year-old chamber, according to version of the WBC association, unanimously defeated American Kevin Johnson on the night of Saturday in Bern (120:108, 120:108, 119:109).
For Klitschko, it was the third successful title defense after his comeback in October 2008.
Previously, the Ukrainian had already twice been world champion of the WBO and WBC associations.
Unfortunately it didn't work out with the knockout that everyone was expecting.
Klitschko extended his record to 39 wins in 41 professional fights, twice he had to admit defeat due to injuries.
Challenger Johnson had to accept his first defeat in the 24th fight.
In front of around 17,000 spectators in the Bernese Arena, the 2.02-metre Klitschko wanted to dictate the fight, especially with his left-hand leader.
The eleven-centimeter-smaller Johnson, who had announced the end of the Klitschko era before the duel, evaded the beatings of the world champion again and again, so that Klitschko could not bring his dangerous rights decisively to the finish.
Overall, passive Johnson did too little for a challenger and only tried to make ends meet.
Although he provoked Klitschko with words and gestures, his blows rarely hit the world champion.
After all, he is only the second boxer after Timo Hoffmann in 2000, who went with Klitschko over the full distance of twelve laps.
The challenger had caused some fuss before the fight in the Swiss capital.
He insulted Klitschko as an "ugly zombie" who "can't do anything right."
At the weigh-in, the champion then allowed himself to be provoked on Friday to shove the sunglasses off Johnson's nose "to see his eyes."
Fourth win after Klitschko's comeback
Klitschko also remained victorious in his fourth fight following his comeback on 11 October 2008.
After almost a four-year time-out, he had beaten the Nigerian Samuel Peter in Berlin and retrieved "his" WBC belt, which he had to hand over without a fight.
This year he defeated Juan Carlos Gomez from Cuba and Chris Arreola from the United States in September.
I only got one blow in this fight and I'm physically in very good shape, Klitschko had justified the quick return to the ring.
His dream, however, remains the unification of all four important titles in the family.
Brother Vladimir is world champion of the WBO and IBF associations.
However, David Haye wears the WBA's belt after he was able to dethrone the 2.13-meter-tall Nikolai Valuyev in November.
Haye had previously canceled in the summer a battle against Vitali Klitschko scheduled for September.
Supposedly, his doctoral company is already planning the fight against Klitschko at London's Wembleystadion.
Kunduz case
Frontal attack from the opposition: Greens parliamentary group leader Jürgen Trittin has called Defense Minister Guttenberg a liar because of false information on the Kunduz affair.
SPD and the left demand clarification from the chancellor.
According to the recent findings on the Kunduz airstrike, the opposition, Defense Minister Karl-Theodor zu Guttenberg (CSU) has assumed a deliberate deception.
The minister had said "knowingly the untruth" to the deadly bombing on two tanker trucks from September, Greens parliamentary group leader Jürgen Trittin said on Saturday in the ARD news program "Tagesschau".
It's commonly called: he lied.
The order for killing was issued in defiance of the rules of the International Protection Force for Afghanistan (ISAF).
That is the substance of the report that NATO had that Mr Guttenberg read, Trittin said.
On Friday, it had become known that the attack was not only about the destruction of the kidnapped vices, but also about deliberately killing two Taliban leaders.
Up to 142 people had been killed or injured in the U.S. airstrike requested by German Kunduz commander Colonel Klein, according to Nato's investigation report.
Greens, SPD and the left on Saturday called on Chancellor Angela Merkel (CDU) to speak immediately before the Bundestag and also to clarify what Guttenberg really knew about the incident.
Ms Merkel has to clarify whether a strategy of targeted killing is part of the federal government's Afghanistan policy - and, whether chancellery, Bundeswehr and news service have endorsed this new strategy, Jürgen Trittin and Greens colleague Renate Künast demanded.
Government spokesman Ulrich Wilhelm firmly rejected the criticism.
The chancellery did not influence concrete deployments of the Bundeswehr in Afghanistan, he said.
On Saturday, doubts had also become loud about the ministry's presentation about the background to the dismissal of Inspector-Inspectore Wolfgang Schneiderhan and State Secretary Peter Wichert.
According to the SPIEGEL, the two Guttenberg have correctly and completely informed about the background of the tanker truck attack.
Officially, it had said they had denied the existence of Nato reports and withheld sensitive details from the minister.
However, from the vicinity of the top two officials, it was said that they had even pointed to the Ministry of Defense Affairs to several reports, including a two-page document by the commanding Colonel Klein and a fielder report.
Wichert is said to have asked in a letter for clarification of the facts, reports the "Frankfurter Allgemeiner Sonntagszeitung".
He has not yet received a response, they say.
The ministry did not comment on the allegations on Saturday, pointing out that this would be clarified in the investigative committee of the Bundestag, which plans to constitute itself next Wednesday.
Dream worlds
Some see images in an alert state and hear voices that others don't see or hear.
The boundaries between imagination and reality are blurred not only in the case of the mentally ill and in the drug frenzy.
As long as it happens in your sleep, it's okay.
If we lie in our dark rooms with our eyes closed, we are allowed to uninhibitedly surrender to the wildest notions and imaginations.
However, those who experience dream-like conditions in wakefulness, sees and hears things that others do not see or hear, are usually considered crazy or drug addicts in the modern Western world, at least as a stranger.
However, such images and voices do not only arise in the case of mentally sick people or in the drug frenzy.
Our imagination is to blame: the same neural processes that enable us to designate houses, to paint pictures or to write novels sometimes ensure that the boundaries between dream, delusion and reality blur.
Dream-like changed waking consciousness states can occur in a variety of forms, in the best case we call them visions, in the worst case delusion.
Even if triggers and effects may differ, the visions of the mystician Hildegard von Bingen, the drug experiences of the new-age prophet Carlos Castaneda, shamanic abracism states, psychosis and hallucinations can be traced back to similar processes.
Hallucinations or delusions arise when the balance of the neural messenger substances of the brain - especially dopamine, norepinephrine, serotonin and acetylcholine - derails and the interaction of different parts of the brain is disturbed.
Complex hallucinations and delusional thoughts occur very frequently during schizophrenic psychosis.
Gravity no longer has power over him
The connection between delusion and dream has occupied philosophers and physicians since antiquity.
Aristotle understood hallucinations as a form of the dream in the awake state, Wilhelm Griesinger, one of the founders of modern psychiatry, stated in 1861 a "great resemblance of irresence with dream states", for Sigmund Freud the dream was even "a psychosis, with all inconsistencies, delusions, sensory illusions of such".
It's autumn 2001, when Henning T. turns into a comic character.
His body is two-dimensional and squeaky colorful.
Gravity no longer has power over him, he floats at the ceiling.
He's feeling a kick.
Behind him winged devil figures with blood-red, skewered faces, teeth-flogging.
They chase him, kick him with heavy, flaming boots, throw him against the walls, he feels the pain on the whole body.
Loud, vicious voices roar from walls and radio.
He can no longer stand them, the voices that insult and insult him.
Henning T. tears two cables out of the steroe system, knots them into a sling and attaches them to a hook to the ceiling.
He climbs on a loudspeaker and puts the noose around his neck.
Jumping.
The sling dissolves.
That's supposed to be a lesson to you, screaming the voices in drummed fur-shredding chorus, "go to the clinic and get healed!"
Henning T. is 40 years old today, he has experienced six acute psychoses, the last is 2 years ago, the first almost 20 years.
It was triggered by LSD.
His diagnosis is schizoaffective-paranoid psychosis, in addition, he is manic-depressed, he takes medication for depression, mania, schizophrenia.
He's learned to live with his illness, he's been married for three years.
In psychosis, I have experienced worst fear and deepest desperation, he says.
The mirages are a cruel reality
About one percent of the population suffers from schizophrenia at least once in a lifetime, in psychiatric clinics it is treated as the second most common disease after depression.
In acute schizophrenic psychosis, sufferers can usually no longer distinguish between the inner and outside worlds, they get into an overexcited state, which can lead to hallucinations, size, relationship or persecuting delusions.
Voice hearing is particularly common - voices that command, comment or dialogue.
Optical hallucinations are also not rare.
The sufferer can no longer separate important things from the unimportant, the brain can no longer filter out disturbing signals, the consciousness is flooded by impressions.
Like the dream, psychosis opens the locks for a flood of ideas and fantasies that come from deeper layers of consciousness.
Reality perception in psychosis is subject-centered, says Professor Thomas Fuch, senior physician of the Department of General Psychiatry at Heidelberg University Hospital.
The schizophrenic is located in the center of what is happening, everything has a meaning directed towards it.
At the same time, he gets into a passive role, much like in a dream, he is overwhelmed by the events and cannot actively shape them.
Schizophrenics therefore often feel threatened, controlled and manipulated.
A comparison of reality is usually not possible for them, the mirages are gruesome reality, waking up excluded.
Having experienced a hallucination once in a lifetime
Unlike in a dream, Fuchs says, "the sensory and spatial structure of reality perceived in psychosis is fundamentally preserved."
The delusional thoughts and hallucinations are based in reality, the alienation of the experience takes place in a real framework.
Exactly why our brain beat caprioles still puzzles researchers.
States of exceptions in every person can cause him to briefly lose reference to reality, Fuchs says.
However, not everyone will develop psychosis.
There seems to be a genetic predisposition to schizophrenia, but twin studies have shown that the genes aren't the only determining factors.
Researchers assume that there is minimal brain damage in those affected.
We suspect that neural maturation of the brain is disturbed at an early stage of development, for example, through a viral disease of the mother during pregnancy, Fuchs explains.
But it remains a mystery how the disturbance of the neural system comes about, which also does not manifest itself for many years.
Environmental influences also play a role - stress or traumatic experiences can be triggers for psychosis if the disposition is present.
Hallucinogenic drugs can also trigger psychosis because they intervene in the transmitter system.
When our brain runs hot
The crazy thing is, psychotists suffer from their hallucinations and swallow expensive drugs to get rid of them, others spend money on drugs to get some, says Lübeck psychology professor Erich Kasten.
Whether mirages are perceived as pleasant or unpleasant is often only a matter of position.
Kasten has been collecting reports of hallucinations and illusions for ten years, last year he evaluated his findings in the book "The unreal world in our minds".
Hallucinations are among the typical side effects of psychoses, but also of astonishingly many other diseases.
In addition to mental disorders such as borderline or depression, this often also includes diseases such as migraine, epilepsy, multiple sclerosis or Alzheimer's and traumatic brain trauma.
Surprisingly, under certain conditions even the brain of healthy people hallucinates.
Hallucinations, according to Kasten, are composed of memory contents, which are usually retained.
They arise when brain areas in which sensory perceptions are processed are overactive or too inhibited; when our brains are running hot or when stimulation is lacking.
Drugs can trigger this condition in the same way as brain damage or psychosis.
But also stimulus deprivation, stress, lovesickness or other mental states.
In recent studies, up to a third of respondents said they had experienced hallucination at least once in a lifetime.
In the summer of 2005 Sarah K. is 16 years old, she lives in a small town in Mecklenburg-Vorpommern and attends high school there.
It's a normal girl, actually.
If it weren't for the pictures and voices.
During the break, it can happen that a lanky boy with long, dark hairs addresses it.
If it responds to him, the classmates look at it in surprise - no one else sees or hears this boy.
They consider the shy girl to be a spun daydreamer, but Sarah K. doesn't dream, she knows.
She's awake, she hears and sees this boy, just as she hears and sees the girl in the bank beside.
She can even feel his presence, at least for moments.
It's a pleasant feeling, she likes the boy.
Am I crazy? she wonders sometimes.
But she knows that this boy and the other mirages aren’t real and disappear.
At night, the appearances are mostly frightening
Often the mirages are also a burden.
When she sits in the classroom, she sometimes cannot follow the teaching material, because the teacher in her eyes and ears just introduces a new classmate, while for everyone else he explains mathematical equations on the blackboard.
At night, the appearances are mostly frightening.
If she waits for sleep in her dark room, which is far too rare and far too late, she often cannot distinguish whether she is awake or is already dreaming whether the pictures she sees, the faces or the girl with the bleeding lacerations on her arms and legs, arise in front of her open or closed eyes.
Leave me alone, she'll yell at the figures.
Parents and friends don't tell them about it, they'd think they're crazy.
Autumn 2009. Sarah K. is 20 years old today.
She graduated from high school last year and lives with her boyfriend.
A year ago, hallucinations stopped.
Since she made contact on the Internet with the psychologist Kasten, she knows exactly that she is not crazy.
Delusion is also a work of art of desperation
If someone is socially isolated or suffers from stimulus deprivation, they are likely to have hallucinations after a while, Kasten says.
I suspect the brain is then under-employed and takes care of its own entertainment.
Sarah K., an only child, was afraid of other children as a little girl, she frequently played alone.
Her hallucinations began when she learned at 13 that the man she's been calling dad since she can remember isn't her bodily father.
Since in visual and acoustic hallucinations, the same brain areas are active, which also process real impressions and sounds, it is difficult for sufferers such as Sarah K. to distinguish mirages and reality. "Every experience," says Kasten, "takes place in the brain, the reality emerges there.
Hallucinations are also images that create the brain, that's why they look so real."
Even nocturnal dream images, Kasten sees as a form of hallucination.
The hallucinations of the healthy are not fundamentally different from those of acute psychosis.
Unlike schizophrenics, mentally healthy people are able to recognize their mirages as unreal.
Kasten therefore advises his patients to value the hallucinations as a special ability.
Border between dream and hallucination
In our highly mind-centered world, imaginary experience is unfortunately mostly pathologised, says Michael Schmidt-Degenhard, professor in Heidelberg and chief physician of the Department of Psychiatry and Psychotherapy at Florence Nightingale Hospital in Düsseldorf.
A psychosis is a serious, sorrowful disease, but it also harbors positives, creative - delusion is also a work of art out of desperation.
Schmidt-Degenhard has explored the so-called oneiroid form of experience, a special form of trauma-ready changed waking consciousness.
Oneiroids are highly complex, detailed quasi-dreams, which the experient considers to be real and which he cannot distinguish from the waking state even in retrospect.
The oneiroid state of consciousness arises in trauma or disease-related extreme situations, for example in polyradiculitis patients, who experience motor paralysis with full consciousness.
If a person is threatened by loss of self and the world, for example by persistent, complete incapacity for movement, he replaces the life-threatening real situation with his own imaginary world, which is decoupled from reality, says Schmidt-Degenhard.
A kind of boundary between dream and hallucination.
Since coping with a situation is not possible in reality, there is a change in the world, but the real situation is reflected in the imagination.
An intense form of sensation and meaning in an unbearable situation in itself, which enables us persons to pursue their own creative ability not to perish in a nothingness of the loss of consciousness.
Everything is real, even later in memory
Zurich-based art historian Peter Cornelius Claussen also describes his Oneiroid experiences in his book "Heart change" as a rescue operation of the soul.
He is 49 years old when his consciousness travels for days after heart valve surgery with subsequent heart transplantation, while his body is doomed to motionlessness in intensive care.
Claussen dives into a self-created world, visits strange times and places: he experiences himself as a bedridden, old man, aboard a train sanatorium, a robot watches over his health.
In the early Middle Ages he lives among nobles who produce intoxicants from blood.
Is abducted by a Greek on a motorcycle in his home country, surfs with his hospital bed in the firn snow of the Alps and hits a Korean mafioso.
He goes up completely in the imagined event, everything is real, even later in memory.
These inner traits are fundamentally different for Claussen from every dream.
Dream is a different state, he describes his experience.
Quite in contrast, the memories from mental journeys are still clear to me after years and in incidental details.
Clearer and more intense even than really experienced.
The intensity of this experience transcends the horizons of our other life-worldly experiences, says psychiatrist Schmidt-Degenhard.
In the end, oneiroids, psychosis, hallucinations and drug frenzy show how brittle our inner images of external reality are awake.
Any change in the state of consciousness, the psychologist sums up caste, "can lead to a deformation of what we call reality."
An experience that can also be enriching.
Snowfall can come
Burda
Children should take over the publishing house at the age of 27
The new publishing boss Kallen is an interim solution: In the long term Hubert Burda wants to put his company in the hands of his two children, he told SPIEGEL.
The holding company will continue to belong to 100 percent of the family in the future.
The publisher Hubert Burda sees his company in the hands of his children in the long term.
Both children inherit at the age of 27 and have a firm will to join the company, the 69-year-old told DER SPIEGEL.
We've settled the details of this as a precaution.
The question of whether they are also active in the company only arises afterwards.
Also the question of whether they are able to do so.
You can't put 7500 employees in front of your children if they'ren't sufficiently empowered.
This kills the children and the company as well.
Burda had announced on Thursday that he was retiring from the board's chairmanship from January, and Paul-Bernhard Kallen appointed his successor.
52-year-old Kallen has been a board member at Burda since 1999.
Initially, he was in charge of technology.
Later came the responsibility for the corporate assets with the area of treasury and, after the departure of Jürgen Todenhöfer also for direct marketing, abroad, printing and financies.
For the first time, no family member is at the head of the publisher.
Burda's children are 19 and 17 years old, his son Felix, who would be 42 years old today, died several years ago.
Kallen's naming also serves to "bridge 10, 15 years," according to Burda.
The holding company should continue to belong to 100 percent of the family in the future, Burda said.
Referring to Schickedanz and Schaeffler, Burda expressed that even family businesses are not safe from demise: "This always affected families who were infected by an addiction to capital who were completely speculated and thought that they could thus become a very big player.
That was never my strategy.
He "never had world power dreams," according to Burda.
I don't see myself as the political preceptor of this country, as it was Springer, and I don't want to play in the world league with 'Time', these shops have become too big.
Perfect beauty
