FonPeek's Promises
Peek is extremely limited, a device with which you can only read your e-mails, without a possibility to see certain attachments or to be able to surf through the Internet.
It enjoyed its five minutes of fame when it voted Time magazine one of the products of the year.
Seeing it on the street is rather unlikely, but the idea in itself is still especially of interest to young people.
In contrast to smartphones, Peek costs very little and has a very favourable flat rate, for example, only half the price of the flat rate of an iPhone.
After the company has seen success with it, it has launched two new devices: one that is intended only for Twitter, the other with a lifetime free service for data, in contrast to Peek, where you have to pay a price six times higher.
Fonpeek, which was presented yesterday at the Le Web event, is the European version of this invention and thus its third version.
It has the same limits as his American counterpart, but has a surprise ready.
The monthly alimony price for the phone, about 12 euros a month, includes roaming for the whole of Europe.
Those who travel around the world with a smartphone already know what it means to have to do without all the data in order to be able to read your emails once you have crossed the national border or if you are desperately looking for a coffee shop with WIFI (or a connection with Fon).
The idea of roaming with flat rate is incredibly seductive and should actually be possible for a long time.
It’s true that some telephone providers already offer special contracts and prizes for stays abroad, but in my experience you already need a doctoral degree in physics and good glasses to be able to read all that fine print.
The European Commission's repeated criticism of affordable roaming tariffs for the whole of Europe almost always went unnoticed.
Outside your own country, you are always afraid to read your e-mails and rightly so.
That a small device like FonPeek (although it's always astonishing that it's called Fon, even though it doesn't own Wifi/Wi-Fi) can offer this type of tariff, means that phone vendors have to slowly but safely rethink and start considering various ways they can deploy their data network.
Kindle is another good example of this, in its prices the downloading of books and further inquiries on the Internet is included.
Without contract and no hooks.
This is an internet-enabled device and you only pay for it once, namely when you buy, and not month after month.
And that's exactly what I expect from FonPeek one day, it's an opportunity to buy with lifelong Internet access like Peek already offers in the U.S.
And not because I want to buy it.
I'll read my e-mails by phone, thank you.
But I expect this because I think it would be a more suitable model for the consumer.
It would be interesting to see a computer with the same system.
Michelle's fetish color
Yellow is without a doubt the American first lady's favourite colour.
Even when Barack Obama took office as president of the United States, she appeared in a dress and a coat of this color and has re-elected the same color to accompany him at his, without a doubt, the second most important moment in his life: the award of the Nobel Peace Prize in Oslo.
Michelle Obama wore very similar models on both occasions: except that it was almost the samele color - a shiny green-yellow - the clothes consisted of three parts - dress, jacket and coat made of the same fabric, and in addition she wore something grossy embroidered or a necklace.
It even goes so far that at the first sight it seemed that the First Lady of the United States wanted to follow the example of the Princess of Asturias and had decided to wear the same dress with more than one occasion.
However, the two dresses are very different in the type of fabric they are made of.
At the inauguration, she wore a lace dress and in Oslo, one made of patterned velvet.
The accessories were also different: for the first time green shoes with half-height heels and for the second time a similar model, but gold-colored.
The hairstyle was also different every time: The hair was put up on this occasion as a wreath of hair, on the previous she wore the hair open.
75% of working mothers have experienced problems at work because of their motherhood
Discrimination at work has increased in recent years, especially among the younger women who want to have children.
This is the conclusion of CSIC's "Fertility and Women's Career in Spain" study carried out in cooperation with the Institute for Women.
The survey of 9,737 women between the ages of 15 and 75 leaves no doubt about this: 75% of working women see their career opportunities in the labour market as restricted due to their maternity.
The study discloses that there are huge differences in the decision to start a family and the number of children depending on whether the women work or not, and if so, what kind of profession they exercise.
Those of them, who don't engage in any other activity outside the house, only work half-day, or don't have a fixed employment contract, are more likely to become mothers and have more children.
In contrast, women with permanent employment, especially in the public sector, are postponing the establishment of a family and motherhood and therefore also have fewer children.
"Women in the age group between 35 and 49 years of age with a permanent employment get their first child on average only 3.7 years after, a number that increases to 4.1 years for women with permanent employment, who have taken longer to achieve this and those who have contracted and the first child only realized much later.
In addition, the type of work also has a major influence on motherhood, albeit less than the calendar in terms of readiness for conception," explains CSIC researcher Margarita Delgado.
The level of education is another factor that must be taken into account in the age of the women during marriage and the first child.
According to the survey, at the time of marriage and the first child, the higher the level of education.
This difference could also be found in women of the same age.
For example, the women in the group between the ages of 35 and 49, depending on whether they only have the secondary school leaving certificate or a higher education, on average each with 25.1 or 32.1 for the first time mother.
Furthermore, depending on their level of education, there is another difference between women.
While the women with a final graduation marry and have their first child, the women with a middle or high graduate do it exactly the other way around and first look for a permanent position and get married afterwards and then have their first child, Delgado notes.
